HT_DATA.load("detail", {"eJ3RzGoQC4s":{"wy":"HN recommends it often and strongly as an eye-opening account of marketing psychology and the engineering of consumer society.","q":{"x":"For the first time in my life I feel like I understood the “why” of how things work in American society.","i":32800759,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":35620952,"author":"badrabbit","month":"2023-04","story":"The Cult of Sigmund Freud","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Shows how Freud influenced modern capitalist life","quote":"This documentary shared on HN a while back was eye opening for me","text":"I wholly distrust anything freudian. Every major claim he made I keep finding serious flaws in it. Freud did not popularize the unconscious or thinking about how you think. Basic children's sunday school at a Church even talks about how you think. Major religions from Christianity to Buddhism talk about how the thought life of a person affects everything and the split and conflicted nature of humans. They just don't use post-freudian terms like conscious and unconscious. What freud popularized was a secular way of coping with how humans think, analyzing it in a purely secular context and considering thousands of years of knowledge on the subject as childish fairytales and defining a framework by which a modern secular world can explain the actions of people and respond to them. Socially, I have seen horrific impacts of his work from criminal punishment to people attempting to understand and help people who have chosen to be evil (worst examples being child rapists, school shooters,etc... not sick, evil! But evil has no place in a freudian context). This documentary shared on HN a while back was eye opening for me, how almost every aspect of modern capitalist life was influenced by freud: [VIDEO] (Century of the self). Worth watching the whole thing. I don't discount psychoanalysis as quackery but I am very hostile to it. I would need to filter any attempts of psychoanalytical thinking with a ton if critical analysis, taking it with buckets of salt!"},{"id":32800759,"author":"orasis","month":"2022-09","story":"Ask HN: What are some of the best documentaries you've seen?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains how Freud and marketing reshaped American society","quote":"For the first time in my life I feel like I understood the “why” of how things work in American society.","text":"“The Century of the Self” by Adam Curtis. [VIDEO] It goes through each decade of the 1900s and explains how Freud’s psychology and the new field of marketing completely reshaped society. For the first time in my life I feel like I understood the “why” of how things work in American society. This film is probably best if you’re age 40+ and actually remember some of the events."},{"id":22979406,"author":"mistermann","month":"2020-04","story":"Anatomists of Melancholy in the Age of Coronavirus","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Compelling documentary on Freud, Bernays and politics that changed commenter's worldview","quote":"It certainly has had a major effect on the way I view how the world works","text":"The documentary Century of Self (re: Sigmund Freud and his nephew Edward Bernays) gets into this type of thing , and many, many others. [LINK] Full: [VIDEO] 10 minute trailer/summary: [LINK] If someone wants to check out the above political aspect, fast forward to the 3 hour mark or so. Fair warning, this would be typically filed under conspiracy theories / propaganda by most people, but even so, it is by far one of the most compelling ones I've seen. It very much advocates for the common man, and I believe makes a decent case. It certainly has had a major effect on the way I view how the world works, but YMMV."},{"id":18728924,"author":"cronix","month":"2018-12","story":"Why Is America So Angry? The Real Roots of American Rage","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Adam Curtis documentaries are riveting","quote":"I'd highly recommend watching his documentaries. All of them. Absolutely riveting stuff.","text":"Having recently discovered Adam Curtis (from other HN posts), I'd highly recommend watching his documentaries. All of them. Absolutely riveting stuff. HyperNormalization: [LINK] Bitter Lake: [LINK] The Trap: [LINK] The Power of Nightmares: [LINK] The Mayfair Set: [LINK] The Century of the Self: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16034465,"author":"stevewillows","month":"2017-12","story":"IKEA Effect","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Documentary on marketing psychology, including the instant-mix egg story","quote":"which is a fantastic documentary, and definitely worth a watch.","text":"I love building IKEA furniture. For me, there is a certain controlled fear that comes with opening the boxes. I know it'll go together, because.. well, its not difficult, but there's always that lingering fear that it won't. In regards to the egg in the instant mix (pancakes, cakes, etc), this is mentioned in Adam Curtis' 'The Century of the Self'[1], which is a fantastic documentary, and definitely worth a watch. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":12879326,"author":"urza","month":"2016-11","story":"Is democracy a failure? (1861)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Shows how PR and advertising manipulate the public","quote":"Anyone who hasnt seen \"The Century of Self\" documentary, please watch it, it will open your eyes","text":"Indeed we should be afraid of modern advertisers. They exploit the nature of human mind, to manipulate us into decisions that are often not in our best interest. Politicians then use advertisers to manipulate masses to ensure power for themselfs. In my country people in last elections elected an Olygarch, multi billionair, who controls whole agriculture, all main media, medical and other industries and now he is writing laws that suits his needs and stealing billions in \"donations\" from us. How is this possible? How was he elected in democracy? How is it possible that he has support of majority of people? Because he has very good PR, advertisement and propaganda team. Anyone who hasnt seen \"The Century of Self\" documentary, please watch it, it will open your eyes: [VIDEO]"},{"id":44977743,"author":"ixtli","month":"2025-08","story":"In Defense of Car-Centered Society","stance":"rec","why":"Covers how industry engineered associations like cars and freedom","quote":"A great documentary called Century of the Self covers this and other related things","text":"Correct. The car industry intentionally socially engineered the US to associate the giant individual and social burden of car ownership with freedom. A great documentary called Century of the Self covers this and other related things ( [VIDEO] )"},{"id":37706402,"author":"LinuxBender","month":"2023-09","story":"Norway wants Facebook behavioral advertising banned across Europe","stance":"rec","why":"Covers how people were taught to desire things they don't need","quote":"covers how businesses and governments were taught to make people desire things they don't need.","text":"There's even an old BBC documentary on this called Century of the Self [1] created by Adam Curtis that covers how businesses and governments were taught to make people desire things they don't need. [1] - [VIDEO] [video][4 hours][996 MB]"},{"id":37070396,"author":"c23gooey","month":"2023-08","story":"Many temptations of an open-source Chrome extension developer","stance":"rec","why":"Opened eyes to damage done by Bernays","quote":"certainly opened my eyes to the damage that Bernays has inflicted on society in general.","text":"The documentary \"Century of Self\" by Adam Curtis [0] certainly opened my eyes to the damage that Bernays has inflicted on society in general. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":20429554,"author":"wizardforhire","month":"2019-07","story":"Six Degrees of Separation at Burning Man","stance":"rec","why":"Reveals how sinister and deep the rabbit hole goes","quote":"If you haven’t seen this documentary it’s worth checking out if you’re curious to see how sinister and deep the rabbit hole goes.","text":"“Probably” doesn’t do it justice. If you haven’t seen this documentary it’s worth checking out if you’re curious to see how sinister and deep the rabbit hole goes. [VIDEO]"},{"id":20315623,"author":"musha68k","month":"2019-06","story":"I’ve Picked My Job over My Kids","stance":"rec","why":"Curtis theory on the self and consumer culture","quote":"reminded me of Adam Curtis' \"Century of the Self\" theory","text":"Your short comment rings very true to me and reminded me of Adam Curtis' \"Century of the Self\" theory which in his latest documentary \"Hypernormalization\" gets developed even further. I guess you might know it but for those who don't - both freely available on youtube. Century of the Self: [VIDEO] Hypernormalization [LINK] Adam Curtis: [LINK]"},{"id":18085978,"author":"compcoffee","month":"2018-09","story":"Ask HN: What are some of the best documentaries you've seen?","stance":"rec","why":"Top documentary pick; all of Curtis is worth watching","quote":"I think all of Adam Curtis' documentaries are worth a watch","text":"> The Century of the Self: [VIDEO] I think all of Adam Curtis' documentaries are worth a watch: [LINK] Some others favourites off the top of my head: 808 (about the drum machine): [LINK] Scratch: [LINK] Hoop Dreams: [LINK] Startup.com (a classic!) : [LINK] The Fog of War: [LINK]"},{"id":13603860,"author":"seltzered_","month":"2017-02","story":"HyperNormalisation (2016) [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Adam Curtis film on consumer culture and psychology","quote":"Worth also checking out his 2002 film 'the century of the self'.","text":"Adam Curtis has a history of making these films. Worth also checking out his 2002 film 'the century of the self'. Wikipedia on Adam Curtis: [LINK] The century of the self on youtube: [VIDEO] Other films of his are also on archive.org"},{"id":48250114,"author":"Bender","month":"2026-05","story":"There's No Reason to Fear an Invasion of Chinese Electric Vehicles","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for emotion-driven consumer decisions","quote":"See documentary Century of the Self...","text":"There's No Reason to Fear an Invasion of Chinese Electric Vehicles Fear is an emotion. People need not make purchasing decisions based on emotions. See documentary Century of the Self... [1] I consider logic such as who is going to repair my vehicle and where am I going to get parts , how long will parts be available and how safe is this thing . Perhaps these are answered questions for the cheaper EV imports but I have not seen this articulated anywhere in a confidence inspiring level of detail especially given the current tension between China and the US. The common answer I hear is \"but EV's are simpler\" which tells me these people have not actually tried to work on one. They are not simple at all and the parts are quite unique and have to be ordered from China . Build out factories in the US and stock up on all the parts first. Show me the USA tested crash safety data, remove all the drive-by-wire nonsense, make the battery packs user serviceable, remove all dial-home telemetry and maybe we can talk otherwise I see this as future throw-away toys that will flood the landfills. [1] - [VIDEO] [video][4 hours][b&w documentary]"},{"id":38047094,"author":"sgu999","month":"2023-10","story":"The Magical Japanese Art of Luggage Forwarding","stance":"reference","why":"Curtis changed how the commenter sees conformity","quote":"","text":"> you’d have much of your individuality grinded out of you That doesn't seem too bad now that Adam Curtis ruined it for me. [0] [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":34717128,"author":"EGreg","month":"2023-02","story":"The Teen Mental Illness Epidemic Began Around 2012","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Kids overdiagnoses, while both parents work in corporations and stick them in a school to sit down and shut up for 10 hours a day: ADHD - prescribe amphetamines (Ritalin, Adderall) Gender Dysphoria - prescribe hormone blockers Autism spectrum - Abilify and Risperdal [LINK] [LINK] Teenager and College Student overdiagnoses: Depression - prescribe SSRIs Diabetes - prescribe Meglitinides Obesity - prescribe diets [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] Adults: Men - Opioid epidemic since 2008 Women - One in four on antidepressants in middle age Internet Addiction - companies need to boost \"engagement\" ADD - notifications and multitasking 24/7 is normal now [LINK] Elderly in nursing homes: Too many to list We need to realize that these \"epidemics\" and \"rises in incidence\" have more to do with the capitalist industry and government working together, than individual incidence. They have women vs men, black vs white, so people are distracted from the real causes: [LINK] Big Tech is a big part of the problem, and much of the unhealthy habits and emergent depression come from the influencer industry. In other demographics, unhealthy influences were specifically pushed and promoted until they became normalized. It takes hold easier when you are young and impressionable: Gangsta rap, Drill music - music industry, corrupts Black youth Instagram and TikTok - influencer industry, corrupts adolescent girls Most of the cartoons you watched, down to the \"programming\", was based around merchandising oppor"},{"id":19523866,"author":"lm28469","month":"2019-03","story":"Boston Dynamics’ new robot stacks boxes [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Suggests loss of free will to consumer culture has long been happening","quote":"","text":"> and will subtly lose free will and never notice they did. Isn't that happening for decades now ? [VIDEO] > the people not doing it will be more and more excluded from the regular system. Same with cars, phones, internet"},{"id":18823109,"author":"throwaway7312","month":"2019-01","story":"Singapore activist found guilty of hosting ‘illegal assembly’ via Skype","stance":"reference","why":"Linked for Edward Bernays's view on manipulating the public","quote":"Edward Bernays certainly thought so.","text":"Open borders / illegal immigration from poor / brown countries is a Republican position. They've pushed it since before Reagan's amnesty to benefit corporate special interests. [1] Democrats are late to that game, but now both parties agree: more cheap immigrants for their corporate masters. The Republican party vehemently opposed Donald Trump during the elections of 2015/6 and throughout his first year in office in large part due to his ambivalence toward immigration. There are some opposed to mass immigration among the elected officials of both parties (growing among the Republicans, shrinking among the Democrats), but they remain a minority. Never mind that large majorities of both Republican and Democrat electorates want less immigration [2]... the corporate sponsors want more, thus so do the representatives. This is how politics works in the United States. The Parties put on a puff show of differing over hot button social issues that whip the electorate into a frenzy. Meanwhile the elected representatives of both parties go to great lengths to satisfy their corporate and international donors, who are the same for the representatives of both parties. The plebes fight over the kabuki theater of climate change and transsexual bathrooms while the rulers work together on issues of substance without the interference or awareness of the common man. Perhaps it's better that way? Edward Bernays certainly thought so. [3] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO]"},{"id":13389180,"author":"Mathnerd314","month":"2017-01","story":"I Am Writing a JavaScript to Rust Transpiler, So Please Just End My Suffering","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Warning: rambling comment. A rambling post deserves a rambling answer, particularly if the rambling is in the wrong direction... [LINK] > Every morning, I wake up, drink a glass of Soylent and recite the following: “Today, I will solve challenging problems. Tomorrow, I will also solve challenging problems. Every day, I will solve challenging problems, and then the robots will take over, and I will die a fulfilled man, and someone will post my obituary on Hacker News.” That's this guy: [LINK] . Pretty sure he occupies a unique niche in the world though. > I am creating This statement is clearly false, as writing blog posts is in no way related to writing a transpiler. Typically all coding posts are post-mortems. > a JavaScript to Rust transpiler in Haskell, I guess this is a takeoff on the C-to-Rust translator ( [LINK] )? (and all the compile-to-JS projects such as GWT, emscripten etc.). But those have actual use cases, whereas going from front-end to deep back-end / systems programming seems like a stretch. It's true there are a lot of programming language and toolkit posts / flames on HN, and I admit they're often kind of shallow. But a small discussion is better than none at all, and it occasionally gets someone knowledgeable to contribute. But there's a qualitative difference between lots of people spending a little time on a subject versus a few people spending a lot of time on a subject. The first is often productive in a wisdom-of-the-crowds sort of way, while the secon"},{"id":12293814,"author":"dredmorbius","month":"2016-08","story":"Can Facebook really make ads unblockable?","stance":"reference","why":"One entry in a reading list on advertising and media","quote":"Adam Curtis, The Century of the Self","text":"Jerry Mander, Four Arguments for the Elimination of Television [LINK] (Mander was himself an ad executive.) Neil Postman, Amusing Ourselves to Death and Technopoly [LINK] [LINK] Adam Curtis, The Century of the Self [LINK] [VIDEO] While we're at it, A Thousand Clowns [LINK] [LINK]"}],"r":[["pxBQLFLei70",2],["H9B5mYfBPlY",1],["GNO_CYUjMK8",1],["G0R09YzyuCI",1],["VZQoAKJPbh8",1],["AnrlSqtpOkw",1],["fpbOEoRrHyU",1],["rFejpH_tAHM",1],["2wq9x2QcZN0",1],["-q7ZVXOU3kM",1],["aB8zcAttP1E",1],["plcc6E-E1uU",1],["vs41JrnGaxc",1],["zz4YVJ4aRfg",1],["06e-PwhmSq8",1],["-f-CFQxaUY4",1],["sWN13pKVp9s",1],["XEVlyP4_11M",1],["9nXop2lLDa4",1],["9W74aeuqsiU",1]]},"2V1FtfBDsLU":{"wy":"HN's go-to link in typing debates and for talk lists, and several call it their favorite programming talk even for non-Clojure users.","q":{"x":"Whole video is gold, is my fav programming talk of all time.","i":23420385,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":23420385,"author":"dominotw","month":"2020-06","story":"A History of Clojure [pdf]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Information programming problem explained by Hickey","quote":"Whole video is gold, is my fav programming talk of all time.","text":"Rich hickey explains at this timestamp in the video, [VIDEO] \"The information programming problem\" Whole video is gold, is my fav programming talk of all time."},{"id":22390394,"author":"hden","month":"2020-02","story":"Requirements volatility is the core problem of software engineering","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Situated programs and changing requirements, relevant beyond Clojure","quote":"Highly recommended to read the transcript or watch the video, even if you are not interested in the language itself.","text":"> Requirements change. Every software engineering project will face this hard problem at some point. Rich Hickey calls this a 'situated' program in his talk from Clojure/Conj 2017, where the program has to deal with information and real-world irregularities for extended periods. While agile disciplines help you from one perspective, your tools (e.g. program language) might also help you solve some of the problems. In this framing, Clojure certainly tries to help where the it matters most. Highly recommended to read the transcript or watch the video, even if you are not interested in the language itself. Video: [VIDEO] Transcript: [LINK]"},{"id":18219401,"author":"afandian","month":"2018-10","story":"Ask HN: What are some of the best technical talks you've heard?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Review of ten years of Clojure and its design wisdom","quote":"Rich Hickey has a lot of excellent stuff to say.","text":"Rich Hickey has a lot of excellent stuff to say. Not only are his ideas philosophically sound, but also real, practical and ready to use in the form of Clojure. This recent review \"10 years of Clojure\", encapsulates a lot of of the wisdom: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37766371,"author":"ndr","month":"2023-10","story":"Strong typing, a hill I'm willing to die on","stance":"rec","why":"Hickey's problem severity ordering and view on types","quote":"Consider reading/watching Rich Hickey's talk Effective Programs [0][1] and Maybe Not [2][3]","text":"Consider reading/watching Rich Hickey's talk Effective Programs [0][1] and Maybe Not [2][3] In [0] in particular there're slides 22/23, here is part of the transcript but makes more sense with the slides on: > And you can call them problems, and I'm going to call them the problems of programming. And I've ordered them here [...] I've ordered them here in terms of severity. And severity manifests itself in a couple of ways. Most important, cost. What's the cost of getting this wrong? At the very top you have the domain complexity, about which you could do nothing. This is just the world. It's as complex as it is. > > But the very next level is the where we start programming, right? We look at the world and say, \"I've got an idea about how this is and how it's supposed to be and how, you know, my program can be effective about addressing it\". And the problem is, if you don't have a good idea about how the world is, or you can't map that well to a solution, everything downstream from that is going to fail. There's no surviving this misconception problem. And the cost of dealing with misconceptions is incredibly high. > >So this is 10x, a full order of magnitude reduction in (?) severity before we get to the set of problems I think are more in the domain of what programming languages can help with, right? And because you can read these they'll all going to come up in a second as I go through each one on some slide so I'm not going to read them all out right now. But importantly t"},{"id":31882498,"author":"frogulis","month":"2022-06","story":"Revisiting the principles of data-oriented programming","stance":"rec","why":"Better explanation of data-oriented ideas and the meaning of simple","quote":"provide a better explanation of these ideas IMO","text":"Rich Hickey's talks \"Simple Made Easy\" [1] and \"Effective Programs\" [2] provide a better explanation of these ideas IMO. The specific definition of \"simple\" is pretty crucial. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":27999678,"author":"blacktriangle","month":"2021-07","story":"How Dwarf Fortress is built","stance":"rec","why":"Higher level problems of business app development and how Clojure addresses them","quote":"The whole talk is worth watching.","text":"[VIDEO] Goes until 43:25. The whole talk is worth watching. It's basically about the higher level problems of business app development, how to address them, and how Clojure does address them."},{"id":18365453,"author":"PKop","month":"2018-11","story":"Minesweeper in Clojure","stance":"rec","why":"Clojure creator explains choosing dynamic over static typing","quote":"Here's a video of the creator of Clojure explaining his preference for dynamic vs static languages","text":"Here's a video of the creator of Clojure explaining his preference for dynamic vs static languages, and how he came to the choice of making Clojure dynamic, that may help inform your decision: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17431264,"author":"rboyd","month":"2018-06","story":"Clojure Error Messages Are Accidental","stance":"rec","why":"Hickey on large scale programming and dynamic types","quote":"Watch Rich's 'Effective Programs' talk [1] and lmk if you still feel that he's out of touch with modern large scale programming.","text":"That's quite a judgement call to make based on the utility of error messages. I can't remember the last time I was blocked based on a Clojure error message anyway. Errors are less frequent and usually pretty obvious. I think you're wrong to say core devs don't understand modern programming, but you'd probably be right to say that they didn't prioritize making it approachable and stooping to the lowest common denominator. And it's not just Clojure, but datomic, core.async, spec -- all solving real problems in modern large scale programming with elegance. Watch Rich's 'Effective Programs' talk [1] and lmk if you still feel that he's out of touch with modern large scale programming. I think he's pretty in tune. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":16839366,"author":"nordsieck","month":"2018-04","story":"Ask HN: What is your favourite tech talk?","stance":"rec","why":"Listed among favourite tech talks","quote":"Effective Programs - 10 Years of Clojure: Rich Hickey","text":"We Really Don't Know How to Compute: Gerry Sussman - [LINK] Zebras All the Way Down: Bryan Cantrill - [LINK] Jonathan Blow on Deep Work: Jonathan Blow - [LINK] Simple Made Easy: Rich Hickey - [LINK] Effective Programs - 10 Years of Clojure: Rich Hickey - [VIDEO] The Last Thing D Needs: Scott Meyers - [LINK]"},{"id":16629630,"author":"joncampbelldev","month":"2018-03","story":"Types, and Why You Should Care [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Hickey on data, reuse and dynamic typing","quote":"I can only recommend watching some of Rich Hickey's talks","text":"One of the big benefits of clojure being dynamic is that everything is data (e.g. a map, set, vector or list). This is what allows reuse. - The vast core library of functions that manipulate those data structures can be used for everything in your program, cos it's all data. - Most clojure libraries take and/or return data, reducing the need for clumsy adaptors, or even worse not being able to get at the data you need cos the library writer was really enthusiastic about encapsulation of everything they thought was of no use to consumers. - You don't have a person class, you have a map with a first name and last name. Now the function that turns first + last name into full name can be reused for any other map with the same keys. (A rather spurious example, but a real one would take a large codebase and an essay to describe) I can only recommend watching some of Rich Hickey's talks, particularly these ones, they're not entirely about types, but they express the above ideas much better than I can: - Simple made easy [LINK] - Effective programs [VIDEO] - Are we there yet? (this one is more about OOP, but unless you're using something like haskell, idris etc its relevant for your type system of choice) [LINK]"},{"id":41466515,"author":"uludag","month":"2024-09","story":"Clojure 1.12.0 is now available","stance":"reference","why":"Hickey describes situational programs where Clojure is used","quote":"Rich Hickey has also mentioned the concept of \"situational programs\"[1]","text":"\"Enterprise\" stuff in a much less \"enterprise\" way is one usage, hence being based off the JVM. Rich Hickey has also mentioned the concept of \"situational programs\"[1] which Clojure is used in a lot. Clojure is also used in a wide variety of other areas like data science[2] or desktop applications[3] much like other general purpose programming languages. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":30327065,"author":"zrkrlc","month":"2022-02","story":"Ask HN: Easiest way to build a CRUD app?","stance":"reference","why":"Situated programs concept matching Clojure's design space","quote":"All of your requirements fit the cluster in designspace that the language was designed to occupy.","text":"I guess no one uses Clojure/Script on HN anymore. All of your requirements fit the cluster in designspace that the language was designed to occupy. (cf. 'situated programs' in [VIDEO] )"},{"id":26587660,"author":"phyrex","month":"2021-03","story":"Rich Hickey and Brian Beckman – Inside Clojure (2009) [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Hickey explains Clojure designed for situated programs","quote":"Clojure has been explicitly designed for 'situated programs'","text":"The creator, Rich Hickey, explains that Clojure has been explicitly designed for 'situated programs' in this talk: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24690767,"author":"adamkl","month":"2020-10","story":"Principles of Data Oriented Programming","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Honestly, I think this series of blog posts could have had a more complete title: \"Principals of Data Oriented Programming (aka, Idiomatic Clojure) \" All of these ideas (and I think they are good ones) are inspired heavily by Rich Hickey's talks and rational behind developing the Clojure language (the author of the post states as much). And while you can use these techniques in other languages/paradigms/problem domains, they are really intended to work well inside the constructs of Clojure, and when applied to \"information-driven situated programs\" [0] (read business applications with dynamic requirements). As for some of the short-comings you mentioned: \"But you still need a mechanism to manage mutating data\" Clojure supports this through the use of locking constructs like atoms. [1] \"I think what it's getting at is that you don't really know the precise type of your data, over time, in a distributed system, so it's good to include the flexibility to handle that. That makes sense to me. but generic data structures aren't necessarily always the right way to handle that.\" Clojure attempts to bridge the gap between generic data-structures and strongly-typed constructs using run-time specifications. [2] I mean, the ideas presented here can be generally useful, but your mileage may vary if the principals take you too far out of the idiomatic for your particular language/paradigm/problem domain. If that's the case, you could find yourself wasting energy swimming up stream. [0] - ["},{"id":18205664,"author":"emmanueloga_","month":"2018-10","story":"Arguing against using protobuffers","stance":"reference","why":"Hickey explains favoring dynamic types and EDN over protobufs","quote":"The talk explains why Hickey favors dynamic types","text":"Agree with other ppl here, an unnecessarily violent rant, and the point the author makes is kind of lost in the title \"Protobuffers Are Wrong\". What they really mean is \"I think Protobuffers type system could be better\". I recently saw a talk by Rich Hickey about Effective Programs [1]. The talk explains why Hickey favors dynamic types, with EDN [2] and transit [3] proposed as an alternative to more statically typed data exchange formats like protobufs and less structured ones like Json (the talk explains the reasons, nearing the end I think). 1: [VIDEO] 2: [LINK] 3: [LINK]"},{"id":18257968,"author":"Jach","month":"2018-10","story":"This Old Lisp","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"As mentioned, types are in the standard, and certain implementations provide compile time warnings of type and other issues...[0] The biggest non-feature advantage (the condition system is a pretty big feature though missing from everything else) is the mindset of writing programs, which other dynamic languages typically don't get right. The idea of \"growing\" a program, and responding to change, is part of that mindset and once it's internalized a lot of the language decisions make a lot more sense. e.g. \"trace\", \"break\", \"compile\" are all part of the standard, all available at runtime, whereas in other languages you tend to have a separation of the runtime from any \"IDE feature\" like debugging break points, tracing function calls, and compiling (not \"eval\"ing) new code. Other languages + IDEs are getting there but there's so much more to go. In Java land, with the support of things like JRebel, we can make pretty significant modifications to a running program like add/change/remove methods to a class. But what about any existing objects I may have instantiated? Those are left around, referring to the old code. In Lisp, redefining classes works out of the box (no need to wait decades for JRebel to show up), and \"update-instance-for-redefined-class\" is a standard generic function, which you can write a version of to handle conversion of existing objects. No mysterious old versions of objects running around, you didn't have to bring down the program to restart it, nice. It's a "},{"id":17905694,"author":"zengid","month":"2018-09","story":"Why I never finish my Haskell programs","stance":"reference","why":"Hickey on static languages offering type puzzles","quote":"static languages present the programmer with neat little puzzles to solve","text":"I think Rich Hickey described it pretty well when he said (paraphrasing from [0]) that static languages present the programmer with neat little puzzles to solve that feels like we're writing applications but we're just creating intricate types and abstractions. I think he has a point, but I certainly don't want to give up the benefits of static languages, like being able to catch all of my silly errors. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":17725033,"author":"shaunparker","month":"2018-08","story":"Problem Solving and Clojure 1.9 with Rich Hickey","stance":"reference","why":"Hickey outlines the 10x problems Clojure targets","quote":"In Rich's \"Effective Programs\" talk [2] he outlines the \"10x problems\" that Clojure was targeted at solving.","text":"I don't find that too surprising, Rich tends to let things bake internally (in his head and at Cognitect) for a while before releasing anything publicly for consumption and feedback. Most of Clojure's current development is around tooling [1] and some changes to move clojure.spec out of alpha. I believe they want to accomplish this for 1.10, but I'm not sure where I remember hearing that. Alex Miller has mentioned that Cognitect built and continues to work on a tool that tracks all of your functions with spec definitions and generatively tests them only when the function or one of its dependencies changes. This would be extremely helpful since generative testing can be expensive to run, especially if you want to generate huge amounts of test cases. It'd be nice to say \"test all of my codebase's functions 10 million times\" and then have that information stored locally with the code. In Rich's \"Effective Programs\" talk [2] he outlines the \"10x problems\" that Clojure was targeted at solving. In it there are two 10x problems that weren't solved by Clojure: resource utilization and libraries. There's not much Clojure can do to solve resource utilization as it's dependent on the host (JVM), but the library problem (especially Clojure libraries with spec definitions) is solvable (though there seems to be some disagreement on whether only allowing growth in libraries in a practical). Pure speculation: All of their tooling projects (spec, codec, tool.deps, and the above mentioned gene"},{"id":15485838,"author":"slowmovintarget","month":"2017-10","story":"Is Haskell the right language for teaching functional programming principles?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"What do you mean by \"doesn't have monads?\" You just write monads in Clojure. They are only a structural formalism. [1] If you meant doesn't have the Maybe monad built in... why would you want that tangled up in your data? [2] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":47584562,"author":"yakshaving_jgt","month":"2026-03","story":"Clojure: The Documentary, official trailer [video]","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"It means nothing! It tells you nothing !","text":"> `a -> a`; `[a] -> [a]`; It means nothing! It tells you nothing ! — Rich Hickey, Effective Programs [0] [0]: [VIDEO] --- A disastrously poor take. I used to work at a Clojure company, and there's no chance I'd ever go back to that."}],"r":[["2U9WMftV40c",1],["wSF82AwSDiU",1],["T2IaJwkqgPk",1],["8455KEDitpU",1],["JvBT4XBdoUE",1],["bpAi70WWBlw",1],["LEZv-kQUSi4",1],["la-sGpTpkxE",1],["sK5i-N34im8",1],["-R-eqJDQ2nU",1],["dGVqrGmwOAw",1],["OQ5jsbhAv_M",1],["LKtk3HCgTa8",1],["YR5WdGrpoug",1],["KAWA1DuvCnQ",1],["zVhQOhxb1Mc",1]]},"fKy7ljRr0AA":{"wy":"HN commenters hold it up as a model of persuasive public speaking and as the go-to reminder of why public broadcasting deserves funding.","q":{"x":"Fred Rogers' 1969 testimony before the Senate in defense of PBS funding is very moving.","i":21747522,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":39121072,"author":"chimeracoder","month":"2024-01","story":"Mr. Rogers' music was intricate (2020) [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Rogers wins over a hostile senator in minutes","quote":"one must-see video outside the show is Fred Rogers testifying before the Senate in 1969","text":"> Doesn't look like he recorded much (outside the show), but definitely will be looking into what he has recorded. For anyone who hasn't seen it, one must-see video outside the show is Fred Rogers testifying before the Senate in 1969, in order to defend funding for public television. He is able to win over an incredibly rude and hostile senator in just a few minutes. [VIDEO]"},{"id":21747522,"author":"kingbirdy","month":"2019-12","story":"Caroll Spinney has died","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Moving testimony that prevented a funding cut","quote":"Fred Rogers' 1969 testimony before the Senate in defense of PBS funding is very moving.","text":"If you haven't seen it before, Fred Rogers' 1969 testimony before the Senate in defense of PBS funding is very moving. He was able to prevent a $10M cut to their $20M funding. [VIDEO]"},{"id":17270118,"author":"selljamhere","month":"2018-06","story":"Mr. Rogers Had a Simple Set of Rules for Talking to Children","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Calm, clear testimony supporting public television funding","quote":"One of my favorite examples of public speaking is Mr. Rogers testifying before a Senate subcommittee","text":"One of my favorite examples of public speaking is Mr. Rogers testifying before a Senate subcommittee in support of funding for national public television. He is calm, thoughtful and thorough through the entire process, speaking simply and without grandeur. It's a small wonder he found such success, and a great example of the importance of clear communication. [VIDEO]"},{"id":46226677,"author":"adfm","month":"2025-12","story":"After NPR and PBS defunding, FCC receives call to take away station licenses","stance":"rec","why":"Why public broadcasting deserves funding","quote":"I offer Fred Rogers testifying before the senate subcommittee on communications as a refresher on why we must continue to fund PBS and NPR.","text":"I offer Fred Rogers testifying before the senate subcommittee on communications as a refresher on why we must continue to fund PBS and NPR. [VIDEO] The person on the other end of that call is thin skinned and likely not hugged much as a child. Sad."},{"id":28617420,"author":"alickz","month":"2021-09","story":"Mister Rogers had a simple set of rules for talking to children (2018)","stance":"rec","why":"Impressive Senate testimony even for non-Americans","quote":"his testimony to the Senate Subcommittee on Communications is very impressive to me.","text":"As a European I never grew up with Mister Rogers, but his testimony to the Senate Subcommittee on Communications is very impressive to me. [VIDEO]"},{"id":18056208,"author":"yoshamano","month":"2018-09","story":"Mr. Rogers vs. the Superheroes","stance":"rec","why":"Rogers fighting for public television funding","quote":"here's his speech to Congress fighting for funding for public television","text":"There was a documentary about him released this year I highly recommend called Won't You Be My Neighbor. [LINK] As far as examples, here's an episode about divorce that I remember helping me deal with my parents' divorce when I was little. [LINK] Or here's his speech to Congress fighting for funding for public television (it's more complicated than just funding for public television, but that's the basic idea). [VIDEO] Or one of my favorites, his acceptance speech for his lifetime achievement Emmy Award. [LINK]"},{"id":17468688,"author":"disqard","month":"2018-07","story":"The Fred Rogers We Know","stance":"rec","why":"Rogers testifying to maintain PBS funding","quote":"I'll leave this link here anyway, for others who might not have seen this.","text":"That's very well articulated. He had no pretense and never came across as patronizing, at least in my (admittedly limited) sampling of old episodes via YouTube. The \"take others for exactly who they are\" mentality captures his earnestness and utter lack of guile in his interactions. I'm sure you've seen him testify before the Senate Subcommittee on Communications to maintain funding for PBS [0], but I'll leave this link here anyway, for others who might not have seen this. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":17625072,"author":"extralego","month":"2018-07","story":"Mr. Rogers was my actual neighbor","stance":"rec","why":"Rogers presenting to the Senate committee in 1969","quote":"Incase anyone hasn’t seen this video of him presenting before the senate committee in 1969","text":"Incase anyone hasn’t seen this video of him presenting before the senate committee in 1969, in support of national public television. [VIDEO]"},{"id":10699560,"author":"hitekker","month":"2015-12","story":"How Elmo Ruined Sesame Street","stance":"rec","why":"Shows how effective Fred Rogers was as a speaker","quote":"I encourage you to watch this,","text":"> Mister Rogers For those who didn't grow up with Mister Rogers, I encourage you to watch this, [VIDEO] , to get a sense of how effective he was as a speaker."},{"id":47780445,"author":"raw_anon_1111","month":"2026-04","story":"Apple App Store threatened to remove Grok over deepfakes: Letter","stance":"reference","why":"Funding attacks date back to at least 1969","quote":"They have been trying to get rid of funding since at least 1969","text":"So exactly when was the US a “good democracy”? 80 years ago segregation was still in the South based on a ruling by the Supreme Court “separate but equal”. Even until the 80s it was legal to arrest a homosexual couple for having sex in their own home based on “sodomy” laws. Today women are dying because doctors are afraid of performing medical necessary abortions to save their lives because they might go to jail They have been trying to get rid of funding since at least 1969 when Mr. Rogers himself went before Congress to try to keep it. [VIDEO] It amazes me that anyone who knows anything about this country actually wants to give it more control of the media or any increased power ."},{"id":39125503,"author":"gjvc","month":"2024-01","story":"Mr. Rogers' music was intricate (2020) [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Rogers explains his view on children and television","quote":"as I think he explains in his statement to the Senate Subcommitte on Communications in 1969","text":"for children who had the television as a babysitter and that's the difference, as I think he explains in his statement to the Senate Subcommitte on Communications in 1969 [VIDEO]"},{"id":32292123,"author":"btilly","month":"2022-07","story":"Mr. Rogers sued the KKK (2021)","stance":"reference","why":"Rogers spoke to Congress the way he spoke to children","quote":"he spoke to them in the way he spoke to all humans.","text":"In other words, he spoke to children as if they are inexperienced humans. No, he spoke to them in the way he spoke to all humans. For example here he is talking to Congress: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24877483,"author":"s5300","month":"2020-10","story":"A conversation with Shel Kaphan, Amazon’s first employee (2016)","stance":"reference","why":"Rogers credited with saving PBS funding","quote":"the U.S. (of which I'm a citizen) has a singular entity to thank for the fact PBS is here today.","text":"No. They don't. As a matter of fact, the U.S. (of which I'm a citizen) has a singular entity to thank for the fact PBS is here today. [VIDEO] And, to add some insult, one of our two leading political parties is currently making fun of that entity. Things are great over here."},{"id":17272035,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2018-06","story":"Mr. Rogers Had a Simple Set of Rules for Talking to Children","stance":"reference","why":"Rogers asking Congress for public television funding","quote":"Here is Mister Rogers talking to the children in Congress","text":"I agree 100% with Berkeley Breathed when he wrote: Mister Rogers: Welcome to my neighborhood, boys and girls! Did you enjoy meeting Senator Kravitz today? I did. He told us about something called \"inflation,\" didn't he? Can YOU say \"inflation?\" Opus: Inphlabph. Mister Rogers: Good! Can you say \"Mister Rogers should be paid more dough?\" Opus: Mister Rogers should - Mister Rogers: Can you put it on a postcard? [LINK] I believe that was a Soupy Sales reference: [LINK] [LINK] Here is Mister Rogers talking to the children in Congress, successfully asking them to send him millions of little green pieces of paper: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["LrObZ_HZZUc",1],["z1ROpIKZe-c",1],["1OfxlSG6q5Y",1],["pcVGqtmd2wM",1]]},"wbSwFU6tY1c":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as a landmark launch broadcast, and also dissect details like the duplicated booster landing feeds.","q":{"x":"You should watch the launch, its probably one of the most interesting engineering projects of the last few years.","i":16383544,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":47618795,"author":"NitpickLawyer","month":"2026-04","story":"Artemis II will use laser beams to live-stream 4K moon footage at 260 Mbps","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Epic launch footage that even inspired sci-fi","quote":"is one of the greatest stuff I've seen in space launches","text":"> I think everyone forgot about early SpaceX product quality. This was 8 years ago and is one of the greatest stuff I've seen in space launches. The footage is so epic that it even got replicated in SciFi series! ... [VIDEO] This was 9 years ago, first droneship landing - [LINK] And this is 18 years ago, their first Falcon1 launch - [LINK] More live video from the ascent than we got on Artemis2 for sure..."},{"id":42734145,"author":"somenameforme","month":"2025-01","story":"Starship Flight 7","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Inspiring broadcast, best watched all the way through","quote":"Such an unbelievable moment.","text":"Just to link that: [VIDEO] Such an unbelievable moment. And I also think an indicator of how much better society could be if we focused more on doing amazing things. The comments on YouTube are just filled with hope optimism and general awesomeness. FWIW that link goes straight to the moneyshot - it's always so much better if you watch it all the way through. It's an amazing broadcast."},{"id":37996771,"author":"somenameforme","month":"2023-10","story":"NASA just sent a software update to a spacecraft 12B miles away","stance":"strong_rec","why":"First successful Falcon Heavy test launch","quote":"I'd strongly encourage checking out their first successful test launch of it","text":"Falcon Heavy has been launching commercial, government, and military payloads successfully for years now. [1] I'd strongly encourage checking out their first successful test launch of it, from 5 years ago. [2] Skip to about 29 minutes in for the money shot. Still gives me goose bumps! So let's compare the two! Falcon Heavy was developed with completely private funding, at a cost of ~$500 million. The prices of the launches are listed in Wiki. They start at $117 million, as NASA paid in 2023 to launch their Psyche craft. The SLS has been in development for more than a decade now, fully paid by the taxpayer, at a cost just about to break the $30 billion mark. The nominal cost per launch is still unknown but will almost certainly top a billion dollars. The last NASA administration gave an estimate of $800-$900 million per launch in 2019 dollars. And SLS cost estimates only go one way. And what do you get for $30 billion, and then another billion per launch? Ideally up to almost twice the capacity of a Falcon Heavy. The reason people are so cool on the SLS is that it's already a very poor value against existing technologies. And will be completely obsolete by the time Starship comes to pass which should not only be able to launch for a fraction of the cost of Falcon Heavy (which is already a fraction of the cost of SLS), but also launch well over the ideal launch capacity of SLS. [1] - [LINK] [2] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":33423069,"author":"somenameforme","month":"2022-11","story":"Falcon Heavy Launch [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Landing video leaves viewers in awe","quote":"It really is just that amazing.","text":"It's hard to measure objectively but I feel I was dramatically more impressed seeing first Falcon Heavy come down than I was seeing the first successful (non-heavy) landing. I've shown a lot of people (who aren't otherwise into space) the original Falcon Heavy landing video: [VIDEO] The most common response, by far, is: \"Is that real?\" It really is just that amazing. Even look at the YouTube comments. It's enough to make even the internet become a place of shared awe, wonder, and inspiration. Makes one wonder what the world would be like if we had media that focused more on things like this and less on things that divide and agitate people."},{"id":23095378,"author":"tpmx","month":"2020-05","story":"SpaceX's Starship SN4 prototype fires rocket engine for first time","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Historic launch, one of the best executed live streams","quote":"That was one the best executived live streams of all time","text":"I remember seeing it around or just after midnight when I was visiting a customer in Taipei. I had fallen asleep two hours earlier, but set a timer to wake up just for this historic event. Totally worth it. That was one the best executived live streams of all time: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16383544,"author":"codeulike","month":"2018-02","story":"Capturing Starman from 1M miles away","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Falcon Heavy launch as top engineering project","quote":"You should watch the launch, its probably one of the most interesting engineering projects of the last few years.","text":"You should watch the launch, its probably one of the most interesting engineering projects of the last few years. [VIDEO]"},{"id":18051061,"author":"TangoTrotFox","month":"2018-09","story":"SpaceX's rise can be traced to a critical launch from a Pacific isle","stance":"reference","why":"Comments show optimism around Falcon Heavy launch","quote":"","text":"I think that's most likely just you projecting your own views. See people's reactions to their launches. YouTube is the home to some of the most antagonistic commentary, yet check out the comments to the Falcon Heavy Launch. If you can make YouTube look like a great group of enthusiastic, optimistic, unified aspirants - then it's safe to say they're already bringing the world like nothing before. And that's with little more than the launch of a special rocket. When SpaceX is putting people on another planet, I think we will have entered into the beginning of a new era in humanity. [1] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":17648179,"author":"TangoTrotFox","month":"2018-07","story":"Take Off to 1960s Space Motels","stance":"reference","why":"Positive YouTube reaction to the launch","quote":"See how people responded to this [1] video of the Falcon Heavy test flight.","text":"We are, at this very moment, living through what will likely be seen as one of the most important and keystone eras in humankind. We are finally taking the steps to move, as a people -- as a species, into space. This is something that will completely transform our species, forever. People millennia from now will wonder how this came to be - how the time that we're living through played out, how people responded to such change. The reality that most people are completely unaware would probably be quite disappointing. One-off bursts of coverage are forgotten as quickly as they emerge. For instance, if we were to poll Americans on our recent achievements in space, what percent of Americans do you think would have any clue about what's going on? Even just of the Falcon Heavy in particular? I think a fair over/under would be 20%. In my experience it's a fair bit lower than that. And the biggest shame here is that people are just jaw dropped at seeing what we're achieving - which makes that ignorance all the more of a shame! Heck, YouTube is home of some the most negative and trollish commenting on the internet. See how people responded to this [1] video of the Falcon Heavy test flight. That a 'mere' technological achievement somehow brought out such strong and positive emotion from so many people, on YouTube of all places. Why aren't the media more actively tapping into that? The media plays a very large role in deciding the zeitgeist of a time. And I think it's a shame that they'"},{"id":16567193,"author":"Mithaldu","month":"2018-03","story":"Falcon Heavy and Starman [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Timestamp showing presenters reacting to empty deck","quote":"Watch exactly one minute after this timestamp","text":"Oooh, so you're simply unaware and your bias prompts you to assume i'm making shit up. Here, let me fix that. Watch exactly one minute after this timestamp, pay attention to the screen at the right edge of the video, in the middle: [VIDEO] Drone ship feed static for a while, then smoke on deck clears, no core visible, deck empty. Both go \"oh.\" and after a pause: \"we're waiting to hear what happens. [...] we'll let you know as soon as we find out\" and \"we'll [...] know [...] whether it's standing\". It is clear during all of this that they're looking at the same video feed as is in the background, because the timing of their reactions is exactly in synch. They saw it didn't land, they found out immediately and didn't let \"us\" know. Instead they claimed it might be possible it is standing despite seeing the empty deck. PS: You could've asked instead of engaging in cheer pressure."},{"id":16319590,"author":"tobijkl","month":"2018-02","story":"SpaceX Falcon Heavy launch webcast (live 12:45 PST)","stance":"reference","why":"Evidence booster landing footage was duplicated","quote":"they used the same footage for both boosters","text":"If you look at the footage closely you see that they used the same footage for both boosters. Nevertheless, the landing was superb. edit: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16319653,"author":"leetrout","month":"2018-02","story":"SpaceX’s Falcon Heavy successfully launches","stance":"reference","why":"Shows both landing feeds were the same","quote":"you could tell they were the same feed","text":"They said in the broadcast they weren't but when it got close to the ground you could tell they were the same feed. [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":16319682,"author":"quentusrex","month":"2018-02","story":"SpaceX’s Falcon Heavy successfully launches","stance":"reference","why":"Duplicate stream in the broadcast","quote":"it was a duplicate of the same stream","text":"There was a mistake as in the feed they claimed they were separate, but in fact it was a duplicate of the same stream: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16319770,"author":"sangnoir","month":"2018-02","story":"SpaceX’s Falcon Heavy successfully launches","stance":"reference","why":"Presenters' reaction at center core landing timestamp","quote":"","text":"> Do we know the fate of the centre core yet? I might be reading into something that's not there, but the presenters (I didn't get their names) acted like they received surprising news in thier monitors at 39:03[1] just as they were about to report on the drone ship landing. Dude: \"We've just gotten confirmation...\" Lady: \"oh!\" Dude: \"Oh\" Lady: \"[laughs] We are waiting to hear what happens...\" 1. [VIDEO]"},{"id":16319801,"author":"peterwaller","month":"2018-02","story":"SpaceX’s Falcon Heavy successfully launches","stance":"reference","why":"Last moments of center core before feed cut","quote":"these are the last few moments before it is lost from the feed","text":"Of the centre core, these are the last few moments before it is lost from the feed. Smoke can be seen... [VIDEO] ... and then back to the presenters. As someone said to me, \"That's their lying face!\" :) Can't fault them for wanting to dwell on the positives though, was an amazing moment to watch. Edit: You can switch cameras on the above youtube video to the countdown net; you can clearly hear them saying \"We lost the centre core\" at 38m30s - not sure if that means \"lost signal of\" or otherwise. The people in the control room appear to become more muted at that point, though they still seem composed. It's really not clear. Edit: On the countdown net you can hear some minutes later \"suspected loss of signal\": [LINK]"},{"id":16319825,"author":"leetrout","month":"2018-02","story":"SpaceX’s Falcon Heavy successfully launches","stance":"reference","why":"Host quote suggests they thought views were different boosters","quote":"I think they did think it was 2 different views.","text":"Ah interesting. I don't think that's what they meant. I think they did think it was 2 different views. [VIDEO] \"even though those look very similar those ... are representative of different boosters\""},{"id":16319857,"author":"daniel-wer","month":"2018-02","story":"SpaceX’s Falcon Heavy successfully launches","stance":"reference","why":"Mission control audio on center core loss","quote":"you can hear \"We have lost the center core","text":"Regarding the center core, if you switch to the second stream (mission control) and go to 38:30, you can hear \"We have lost the center core\" - could be referring to the signal though. [VIDEO]"},{"id":16319861,"author":"leetrout","month":"2018-02","story":"SpaceX’s Falcon Heavy successfully launches","stance":"reference","why":"Same glare in both feeds","quote":"the glare shows up in both feeds","text":"Agree, same view of the flame of the same booster, indicating it's the same camera on the same booster... the glare shows up in both feeds. [VIDEO] If it were two different cameras we'd see two different landing pads. In the end of the video both feeds are coming down on the northern pad. Edit: Here's the sat image from december with notes added [LINK]"},{"id":16330014,"author":"acdha","month":"2018-02","story":"Apple Backs AV1: What Does This Mean for the Future of Video Codecs","stance":"reference","why":"Used as a test stream for decoding CPU usage","quote":"","text":"> Works fine. There are no CPU fans, no struggling to maintain 30 frames per second. I've played back 1080p30 VP9 video in Firefox on a 12 year old Intel Core 2 Duo desktop with no issues. That's not the experience I've seen on newer hardware and there are plenty of issues like [LINK] , not to mention extensions like [LINK] / [LINK] with hundreds of thousands of users. > VP9 decodes faster than H.264 at same picture quality because the bitrate is lower That's comparing ffmpeg's decode performance on the CPU using a single clip from one video. What I'm talking about is the difference which hardware acceleration makes, especially since older systems also have older CPUs without the newer instructions used by high-performance software decoders. VP9 implementations have been increasingly optimized over the years but it's really challenging to beat hardware performance even before you consider the power budget. As a simple example, I opened [VIDEO] on a 2.13 GHz Intel Core 2 Duo (2010 MacBook Air). That's playing a 1280x720 stream. In all browsers, even with ads blocked by /etc/hosts I had to wait ~20 seconds for the 80+% CPU from the YouTube JavaScript to settle down before starting playback. In Safari, that video takes between 3 and 8% CPU usage with no dropped frames. In Chrome, that's 80-120% CPU usage and about a 5% dropped frame rate. Firefox is interesting: it ran at about 15-20% CPU usage, which is worse than Safari but still much better than Chrome. I thought that was odd"},{"id":16363578,"author":"Stratoscope","month":"2018-02","story":"SpaceX's Falcon Heavy center booster lacked ignition fluid to re-light engines","stance":"reference","why":"Monitor in background shows empty drone ship pad","quote":"there is a monitor at the far right center of the video that shows the continuing camera feed","text":"The video feed didn't stop working, or if it did, it was only for a brief moment. If you watch this part of the YouTube stream, after it cuts back to the two hosts there is a monitor at the far right center of the video that shows the continuing camera feed of the drone ship landing pad. Only part of the monitor is visible, but after the smoke clears over the next few minutes you can see the empty pad: [VIDEO] You can also see the surprised reactions of the hosts during these few minutes, and how relieved they were when John Insprucker joined them to wrap things up. It would have been interesting to be a fly buzzing around their earpieces to hear what the producers were telling them! People have speculated that with everything else in the mission being so spectacularly successful, they wanted the initial news reports to be about that success instead of the one core that failed to land. Knowing how the news works - \"Here's what's wrong with the world today!\" - I can sympathize with that."},{"id":16320404,"author":"huhtenberg","month":"2018-02","story":"SpaceX Falcon Heavy launch webcast (live 12:45 PST)","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"now it's obvious that they have a steady feed at all times, just censoring it","text":"So much for their constant deferrals to the \"signal loss\" due to \"drone ship vibration\" and other phony reasons. It sounded weird on all prior occasions, but at least now it's obvious that they have a steady feed at all times, just censoring it. What I don't understand why they think they need to lie in the first place. Just cut the damn feed and say \"the control cut the feed\". All these cutesy giggles, Oh's and Ah's, \"we are hoping to get it back soon\" when the commenters are clearly looking at the live feed themselves [1]... this is just wrong. [1] [VIDEO] - see the timing of them saying \"Oh\" and the smoke clearing off the drone ship."}],"r":[["XXBxV6-zamM",2],["ipAnwilMncI",1],["5Ge2RcvDlgw",1],["CK62I-4cuSY",1],["bvim4rsNHkQ",1],["nQpxAvjD_30",1],["j5v8D-alAKE",1],["5eW6Eagr9XA",1]]},"oHlpmxLTxpw":{"wy":"The standard link in threads about child autonomy, helicopter parenting and car-centric planning.","q":{"x":"Not Just Bikes has a really good video on why suburbia (usually single family housing) is often worse for children than denser environments.","i":35551205,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":39192060,"author":"malermeister","month":"2024-01","story":"Safetyism killed playtime","stance":"rec","why":"Moving to the Netherlands so kids can play and meet friends independently.","quote":"There's a great video by the urbanist youtuber Not Just Bikes about this very topic","text":"There's a great video by the urbanist youtuber Not Just Bikes about this very topic - he ended up moving to the Netherlands so his kids can safely play on the street and independently meet up with friends: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35551205,"author":"ignormies","month":"2023-04","story":"Kids Toys Suck (2011)","stance":"rec","why":"Why suburbia is worse for children than dense neighborhoods.","quote":"Not Just Bikes has a really good video on why suburbia (usually single family housing) is often worse for children than denser environments.","text":"The issue isn't purely house vs apartment. It's a symptom of our neighborhood design. Not Just Bikes has a really good video on why suburbia (usually single family housing) is often worse for children than denser environments. [VIDEO]"},{"id":34628506,"author":"CalRobert","month":"2023-02","story":"Connecticut Parents Arrested for Letting Kids Walk to Dunkin' Donuts","stance":"rec","why":"Case against raising kids in American suburbs.","quote":"Yet again this video on how there is something deeply, horrifyingly wrong with raising kids in American suburbs is apt.","text":"Yet again this video on how there is something deeply, horrifyingly wrong with raising kids in American suburbs is apt. [VIDEO] Visited Utrecht in December and visiting Freiburg in April to decide where to live."},{"id":33629585,"author":"noahtallen","month":"2022-11","story":"Mom handcuffed, jailed for 8-year-old son walking half a mile","stance":"rec","why":"How suburban infrastructure removes children's independence.","quote":"There are a lot of excellent videos about this sad problem, like this","text":"Well, part of the problem is our infrastructure choices over the past many decades. When you build suburbs that are very isolated from places you need to go and then need to drive to literally everything you do, nearly everyone starts viewing normal activities like walking to a grocery store as abnormal. As a result, kids can’t actually go anywhere until they turn 16 and get a car. :/ There are a lot of excellent videos about this sad problem, like this: [VIDEO] But at the end of the day, when it’s not actually possible to be independent every day, you just loose this culture. And countries that made different infrastructure and development decisions 6 decades ago are in a much better place today — it doesn’t have to be this way."},{"id":33281697,"author":"NegativeLatency","month":"2022-10","story":"Barcelona-style “superblocks” could make cities greener and less car-centric","stance":"rec","why":"Raising kids in non car-dependent places.","quote":"You might be interested in this","text":"Once the environment is safe for kids you don't have to do as many logistics for your children. You might be interested in this \"Not Just Bikes\" video about raising kids in non car dependent places: [VIDEO] or this one about kids who bike to school in a group for safety: [LINK]"},{"id":32363577,"author":"evolve2k","month":"2022-08","story":"Marc Andreessen says he’s for new housing, but records tell a different story","stance":"rec","why":"Differences between US and European cities.","quote":"NotJustBikes has some excellent videos on the differences between US and European cities","text":"NotJustBikes has some excellent videos on the differences between US and European cities, this one captures many of the issues - [VIDEO]"},{"id":32188450,"author":"xputer","month":"2022-07","story":"Why we ignore thousands of daily car crashes","stance":"rec","why":"Channel showing US road infrastructure falls short on safety.","quote":"Watch some videos from this channel","text":"Watch some videos from this channel and you'll be able to see how the US definitely does not do enough in terms of creating safe road infrastructure for all: [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":47189329,"author":"Mawr","month":"2026-02","story":"Cardiorespiratory fitness is associated with lower anger and anxiety","stance":"reference","why":"Kids can bike independently in cycling-friendly places.","quote":"","text":"Calling everything but cars exclusionary is mildly hilarious. First of all, you're simply forgetting about public transport. That's needed too and pretty much covers all your concerns. However... 1. The disabled? How many of those do you think are disabled in just the right ways to still be able to drive? You know \"disabled\" doesn't just mean \"missing a limb\", right? So uh, if the only option is to drive and someone's disabled and therefore can't drive, what do they... do? Anyhow: [1] 2. Kids? How many kids do you see driving cars around? You know 10+ year olds can just... bike on their own, right? Like, to school, etc.? [2] [3] [4] Below that age, just bike them around? [5] ;) > Forcing You know nobody's forcing you to do anything, right? Like, you can still own and use your car, there are some very valid use cases best served by them. For example, around half of the Dutch own a car. They just don't use them as much for really dumb purposes, like driving 5km to a grocery store, because the nearest one is within walking or cycling distance. The problem is that driving has been so heavily subsidized, that we've come to take it for granted and are now truly shocked when asked to pay more to even somewhat offset the real costs. And I'm afraid there's no viable path forward that doesn't involve eventually paying in full. > Forcing people to bike and walk everywhere sounds a lot better if you're in a moderate climate where bad weather means you need to pack a light jacket and wait"},{"id":43990006,"author":"andrepd","month":"2025-05","story":"Changes since congestion pricing started in New York","stance":"reference","why":"Kids do better where they can bike with friends.","quote":"Kids are infinitely better off somewhere they can just bike places with their friends","text":"I'm already imagining what kind of arguments you are preparing. Kids are infinitely better off somewhere they can just bike places with their friends, compared to a car-centric hellhole: [VIDEO]"},{"id":40310729,"author":"iknowstuff","month":"2024-05","story":"Developing Palo Alto is worth $1T","stance":"reference","why":"Dutch kids' independence versus North American suburban upbringing.","quote":"the stark contrast between the freedom and independence of Dutch children versus the restrictive upbringing in car-dependent suburbs of North America","text":"[VIDEO] Dangers of Car-Dependent Suburbs: How Urban Planning Affects Parenting In this segment, the speaker highlights the stark contrast between the freedom and independence of Dutch children versus the restrictive upbringing in car-dependent suburbs of North America. By comparing their own childhood in London, Ontario, to their current experiences in Amsterdam, the speaker argues that the Dutch city's design and culture enable children to develop autonomy and independence, whereas North American suburbs lack the necessary infrastructure and social norms to allow children to thrive without excessive parental supervision"},{"id":40369054,"author":"Mawr","month":"2024-05","story":"Parking reform legalized most of the new homes in Buffalo and Seattle (2023)","stance":"reference","why":"Kids riding independently given good cycling infrastructure.","quote":"they could just ride there themselves [2].","text":"Wouldn't it be nice if the only other car traffic on the road was there because of use cases similarly niche to yours? Public transportation is great at eliminating the need for using a car for completely overkill trips like commuting and basic shopping. Having said that, given good cycling infrastructure, chances are you could either bring your kids along on a cargo bike [1] or they could just ride there themselves [2]. [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38379688,"author":"k99x55","month":"2023-11","story":"Children, left behind by suburbia, need better community design","stance":"reference","why":"Car-centric streets leave no space for children.","quote":"Not Just Bikes video on suburbia","text":"Screens are part of the problem but the biggest cause is lack of space to be a child, and the biggest culprit is our car-centric urban planning which transformed our streets into car storage space / racing track. Our streets have become hostile to anything but cars. Not Just Bikes video on suburbia: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36589897,"author":"CalRobert","month":"2023-07","story":"Dutch rules will soon prevent schoolchildren from having a phone in classroom","stance":"reference","why":"Summarizes the case against car-dependent suburbs for kids.","quote":"This is the gist of it","text":"This is the gist of it: [VIDEO] But I grew up in central California and surroundings in the 90's and rode my bike everywhere. And it was horrible. I nearly died multiple times, and drivers were sometimes furious at someone biking on the road. I rode from Sac to Folsom once over a narrow bridge with no shoulder and can't believe I lived. I thought it might be better in cities, but Berkeley, while better, was still mediocre, and Santa Monica was bad. Really, everywhere in the US is somewhere on the spectrum from \"merely horrible\" to \"dire hellscape\". Ten years ago my wife and I moved to Ireland. Ireland is also bad, but still massively better than anywhere I've lived in the US. But now we have young kids, and we don't want \"better than the US\", we want \"good enough your child can bike to school, and when people kill children with their cars the people are angry with the driver, not sympathetic towards them\". Which means the Netherlands, or perhaps Denmark. So that's where we're going. Ireland being wildly incompetent bureaucratically it took 4 years just to process mine and my wife's naturalisation applications."},{"id":35419097,"author":"tester457","month":"2023-04","story":"Giving kids no autonomy at all has become a parenting norm","stance":"reference","why":"Sprawl correlates with reduced autonomy for children.","quote":"There is a correlation with urban sprawl and the autonomy of children[1].","text":"There is a correlation with urban sprawl and the autonomy of children[1]. Depending on where you live, it's harder young people to get around compared to 40 years ago. Like this HN user's experience in this same thread[2]. By making society car centric we have made it difficult for children to exercise independence even if their parents allowed them to. In a non metro area your options as a teen to go somewhere are to beg your parents to drive you somewhere or to walk for hours to a bus stop. These barriers and the rise in pedestrian fatalities do not incentivize autonomy. [1] [VIDEO] Why we won't raise our kids in suburbia [2] [LINK]"},{"id":35008119,"author":"moving_sofa","month":"2023-03","story":"Honestly, It's Probably the Phones","stance":"reference","why":"Car-centric planning denies mobility to young and old.","quote":"What also contributes to <18 (and the elderly) social isolation is the car-centric urban planning","text":"What also contributes to <18 (and the elderly) social isolation is the car-centric urban planning which denies mobility freedom to this demographics. This is even more relevant in North America Why We Won't Raise Our Kids in Suburbia: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32544948,"author":"bewcin","month":"2022-08","story":"Ask HN: Where Is the Best Place to Live in SoCal?","stance":"reference","why":"Why suburbs are poor for families without cars.","quote":"Relevant video","text":"My family had the same mindset when we moved to SoCal in 2011. Unfortunately, you may want to throw dreams of \"walkable\" out the window. In SoCal you will absolutely need a car. The best schools are usually in the suburbs, but the suburbs are always the least walkable areas. Relevant video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31957808,"author":"stewartmcgown","month":"2022-07","story":"New California law requires high schools to start later","stance":"reference","why":"Suburban car dependence limits children's independence.","quote":"65% of kids in America are driven to school by their parents.","text":"should be in school by 7am or it won't build character? 65% of kids in America are driven to school by their parents. the socioeconomic state of that country including mass, sprawling suburbanisation with inherent car-addiction has made it very difficult for children to achieve even a modicum of agency in their lives. I do not find it surprising the way it is impacting on American children. source: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32268876,"author":"cardanome","month":"2022-07","story":"ESG should be boiled down to one simple measure: emissions","stance":"reference","why":"Dutch cities as safe places to raise kids.","quote":"Cities in the Netherlands are very good and safe places to raise kids in","text":"Cities in the Netherlands are very good and safe places to raise kids in: [VIDEO] It is just a matter for human instead of car-centric city planning. This will also help with poverty and crime."},{"id":31732839,"author":"david-gpu","month":"2022-06","story":"Ask HN: Have You Regretted Immigrating?","stance":"reference","why":"North American cities' car dependence compared with better alternatives.","quote":"how car-dependent they are [0].","text":"Toronto, by any chance? One other thing that frustrates me about most cities in North America is how car-dependent they are [0]. [0] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["bglWCuCMSWc",2],["040ejWnFkj0",1],["Ot4qdCs54ZE",1],["YHLU0Uej1WA",1],["REni8Oi1QJQ",1],["KPUlgSRn6e0",1],["r-TuGAHR78w",1],["0lvUByM-fZk",1],["CTV-wwszGw8",1],["bnKIVX968PQ",1],["uxykI30fS54",1],["d8RRE2rDw4k",1],["42oQN7fy_eM",1],["ORzNZUeUHAM",1],["Uhx-26GfCBU",1],["M8F5hXqS-Ac",1],["7IsMeKl-Sv0",1],["GwRRSJ_wtIg",1],["d6DBKoWbtjE",1]]},"tISy7EJQPzI":{"wy":"Recommended whenever versioning, semver, monorepos and backward compatibility are debated, for its nuanced treatment of dependencies.","q":{"x":"a much more nuanced look at dependencies & versioning","i":24926736,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":25218504,"author":"ithkuil","month":"2020-11","story":"An ex-Googler’s guide to dev tools","stance":"rec","why":"Large-scale code changes via automation and backward-compatible increments","quote":"I recommend this talk","text":"Usually that problem is dealt with combination of: * Automation. * Ability to change other people's code safely, possibly gated by code review approval. Cultural and technical (e.g. unit tests must exist and be maintained) barriers may apply. * Making incremental changes that are backward compatible (at least until you migrated all clients so you can cleanup later) I recommend this talk [VIDEO] (even if the scope is broader than your question) (ex-googler)"},{"id":24926736,"author":"klodolph","month":"2020-10","story":"Bazel Build System Support for LLVM","stance":"rec","why":"Nuanced look at dependencies and versioning beyond semver","quote":"a much more nuanced look at dependencies & versioning","text":"> If you go listen to Google people talk about dependency/version management. It's like they think it's global lock vs inconsistency. Monorepo or chaos. This false choice irks me. I watched this talk: [VIDEO] I’d say this is a much more nuanced look at dependencies & versioning. We all know these days that something like semantic versioning is not enough, because it is too easy to depend on behavior which the developers of your dependencies did not expect you to depend on. So you need something more powerful than just having a bunch of developers get together and agree not to break each others’ code. Bazel is a part of that solution—if you can analyze and understand the dependencies in a project, and do so in a very automatic and reliable way, you can provide people with tools tools for updating downstream code when upstream code changes. I think people are focusing too much on Bazel as only a build system and not as part of a platform for building developer tooling, and people are focusing too much on Bazel’s original incarnation as a build system for a massive monorepo. I think there’s also a reality that we must deal with that is—as software gets larger and more complex, and as we have more dependencies, relying on version pinning for stability is losing ground as a technique for making our software more reliable. We have many more dependencies in our software now, so just subscribing to a mail feed of all of the version bumps in the libraries you use would be enough to dr"},{"id":23982471,"author":"s3cur3","month":"2020-07","story":"Stop Abusing Semver","stance":"rec","why":"Hyrum's law and testing to always take the latest dependency","quote":"at sufficient scale, there’s no such thing as a non-breaking change","text":"For another perspective on “semver is a lie,” see the “Live at Head” talk from Titus Winters of the Google core C++ team. [VIDEO] TL;DW: By Hyrum’s law, at sufficient scale, there’s no such thing as a non-breaking change (including changing comments). Embrace that, and test sufficiently that you can always take the latest dependency but detect when it breaks."},{"id":21383747,"author":"kinjba11","month":"2019-10","story":"Why async fn in traits are hard","stance":"rec","why":"Diamond dependency problem and semantic versioning limits","quote":"This talk is one of the better ones about this","text":"Semantic versioning isn't a silver bullet, unfortunately, due to the 'Diamond Dependency Problem'. That is, A depends on B and C, but B and C depend on different versions of D. Easy to fix, until you scale that up to thousands of nodes in a dependency graph. This talk is one of the better ones about this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19694362,"author":"jacobparker","month":"2019-04","story":"Keeping master green at scale","stance":"rec","why":"Related talk on live at head approach from Google","quote":"Here's a good related talk: C++ as a \"Live at Head\" language","text":"> You are totally correct that to achieve the same performance, correctness, and overall master level \"green\"ness in a multirepo system you would have to either define or detect dependencies and dependent repos, build the entire affected chain, and test the result. That part is much easier in monorepo. You also would need to do that as an atomic operation (in the mono-repo + especially with a commit queue you're building on the atomicity of git). Having to unwinding that transaction if you aren't atomic can get you into a big mess at larger scales. Here's a good related talk: C++ as a \"Live at Head\" language: [VIDEO] by Titus Winters (from Google)."},{"id":16365037,"author":"ot","month":"2018-02","story":"Advantages of monolithic version control","stance":"rec","why":"Google talk on C++ and eliminating versioning","quote":"Relevant talk by Google at CppCon last year focused on C++, but really talks about getting rid of versioning in general.","text":"Relevant talk by Google at CppCon last year focused on C++, but really talks about getting rid of versioning in general. [VIDEO]"},{"id":15891583,"author":"nsm","month":"2017-12","story":"Why Google stores billions of lines of code in a single repository (2016)","stance":"rec","why":"Google C++ team on building from source in a monorepo","quote":"There is a great talk by their C++ dev team","text":"There is a great talk by their C++ dev team about how a build everything from source, mono repo really helps with C++ change enablement [VIDEO] Personally, I’d have to agree that at a minimum a mono repo per distinct product role (server side in one mono repo, desktop software in one mono repo) is immensely valuable."},{"id":32354573,"author":"Xunjin","month":"2022-08","story":"C23 is finished: Here is what is on the menu","stance":"reference","why":"Source of the engineering-over-time quote","quote":"Engineering is programming integrated over time","text":"This kind of comment is not helpful at all, I use Rust today however, yet I cannot sometimes deny, it's better to use C depending on the target and restrictions by hardware compilers. Every language has downsides/upsides and time. There is a nice quote by Titus Winters: “Engineering is programming integrated over time”[0], this helped me a lot in learning that context matter for tools/languages/frameworks, and you can learn with their mistakes. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24492816,"author":"JamesSwift","month":"2020-09","story":"Go’s Major Versioning Sucks – From a Fanboy","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on how breaking changes are broader than semver admits","quote":"","text":"100% agree. Semver is a _social_ construct not a _technical_ one. There is Semver the spec as originally designed, and Semver as it is currently observed. Semver has become something other than it was originally intended, likely because the idea of \"breaking changes\" ends up being way more encompassing than people wanted to admit [1]. To ignore that reality is a futile fight. [1] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":23982859,"author":"rurounijones","month":"2020-07","story":"Stop Abusing Semver","stance":"reference","why":"Talk covering issues with semantic versioning","quote":"he goes into good details about issues with Semver.","text":"Not that cut and tried as it depends what \"goes into production\" means. As an example: Lets say I have an open-source library that I am developing. It is currently version 0.x.y and someone depends on it (without my knowledge because that is usually how it is). Is that now in Production? Should I be forced to make it 1.0.0 even though I am still making large numbers of breaking changes? That is a simple example off the top of my head. I am watching [VIDEO] (Linked from another comment) and he goes into good details about issues with Semver."},{"id":23269297,"author":"joshuamorton","month":"2020-05","story":"Goals and Priorities for C++","stance":"reference","why":"Abseil provides five years of compatibility plus update tooling","quote":"they will provide backwards compatibility for 5 years, and provide tooling to update source code to new constructs","text":"Specifically, absl (Google's C++ library already provides this guarantee). In general the idea is that they will provide backwards compatibility for 5 years, and provide tooling to update source code to new constructs. This is covered somewhere in this talk: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22710360,"author":"lytigas","month":"2020-03","story":"What If C++ Abandoned Backward Compatibility?","stance":"reference","why":"Google's Codebase Cultivator explaining tooling-based upgrades for Abseil","quote":"Here's Google's \"Codebase Cultivator\" explaining the idea for their Abseil library","text":"Some here are saying \"Why not just use Rust/D/Zig/Nim/Crystal if you're going to break backwards compatibility?\" I believe the proposal is closer to \"We've removed this old feature, run this tool to replace it with the new one.\" C++ will keep looking like C++, not like Rust. Here's Google's \"Codebase Cultivator\" explaining the idea for their Abseil library: [VIDEO] I remember watching another talk where they propose something similar for the language itself, but I can't find it at the moment."},{"id":19799144,"author":"joshuamorton","month":"2019-05","story":"Benefits of a Monorepo","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> add a huge tree of dependencies without having to think about it. Keep in mind that the alternative is \"I have to build (or pull a prebuilt version of) the entirety of every repo that I depend on\". An entire repo is always going to be larger than a single target. Yes you're right that pulling in the entirely of {{some large tool}} because you want `tool.strings.util.mangle_name` is bad. The alternatives are 1. (multi-repo) You stick mangle name in its own repo (think `left-pad`) 2. (monorepo) Depend on `tool` in its entirety 3. (either, though more common in multi-repo) Everyone re-implements mangle_name. If the way the name is mangled is important, and it changes in the future, you have major issues and incompatibilities. (think IPV4 -> IPV6 for an example where a \"global\" `parse_addr` function would have made everyone's lives easier). You can bet on the assumption that mangle_name will never change, but then you aren't doing software engineering[1] All of these are the wrong solution 4. Core utilities like this get factored out into their own files. In a multi-repo environment, everyone depends on the entire core/util repo. In a monorepo environment, you depend on the granular files you need. 4 is the only correct solution, and note that in a monorepo environment it results in you depending on fewer things than in a multi-repo environment. Yes there are a lot of ways to do things wrong, But that's true no matter what tooling you use. When used \"correctly\", monorepos have "},{"id":16272811,"author":"ndh2","month":"2018-01","story":"C++ and the Culture of Complexity (2013)","stance":"reference","why":"Google has tooling to automatically upgrade C++ code","quote":"","text":"Lots of posts here frame it like \"Yes, it's a bit of a complex language, but the C++ committee has the difficult job of keeping everything backwards compatible, so it's understandable.\" But that's not the reality. The reality is that they (or Stroustroup) made that decision, and that decision was a mistake. The correct way to deal with backwards compatibility issues is the way Go does it, namely to write tools to automatically upgrade code to a newer API [1]. And as that smug Google guy explained here [2], they have something like this for C++, too. They just don't bother sharing it with us peasants. The fundamental mistake of C++ is to religiously keep backwards-compatibility with everything for all eternity. It's so easy to say that backwards-compatibility is a good thing. It's obvious! But ultimately that decision is the reason for all the problems that C++ has. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["KTzGBJPuJwM",1],["mceaM2_zQd8",1],["9crggox5rbc",1],["OBPpRqxY8Uw",1],["7ZVWIELHQQY",1],["yCsgoLc_fzI",1],["jyQwgBAaBag",1],["jN7mSXMruEo",1],["baxtyeFVn3w",1],["bSkpMdDe4g4",1]]},"IV3dnLzthDA":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it whenever leaded fuel, toxic chemicals or corporate profit motives come up, praising its storytelling and accessible science.","q":{"x":"here's an excellent one by Veritasium on how it happened.","i":38039511,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":38039511,"author":"belltaco","month":"2023-10","story":"EPA moves towards banning leaded aviation gas","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Fascinating storytelling with accessible science.","quote":"here's an excellent one by Veritasium on how it happened.","text":"If you want a fascinating story telling video with a lot of sciency stuff for the relative layman, here's an excellent one by Veritasium on how it happened. \"The Man Who Accidentally Killed The Most People In History\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":46350837,"author":"amiga386","month":"2025-12","story":"Why did we use leaded petrol for so long? (2017)","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why ethanol was passed over for lead.","quote":"Veritasium did a great video on Midgley","text":"Veritasium did a great video on Midgley: [VIDEO] And therein they give the reason why ethanol was passed over: a lot of it is required to be effective (~10% of the fuel mixture), seriously dampening the profit margin of fuel sales! It works, but tetraethyl lead is so much cheaper"},{"id":38718370,"author":"thret","month":"2023-12","story":"U.S. workers say banned weed killer in 50 countries is giving them Parkinson's","stance":"rec","why":"Explains inventions and how bad they were for society.","quote":"This youtube channel does a good job of explaining his inventions and just how bad for society they were.","text":"[VIDEO] This youtube channel does a good job of explaining his inventions and just how bad for society they were."},{"id":32688771,"author":"blensor","month":"2022-09","story":"FAA approves unleaded fuel for piston fleet","stance":"rec","why":"Story of inventor of tetraethyllead and its impact.","quote":"Have you seen this Veritasium video about the man who came up with adding tetraethyllead to gasonline","text":"Have you seen this Veritasium video about the man who came up with adding tetraethyllead to gasonline and it's negative impact on humanity? [VIDEO] Given how dangerous lead is in even the tiniest quantities it is really surprising that there has not been more pressure to change that."},{"id":31425470,"author":"jeffxtreme","month":"2022-05","story":"The impact of childhood lead exposure on adult personality (2021)","stance":"rec","why":"Story behind lead in gasoline; interesting though sad.","quote":"it's a really interesting albeit sad video","text":"(2021) Veritasium covers the story behind lead in gasoline in [VIDEO] , it's a really interesting albeit sad video. If I recall correctly, he mentions this study in the video."},{"id":31124272,"author":"wirelesspotat","month":"2022-04","story":"Regular blood donations can reduce “forever chemicals” in the bloodstream: study","stance":"rec","why":"Story of Midgley and leaded gasoline.","quote":"Veritasium has a great YouTube video about this","text":"I don't like how \"toxic forever chemicals in the bloodstream\" is a normal thing Veritasium has a great YouTube video about this: \"The Man Who Accidentally Killed The Most People In History\"[0] [0] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":48874318,"author":"dbg31415","month":"2026-07","story":"Leaded Gas Was a Known Poison the Day It Was Invented (2016)","stance":"reference","why":"Cited alongside a Franklin quote on lead.","quote":"","text":"The Man Who Accidentally Killed The Most People In History [VIDEO] > This, my dear friend, is all I can at present recollect on the Subject. You will see by it, that the Opinion of this mischievous Effect from Lead, is at least above Sixty Years old; and you will observe with Concern how long a useful Truth may be known, and exist, before it is generally receiv'd and practis'd on. > Benjamin Franklin, 1786"},{"id":45714835,"author":"gwd","month":"2025-10","story":"A worker fell into a nuclear reactor pool","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for inventor pouring lead additive over hands.","quote":"","text":"I mean, apparently the inventor of lead additive to gasoline used to pour the chemical over his hands to demonstrate how safe it was -- even though he knew it was actually quite toxic. So there are people who will knowingly give themselves small doses of poison to keep the money flowing. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":32008240,"author":"adolph","month":"2022-07","story":"Auto manufacturer family tree: Who owns what?","stance":"reference","why":"Video treatment of TEL and GM history.","quote":"Video treatment","text":"Oh, the link between GM and leaded gasoline makes more sense in that context. TEL had been identified chemically in the mid-19th century, but General Motors had discovered its effectiveness as an antiknock agent in 1921, after spending several years attempting to find an additive that was both highly effective and inexpensive. [LINK] Video treatment: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31377336,"author":"d0mine","month":"2022-05","story":"IQ scores are falling and have been for decades (2018)","stance":"reference","why":"Cited regarding lead's omission from IQ decline discussion.","quote":"","text":"It is illuminating that the paper doesn’t mention lead while discussing IQ decline in the recent decades. [VIDEO]"},{"id":31128352,"author":"mrmuagi","month":"2022-04","story":"Regular blood donations can reduce “forever chemicals” in the bloodstream: study","stance":"reference","why":"Cited on leaded fuel and profit motives.","quote":"","text":"The people vested in using lead in fuel said so out of profit motive, but it seems lead was widely known to be harmful for hundreads of years [0] before it was using it fuel. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":31196887,"author":"sschueller","month":"2022-04","story":"EU considers banning use of up to 12,000 toxic chemicals","stance":"reference","why":"Shows known toxins pushed into public use.","quote":"even chemicals or elements we know are toxic for hundreds of years get pushed into the public and cause huge harm","text":"Good because as we just seen in the latest Vartasium video [1], even chemicals or elements we know are toxic for hundreds of years get pushed into the public and cause huge harm. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["Qct6LKbneKQ",1],["XGo4PJd1lng",1],["qbKGw8MQ0i8",1],["jbkSRLYSojo",1]]},"helScS3coAE":{"wy":"HN readers recommend it as the best introduction to robust-first computing, where randomness and fault tolerance are built into the architecture.","q":{"x":"it's certainly esoteric, but has some extremely important properties.","i":14245485,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":14236973,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2017-05","story":"Robust-First Computing: Distributed City Generation (2015) [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Talk on an inherently robust sorting machine in the Movable Feast Machine simulator.","quote":"describes an inherently robust sorting machine, like \"sorting gas","text":"A Movable Feast Machine [1] is a \"Robust First\" asynchronous distributed fault tolerant cellular-automata-like computer architecture. The video \"Distributed City Generation\" [2] demonstrates how you can program a set of Movable Feast Machine rules that build a self-healing city that fills all available space with urban sprawl, and even repairs itself after disasters! The paper \"Local Routing in a new Indefinitely Scalable Architecture\" [2] by Trent Small explains how those rules work, how the city streets adaptively learn how to route the cars to nearby buildings they desire to find, and illustrated the advantages of \"Robust First\" computing: Abstract: Local routing is a problem which most of us face on a daily basis as we move around the cities we live in. This study proposes several routing methods based on road signs in a procedurally generated city which does not assume knowledge of global city structure and shows its overall efficiency in a variety of dense city environments. We show that techniques such as Intersection-Canalization allow for this method to be feasible for routing information arbitrarily on an architecture with limited resources. This talk \"Robust-first computing: Demon Horde Sort\" [4] by Dave Ackley describes an inherently robust sorting machine, like \"sorting gas\", implemented with the open source Movable Feast Machine simulator, available on github [5]. A Movable Feast Machine is similar in many ways to traditional cellular automata, except for a few "},{"id":14245485,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2017-05","story":"Esoteric programming paradigms","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Demon Horde Sort talk on robust sorting in the Movable Feast Machine.","quote":"it's certainly esoteric, but has some extremely important properties.","text":"I recently submitted a link about \"Robust First Computing\". It didn't get any response, but I'll repeat the link and description here, since it's certainly esoteric, but has some extremely important properties. Robust-First Computing: Distributed City Generation [LINK] A Movable Feast Machine [1] is a \"Robust First\" asynchronous distributed fault tolerant cellular-automata-like computer architecture. The video \"Distributed City Generation\" [2] demonstrates how you can program a set of Movable Feast Machine rules that build a self-healing city that fills all available space with urban sprawl, and even repairs itself after disasters! The paper \"Local Routing in a new Indefinitely Scalable Architecture\" [2] by Trent Small explains how those rules work, how the city streets adaptively learn how to route the cars to nearby buildings they desire to find, and illustrated the advantages of \"Robust First\" computing: Abstract: Local routing is a problem which most of us face on a daily basis as we move around the cities we live in. This study proposes several routing methods based on road signs in a procedurally generated city which does not assume knowledge of global city structure and shows its overall efficiency in a variety of dense city environments. We show that techniques such as Intersection-Canalization allow for this method to be feasible for routing information arbitrarily on an architecture with limited resources. This talk \"Robust-first computing: Demon Horde Sort\" [4] by "},{"id":34262052,"author":"camel-cdr","month":"2023-01","story":"Artificial Life Creation T-0 and Launching","stance":"rec","why":"Explains the general idea of asynchronous cellular automata on tiles.","quote":"I think this video explained the general idea quite well","text":"As I understood it: The T2 tiles are \"normal\" microcomputers, that run a special software stack implementing the cellular automata. The tiles communicate over the connections, and essentially just act like a bigger automata grid. But the interesting part is that there is no global synchronization, and this architecture could be scaled indefinitely wide, by just adding more tiles. The impressive/hard part is creating the asynchronous automata, where each cell can only see a fixed amount of neighbors, and creating something from that. Edit: I think this video explained the general idea quite well: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21858577,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2019-12","story":"Can Programming Be Liberated from the von Neumann Style? (1977) [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Reposted explanation of robust-first computing with demo and talk links.","quote":"I've posted about this before, but it's worth repeating here","text":"I've posted about this before, but it's worth repeating here: [LINK] DonHopkins on Oct 26, 2017 | parent | favorite | on: Cryptography with Cellular Automata (1985) [pdf] A \"Moveable Feast Machine\" is a \"Robust First\" asynchronous distributed fault tolerant cellular-automata-like computer architecture. It's similar to a Cellular Automata, but it different in several important ways, for the sake of \"Robust First Computing\". These differences give some insight into what CA really are, and what their limitations are. Cellular Automata are synchronous and deterministic, and can only modify the current cell: all cells are evaluated at once (so the evaluation order doesn't matter), so it's necessary to double buffer the \"before\" and \"after\" cells, and the rule can only change the value of the current (center) cell. Moveable Feast Machines are like asynchronous non-deterministic cellular automata with large windows that can modify adjacent cells. Here's a great example with an amazing demo and explanation, and some stuff I posted about it earlier: [LINK] Robust-first Computing: Distributed City Generation: [LINK] A Movable Feast Machine [1] is a \"Robust First\" asynchronous distributed fault tolerant cellular-automata-like computer architecture. The video \"Distributed City Generation\" [2] demonstrates how you can program a set of Movable Feast Machine rules that build a self-healing city that fills all available space with urban sprawl, and even repairs itself after disasters! The pa"},{"id":19576267,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2019-04","story":"A Conversation with Language Creators: Guido, James, Anders and Larry [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Demon Horde Sort exemplifies robust-first computing that tolerates random failures.","quote":"Check out how the city seamlessly regrows after you nuke it","text":"Actually, the Moveable Feast Machine is a \"Robust First\" asynchronous distributed fault tolerant cellular-automata-like computer architecture that DOES reason about what might happen if computers randomly explode in mid-program! (One of my favorite topics, that I've written about in other threads!) [LINK] Check out how the city seamlessly regrows after you nuke it: [LINK] > Robust-first Computing: Distributed City Generation: A rough video demo of Trent R. Small's procedural city generation dynamics in the Movable Feast Machine simulator. See [LINK] for more information. And here's a robust, self-healing membrane, that tolerates random failures (like tiny little exploding computers): [LINK] >To demonstrate the robustness of three membrane implementations in the MFM, an instance of each was placed side by side and subjected to a failure probability of 5e-5 per site per EPS. That is, each site is erased, on average, every 20,000 event cycles. Intercellular Transport also demonstrates some robust chemical and biological programming metaphors: [LINK] >Two cells are interconnected by self-healing wire. The magenta substance is being passed from the cell in the west to the cell in the east. The black particles are an ectoplasm, allowing the membrane to identify sites outside of the cell. Similarly, the yellow and blue particles are an endoplasm specific to the cell in which they reside, allowing the membrane to identify sites inside of it. Demon Horde Sort exemplifies the Robust Fi"},{"id":15560845,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2017-10","story":"Cryptography with Cellular Automata (1985) [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Robust-first computing examples and how it differs from cellular automata.","quote":"Here's a great example with an amazing demo and explanation","text":"A \"Moveable Feast Machine\" is a \"Robust First\" asynchronous distributed fault tolerant cellular-automata-like computer architecture. It's similar to a Cellular Automata, but it different in several important ways, for the sake of \"Robust First Computing\". These differences give some insight into what CA really are, and what their limitations are. Cellular Automata are synchronous and deterministic, and can only modify the current cell: all cells are evaluated at once (so the evaluation order doesn't matter), so it's necessary to double buffer the \"before\" and \"after\" cells, and the rule can only change the value of the current (center) cell. Moveable Feast Machines are like asynchronous non-deterministic cellular automata with large windows that can modify adjacent cells. Here's a great example with an amazing demo and explanation, and some stuff I posted about it earlier: [LINK] Robust-first Computing: Distributed City Generation: [LINK] A Movable Feast Machine [1] is a \"Robust First\" asynchronous distributed fault tolerant cellular-automata-like computer architecture. The video \"Distributed City Generation\" [2] demonstrates how you can program a set of Movable Feast Machine rules that build a self-healing city that fills all available space with urban sprawl, and even repairs itself after disasters! The paper \"Local Routing in a new Indefinitely Scalable Architecture\" [2] by Trent Small explains how those rules work, how the city streets adaptively learn how to route the ca"},{"id":10723354,"author":"Kapura","month":"2015-12","story":"It Probably Works: Probabilistic algorithms are all around us","stance":"rec","why":"Ackley's robustness-first model with randomness built into each cell.","quote":"Another interesting related field is Dave Ackley's Robustness-First Computing model[1]","text":"Fantastic article. Probabilistic algorithm design is fascinating to me because it's all about making sure that the system behaves as expected, rather than every individual operation being perfect. Implementing most random algorithms in practise strongly reminds me of studies of populations & behaviours of organic life (which is much better than the rube goldberg/spaghetti factories which sometimes arise as \"solutions\" to complex problems). Another interesting related field is Dave Ackley's Robustness-First Computing model[1], which operates with every atomic computational cell having some elements of randomness built in. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":22680836,"author":"raidicy","month":"2020-03","story":"Self Replicating Computer Programs","stance":"reference","why":"Source where the commenter first learned of the movable feast machine.","quote":"I first heard about it from this video[0]? He calls it a \"movable feast machine\".","text":"Is this at all related to \"Robust-first computing\"? I first heard about it from this video[0]? He calls it a \"movable feast machine\". What interested me about cellular automation is that in my reading of Neumann, it is said he was working on using cellular automata to construct fully replicating machines. It is also said he probably would have gone on to develop said architecture in full had he not died. Is your research in anyway related? I'm interested but woefully lacking in formal cs training. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":10606150,"author":"justifier","month":"2015-11","story":"The Movable Feast Machine","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for the simulation's shape as a simplified representation of a membrane.","quote":"","text":"the 'grid' changing bounds is the intended mode of existence in regard to demon horde the paper(o) states 'channel size increases performance' but this should be uncontroversial more resources give better results it will be an optimisation problem to determine necessary resource alocation for desired results the shape of the simulation was stated(i) to be a simplified representation of the functionality the paper discusses 'a movable membrane whose contents cannot diffuse out', the figure is an almost organic shape, much more adaptable than the rectangular simulation demonstration (o) comjnl.oxfordjournals.org/content/56/12/1450.full.pdf+htmlb (i) [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["v4Wy7gRGgeA",1],["RxD6H3ri8RI",1],["4nigRT2KmCE",1],["KVN_0qvuhhw",1],["yMjPqr1s-cg",1],["z_dt7NG38V4",1],["whPWKecazgM",1],["pqQSwQLDIK8",1],["AdNJ3fydeao",1],["8Gv0H-vPoDc",1],["wpOxbesRNBc",1],["7hwO8Q_TyCA",1],["mZyvIHYn2zk",1],["I4flQ8XdvJM",1],["8QiPFmIMxFc",1],["1iAJPoc23-M",1],["Jd3-eiid-Uw",1],["LDPMpc-ENqY",1]]},"DmT80OseAGs":{"wy":"Strongly recommended as a beautiful demonstration that APL can be read and reasoned about step by step.","q":{"x":"I've always liked this video, where one of the Dyalog developers effortlessly writes a Sudoku solver in APL","i":30796413,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":30796413,"author":"lizardactivist","month":"2022-03","story":"Dyalog APL 18.2","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Dyalog developer effortlessly writes Sudoku solver in APL","quote":"I've always liked this video, where one of the Dyalog developers effortlessly writes a Sudoku solver in APL","text":"I've always liked this video, where one of the Dyalog developers effortlessly writes a Sudoku solver in APL: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24172540,"author":"moonchild","month":"2020-08","story":"J Notation as a Tool of Thought","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Scholes' illustrative APL videos","quote":"John scholes's videos are excellent and very illustrative","text":"> My point was that general programming usually looks more like writing the sorting algorithm than simply calling it. Thus, I would be more interested in seeing real examples of how the properties of J allow you to write algorithms more concisely or otherwise better. Ah - I see. Here[1] is quicksort in j. John scholes's videos are excellent and very illustrative, though they deal with apl rather than j; conway's game of life[2] and sudoku[3]. > The article showed how J solves some mathematical problems with built in functions or their built-in modifiers. But in general programming, you won't find a function that does exactly what you wanted, maybe with a little adverb. You'll usually have to write that function yourself, to describe how it works in terms of some of those pre-existing functions. Right. This is true, but you generally stay much closer to the builtins in j than you do in other language. > Note that your second example could be expressed as something like > x.OrderBy(y => y[1]) > In C#, assuming x is int[N][M]. I understand that J's version can work with many other shapes of X, but that is hard to appreciate without seeing example of how that comes in handy when writing some other (hopefully well known) algorithm. Right; you mentioned sort predicates, so I was giving an example of a way in which you could sort according to another relation. My point is: being able to change the layout of your data so easily can obviate sort predicates. (Though I do think a sort a"},{"id":20729199,"author":"paliilap","month":"2019-08","story":"J can look like APL or English","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Scholes narrating APL solutions step by step","quote":"His videos on depth-first search (1) and solving sudoku (2) are also brilliant.","text":"That 1975 APL demonstration is lovely. Much has been preserved in Dyalog and GNU APL - it's very interesting to see how timeless the core APL functions are. I'm very fond of a number of videos by the late John Scholes narrating APL solutions step by step, including the well-known demonstration of Conway's Game of Life in APL (0). His videos on depth-first search (1) and solving sudoku (2) are also brilliant. (0) [LINK] (1) [LINK] (2) [VIDEO]"},{"id":20325129,"author":"geocar","month":"2019-07","story":"Non-English-based programming languages","stance":"strong_rec","why":"John Scholes' beautiful APL demonstrations","quote":"the late John Scholes did a number of absolutely beautiful demonstrations programming in APL","text":"APL is great. If you haven't seen them, the late John Scholes did a number of absolutely beautiful demonstrations programming in APL[1], [2], [3]. [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO] [3]: [LINK]"},{"id":22523138,"author":"kick","month":"2020-03","story":"Stages of denial in encountering K","stance":"reference","why":"Shows APL can be read and talked about","quote":"some videos disproving your statement","text":"I'm very much a believer in concise notation (more than most people I've worked with), but the advantages of having a name that can be unambiguously spoken are large enough to outweigh a small constant factor. I don't believe this holds up to scrutiny. APL as a language was made to be spoken, and it seems to have succeeded in that. Alan Perlis briefly touched on that in a great paper,[1] and just about everyone who's worked with the language and its derivatives agrees that your statement isn't true. The idea that APL and its derivatives can't be \"read and talked about\" is frankly ignoring reality by any stretch of the imagination. For example, here's [2] [3] [4] some videos disproving your statement, the first two being APL, the last being k itself. For extra bonus points, see Aaron Hsu's talks [5]; pick any of them, they're all pretty good, and most have a segment in them explaining how that viewpoint is wrong. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO] [4] [LINK] [5] [LINK]"},{"id":9901399,"author":"wickawic","month":"2015-07","story":"Solving Every Sudoku Puzzle (2006)","stance":"reference","why":"Sudoku solver in APL as insight into coder","quote":"Here are some other sudoku solvers that give more insight into the coder than the problem","text":"I've noticed that solving a sudoku is something of a rosetta stone for how people program. Norvig's solution is very straightforward in hindsight, but I think that code was either the result of many iterations or of a career of making progressively more readable code. Here are some other sudoku solvers that give more insight into the coder than the problem: Sudoku in APL: [VIDEO] Test driven sudoku: [LINK]"}],"r":[["DsZdfnlh_d0",2],["CBrj4S24074",1],["a9xAKttWgP4",1]]},"Sp9H0MO-qS8":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a clear, enjoyable look at why simple designs are brilliant, and as a pointer to a channel with a hacker's-eye view of everyday technology.","q":{"x":"Anyone interested in how the traditional american coffee maker works should watch this.","i":34315000,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":34313980,"author":"LesZedCB","month":"2023-01","story":"My Coffee Maker Just Makes Coffee","stance":"strong_rec","why":"35-minute look at the design in simple coffee machines","quote":"its 35 minutes, but i highly recommend it!","text":"Technology Connections on youtube has an awesome video about coffee makers! its 35 minutes, but i highly recommend it! [VIDEO] i think it's actually amazing how much design went into these \"dead simple\" coffee machines!"},{"id":39108786,"author":"mewse-hn","month":"2024-01","story":"The humble brilliance of Italy's moka coffee pot (2018)","stance":"rec","why":"Demos an original Mr. Coffee against modern drip machines","quote":"Technology Connections on youtube has a video about drip coffee makers which is very good","text":"Drip coffee makers are just better than the old percolators. Technology Connections on youtube has a video about drip coffee makers which is very good (he demos an original mr. coffee machine which has some differences vs modern machines) [VIDEO] He also did a vid on percolators recently which I haven't watched but the thumbnail suggests its an extended rant about how they suck [LINK]"},{"id":38809942,"author":"aksss","month":"2023-12","story":"The surprising genius of sewing machines [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Exploration of the coffee machine from a recommended channel","quote":"Just a friendly recco for the Technology Connections YouTube channel.","text":"Just a friendly recco for the Technology Connections YouTube channel. Really liked his exploration of the Coffee Machine and other vids. Not affiliated in any way. [VIDEO]"},{"id":34315000,"author":"xeromal","month":"2023-01","story":"My Coffee Maker Just Makes Coffee","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how American drip coffee makers work, including bimetal valve","quote":"Anyone interested in how the traditional american coffee maker works should watch this.","text":"Anyone interested in how the traditional american coffee maker works should watch this. [VIDEO] Very fun video and I love the concept of using a piece of metal as a temperature-sensitive valve."},{"id":34315229,"author":"femto113","month":"2023-01","story":"My Coffee Maker Just Makes Coffee","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why simple drip machines are both stupid and brilliant","quote":"Technology Connections made a great video explaining how stupid/brilliant they are","text":"Simple drip machines are a marvel--I spend more on a month's supply of beans than I do on the maker, and have been getting about 5 years daily service out of each one. Technology Connections made a great video explaining how stupid/brilliant they are [VIDEO]"},{"id":32712891,"author":"bcjordan","month":"2022-09","story":"Interest in heat pumps has increased dramatically in recent years","stance":"rec","why":"Defense of the simple drip coffee maker from a fascinating tech channel","quote":"The TC channel is definitely a hacker's eye view in to all the technologies that we take for granted every day.","text":"Came here to post this :D The TC channel is definitely a hacker's eye view in to all the technologies that we take for granted every day. Tons of fascinating tech history covered, too. Other fun ones: - a defense of the simple drip coffee maker [VIDEO] - why do the turn signals not sync up when you're at a stop light? [LINK] - how humidifiers work and ehy the simple swamp cooler style are pretty ideal [LINK] - why dishwasher detergent packs are stupid (use powder/liquid) and why you should use the pre-wash slot and not bother pre rinsing your plates [LINK] - why do light switches click? [LINK]"},{"id":32464229,"author":"pfdietz","month":"2022-08","story":"Declining quality of consumer-grade products – 2009 fridge compressor autopsy","stance":"rec","why":"Compares original Mr. Coffee with modern drip makers and their simplification","quote":"I just watched an amusing video that compares Mr. Coffee's original machine to modern drip coffeemakers.","text":"I just watched an amusing video that compares Mr. Coffee's original machine to modern drip coffeemakers. Mr. Coffee would cost $300 today (adjusted for inflation). There's been great simplification of the design over the years. [VIDEO] Given a more than order of magnitude reduction in price, a shorter lifespan can be tolerated, and could even be a rational consumer choice."},{"id":32664638,"author":"RosanaAnaDana","month":"2022-08","story":"In the battle over the right to repair, open-source tractors offer alternatives","stance":"reference","why":"Shows how modern drip makers gum up compared to Mr. Coffee","quote":"The specific mechanism that gums up the works occurs at 6:45.","text":"[VIDEO] The specific mechanism that gums up the works occurs at 6:45. When you raise the lid of the modern version, grounds and coffee bits that have dusted up to the lid of the coffee maker drip via condensation into rear water reservoir. For greater detail, juxtapose minutes 8-11 with minutes 16-21. Mr. Coffee represents the king of drip still. And it is the greatest contributor to this fundamental design flaw (imp)."}],"r":[["by1xpy8ob8E",1],["MFEHFsO-XSI",1],["43XKfuptnik",1],["hX2aZUav-54",1],["n3Xv_g3g-mA",1],["31CcclqEiZw",1],["2z5A-COlDPk",1],["oHeehYYgl28",1],["d1EB1zsxW0k",1],["gkj_91IJVBk",1]]},"v7Mt0GYHU9A":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a terrific talk on APL readability and on the idea of idioms over libraries.","q":{"x":"On the readability of APL (terrific video)","i":19610929,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":32497070,"author":"myco_logic","month":"2022-08","story":"Programming breakthroughs we need","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Fantastic talk on idioms over libraries in array languages","quote":"his fantastic talk on anti-patterns in the Iversonian languages","text":"> It feels like there's an unexplored dimension of abstraction here. Something where generics, interfaces and higher order functions are too static and low level primitives. Something where it's really difficult to pinpoint what exactly is the repeated pattern here and how to exploit it. Instead of generic framework that can do everything, I'd like an efficient way do something specific. This bit reminds me a lot of a concept Aaron Hsu mentions in his fantastic talk on anti-patterns in the Iversonian languages[0], a paradigm he terms \"Idioms over Libraries\". The idea being that because of the expressive power of APL's primitives programmers often opt for utilizing easily recalled snippets of symbols over the importing of library code to implement common functionality. This facet of array-lang culture can be seen with sites like APL Cart[1], which serve as indexes of these frequently used snippets. I think a lot of people miss that this is one of the major advantages induced by the terseness of these languages (it's not just about code-golfing for the implicit joy of laconicism). By allowing for the direct expression of algorithms in the same context they are being used, APL and its kin enable one to tweak and optimize these idioms on a case-by-case basis: precisely the kind of specificity the author of this article is asking for. [0]: [VIDEO] [1]: [LINK]"},{"id":19610929,"author":"jonahx","month":"2019-04","story":"J Language – New Website","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Terrific talk on APL readability","quote":"On the readability of APL (terrific video)","text":"All of the languages you mentioned are watered-down and inferior versions of the programming models invented by APL and J. By that I don't mean that APL/J are unilaterally better -- there are plenty of practical reasons to choose R/Matlab/numpy -- but they are unquestionably more beautiful and visionary. They hang together at a deep level in a way those languages do not. They are also much harder to learn, and much more fun. On the readability of APL (terrific video): [VIDEO] Alan Perlis on its beauty: [LINK]"},{"id":21802405,"author":"xelxebar","month":"2019-12","story":"APL and J (2015)","stance":"rec","why":"Compares APL with other languages via human-computer interfaces","quote":"Aaron Hsu has a really interesting talk","text":"Always excited to see APL/J content on the top page. Happily, this coincides with a brand new release of the J interpreter, namely J901. It's so new that the homepage[0] still claims 901 is in beta. The install page[1], however, does have up to date information. If you would like to taste a bit of the difference before deciding to jump in, Aaron Hsu has a really interesting talk[2] that compares APL and \"other\" languages via the lens of Human-Computer Interfaces. I am just beginning my journey into J, but so far it has been extremely rewarding. Head on over to #jsoftware on Freenode. The channel is super small, but has some really friendly and helpful people on it! [0]: [LINK] [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20300358,"author":"kadblas","month":"2019-06","story":"Why I think Haskell is the best general purpose language as of June 22 2019","stance":"rec","why":"Introduces the principle of idioms over libraries","quote":"The main takeaway from this video for me was the principle of","text":"Qualification: I was introduced to FP a number of years ago and have finally reached a point where I feel that I have a deep understanding of FP in general. In my mind, Haskell is the \"best\" language out there (\"best\" in that it provides the best overall gain when all tradeoffs are considered). I'm no longer confident that Haskell is the \"best\" language anymore. I was recently introduced to a language that might be even better than Haskell: Dyalog APL. ( [LINK] ). Try it out yourself ( [LINK] ) To explain why, I recommend watching Aaron Hsu's videos on Dyalog APL: - Does APL need a Type System? ( [LINK] ) - This is actually a very interesting question that is more nuanced than upon first glance. - Design Patterns and Anti-Patterns in APL ( [VIDEO] ) - The main takeaway from this video for me was the principle of \"Idioms over libraries.\" - Higher Performance Tree-Wrangling, the APL way ( [LINK] ) - Or how to model Trees without using pointers by using \"Inverted Tables.\" (An inverted table is a table where the columns are the rows and the rows are the columns)"},{"id":28494926,"author":"invalidOrTaken","month":"2021-09","story":"K Programming Language","stance":"reference","why":"Repost of an earlier comment on expressivity","quote":"","text":"Comment from a year ago( [LINK] ): >A story, since in retrospect I think it's worth telling. Some years ago I was at the SF Clojure meetup. Arthur Whitney's daughter worked at the sponsoring company, and he agreed to come tell us about K. >In retrospect, I don't think we gave him the welcome he deserved. No one was rude or anything, but it seemed there was a disconnect: Arthur was keen to show off how fast K (and Kdb) was, over zillions and zillions of rows. >But the thing that the Clojurists were all searching for (that got them into Clojure in the first place) was expressivity. Is Clojure fast? I don't know, generally the problems I face come down to avoiding balls of mud rather than performance bottlenecks. And I think that was true of most there. >So Arthur got something of an underwhelming reception. I remember someone asking \"Does K have the ability to self-modify, a la Lisp macros?\" When Arthur said no, you could see most people in the room just sort of mentally shrug and move on. >And this was too bad. Because recently I've been playing around with J (another APL descendant) and been very impressed by some expressivity/readability benefits. Some small things that have very big effects on the codebase you actually end up with. >The first thing is the avoidance of abstraction. To use a Twitterism: >Broke: Just write your code and don't divide it into functions, creating one long main method >Woke: Divide your code up, naming parts that get reused >Bespoke: If your code "},{"id":22562344,"author":"invalidOrTaken","month":"2020-03","story":"K Language (2011)","stance":"reference","why":"Tangential mention in a long anecdote","quote":"","text":"A story, since in retrospect I think it's worth telling. Some years ago I was at the SF Clojure meetup. Arthur Whitney's daughter worked at the sponsoring company, and he agreed to come tell us about K. In retrospect, I don't think we gave him the welcome he deserved. No one was rude or anything, but it seemed there was a disconnect: Arthur was keen to show off how fast K (and Kdb) was, over zillions and zillions of rows. But the thing that the Clojurists were all searching for (that got them into Clojure in the first place) was expressivity . Is Clojure fast? I don't know, generally the problems I face come down to avoiding balls of mud rather than performance bottlenecks. And I think that was true of most there. So Arthur got something of an underwhelming reception. I remember someone asking \"Does K have the ability to self-modify, a la Lisp macros?\" When Arthur said no, you could see most people in the room just sort of mentally shrug and move on. And this was too bad. Because recently I've been playing around with J (another APL descendant) and been very impressed by some expressivity/readability benefits. Some small things that have very big effects on the codebase you actually end up with. The first thing is the avoidance of abstraction. To use a Twitterism: Broke: Just write your code and don't divide it into functions, creating one long main method Woke: Divide your code up, naming parts that get reused Bespoke: If your code is made up of really really short things, i"},{"id":21654779,"author":"arcfide","month":"2019-11","story":"Getting Started with APL (2017)","stance":"reference","why":"Author's talk on APL being natural for humans","quote":"there is some evidence to suggest that APL is actually arranged in a manner more natural for the human faculty than most computing languages","text":"It's really no more difficult than learning recursion, pointers, memory allocation, type theory, functional programming, iterative programming, OOP, or any other of the myriad techniques that we use for computing. As I mention in my talk on APL design patterns, it's largely a matter of what we have been trained to understand, and there is some evidence to suggest that APL is actually arranged in a manner more natural for the human faculty than most computing languages: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20320148,"author":"a-saleh","month":"2019-06","story":"Non-English-based programming languages","stance":"reference","why":"Amusing glimpse of symbol-only programming","quote":"was amused that there is this world where programming is just symbols :D","text":"Maybe look at a different programming paradigm then? :) I remember trying to learn APL and then watching [VIDEO] and was amused that there is this world where programming is just symbols :D So maybe look at i.e. something apl-like, or maybe ml or haskell or erlang, where so many things you can do with functions and composing them, or even Prolog, where everything could be thought of as a set of rules/constraints to be solved (I think? haven't programmed in Prolog since uni :) )"},{"id":16513620,"author":"ognyankulev","month":"2018-03","story":"APL Style: Patterns/Anti-patterns","stance":"reference","why":"Second recording of the same APL patterns talk","quote":"","text":"Two talks with these slides: 1. [LINK] (Dyalog17: Patterns and Anti-patterns in APL: Escaping the Beginner's Plateau) 2. [VIDEO] (Design Patterns vs Anti pattern in APL by Aaron W Hsu at FnConf17)"}],"r":[["z8MVKianh54",2],["hzPd3umu78g",1],["oP3c1h8v2ZQ",1],["V-1RhQ1uuQ4",1],["R7EEoWg6Ekk",1],["1PGm8LslEb4",1],["FY_NtO7SIrY",1],["f_0QlhYlS8g",1]]},"P9M__yYbsZ4":{"wy":"HN users send it to anyone asking about thorium or molten salt reactors as a compact overview that answers common anti-nuclear objections.","q":{"x":"I highly recommend this video on liquid-fluoride thorium reactors (LFTR)","i":4857422,"y":"2012"},"m":[{"id":4857422,"author":"NatW","month":"2012-12","story":"Norway is building thorium reactor","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Informative on LFTR design and countering anti-nuclear arguments.","quote":"I highly recommend this video on liquid-fluoride thorium reactors (LFTR)","text":"For those who haven't seen it, I highly recommend this video on liquid-fluoride thorium reactors (LFTR): [VIDEO] ! I passed the video by my uncle who was an expert/engineer dealing with control and safety of water cooled reactors. This is what he had to say: \"I found the video very informative technically, as well as being effective in countering many of the the arguments of those who profess to be against nuclear power in any form. I also found the details of the design of the liquid thorium fluoride reactor intriguing. To become an advocate for further investment in this concept, I would have to learn more about how the concept deals with the safety issues involved in the handling of fluorine gas and fluoride materials.\""},{"id":8863989,"author":"badloginagain","month":"2015-01","story":"ThorCon Power","stance":"rec","why":"Overview of molten salt reactors.","quote":"If you are interested, you can get a great overview here","text":"When nuclear research was first being developed, it was for the specific purpose of building the bomb. When the war ended, and the industry switched to civilian applications, they decided to go with Uranium because they had already worked out many of the issues. Still, the US Airforce wanted a nuclear-powered bomber shortly after the Navy showed their nuclear sub and before ICBM's deprecated \"end-of-days\" long-range bombers. A water-contained reactor wouldn't work in an airplane, so they funded the development of the MSR (molten-salt-reactor). Unfortunately, when they shuddered the project after the implementation of practical ICBMs, industry politics discredited MSR's in favour of the reactor-types we have now. After fukushima, a grassroots campaign has been undertaken to resume research of MSR's as a replacement of fossil fuels for scalable carbon-free energy creation. If you are interested, you can get a great overview here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":8864577,"author":"badloginagain","month":"2015-01","story":"ThorCon Power","stance":"rec","why":"Overview of molten salt reactors.","quote":"This has a pretty good overview","text":"Uranium reactors use something like 2% of the total energy available in the uranium. Molten-salt-reactors use something like 98% of the available energy. Additionally, the 2% that is waste contain extremely rare derivatives that can be used as cancer \"smartbombs\", metals pivotal to deep space NASA missions, etc. This has a pretty good overview: [VIDEO]"},{"id":5272251,"author":"meztez","month":"2013-02","story":"Ask HN: What do you know about thorium as an energy source?","stance":"rec","why":"Thorium reactor overview.","quote":"There is a great video on thorium reactor that was posted here or on reddit a while ago.","text":"I assume it would cost a lot in R&D although other nations are currently experimenting LFTR. Beside that, I don't think the cost-benefit is at a point where it would make sense. It's one thing to produce energy but it's another entirely to distribute it. We need to develop both source and distribution. There is a great video on thorium reactor that was posted here or on reddit a while ago. [VIDEO] That's the extend of my knowledge on thorium."},{"id":3499203,"author":"JerkyTreatz","month":"2012-01","story":"We don't have abundant solar power because of financing, not science","stance":"rec","why":"Short introduction to liquid fluoride thorium reactors.","quote":"Learn about it in 5 minutes","text":"Solar is not a viable means for today's energy consumption, and it certainly won't be a viable means for tomorrow's energy consumption. The energy race has been decided already, and the only answer to fossil fuels is Liquid Floride Thoriam Reactors (LFTR). It's an alternative form of nuclear energy that has been around since the Manhattan Project. Unlike the current nuclear power plants of today, it is impossible for a nuclear explosion to occur, there is no waste generated (well, there is about 1% waste, but the waste generated is actually a very rare metal NASA uses to power deep space satellites), and Thorium (the equivalent to Uranium) is 1000x more abundant than Uranium. LFTR will be the worlds core energy source within 30 years. China is the only country actively developing a LFTR program- if it comes to fruition, they could be the power suppliers of tomorrow. Learn about it in 5 minutes: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31249309,"author":"maerF0x0","month":"2022-05","story":"A first for Canada: New type of nuclear plant opening by 2028","stance":"reference","why":"Gordon McDowell's thorium advocacy in Calgary.","quote":"Gordon McDowell was active in Calgary trying to spread awareness of the technology.","text":"This could be a boon for the Tar sands in Alberta. As I understand it a very large amount of oil there is used to create heat for the purpose of releasing the oil from the tar sands and generating nuclear (ie steam) power there could create a good amount of lower temp heat for use in industry (also heating homes if such a project were undertaken). This could really help the oil sands be less carbon emitting. I do wish it was a thorium based project though. Gordon McDowell was active in Calgary trying to spread awareness of the technology. Sadly it always feels \"a decade away\" ... [VIDEO]"},{"id":3409093,"author":"jasonkeene","month":"2011-12","story":"Rare earth crisis: Innovate, or be crushed by China","stance":"reference","why":"Thorium comes up alongside rare earth minerals.","quote":"Thorium comes up with rare earth minerals.. we can use it as a sustainable source of energy.","text":"[VIDEO] (skip to 1:42:05) Thorium comes up with rare earth minerals.. we can use it as a sustainable source of energy."}]},"PbmJkMhmrVI":{"wy":"HN links it to correct the common misconception about dark matter and as a counterpoint to popular science coverage, though one commenter found it muddled.","q":{"x":"If you're interested in learning more I recommend you watch \"dark matter is not a theory","i":37895569,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":38489324,"author":"badosu","month":"2023-12","story":"Astronomers posit that we live in a giant void","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Dense evidence for dark matter over modified gravity.","quote":"This is a dense video, I highly recommend it though","text":"This is a dense video, I highly recommend it though: [VIDEO] TLDW: there's a lot of evidence on why the phenomena we watch is mostly favored to be described as 'something' that is conveniently called dark matter as opposed to a revision of gravity."},{"id":41369781,"author":"bunderbunder","month":"2024-08","story":"The possibilities for dark matter have shrunk","stance":"rec","why":"Nice counterpoint to popular discussion of dark matter.","quote":"these YouTube videos are a nice counterpoint to a lot of popular communication and discussion on dark matter","text":"If you've got the time, these YouTube videos are a nice counterpoint to a lot of popular communication and discussion on dark matter: Dark matter is not a theory: [VIDEO] That dark matter video aged like milk: [LINK] the tl;dr is that \"dark matter\" is a bit of a misnomer. (But also a fun name so it's probably here to stay through sheer memetic momentum.) Dark matter is not actually a hypothesized kind of matter. Dark matter is just an unresolved discrepancy between theory and observation. I'll say that again: \"dark matter\" is not an attempt at explaining the discrepancy; dark matter is the discrepancy. There are some hypotheses that postulate some kind of as-yet-unobserved matter that could resolve the discrepancy, but these hypotheses are not the actual problem that we call \"dark matter\". And not all hypotheses postulate unobserved or exotic matter. Some of them suggest alternative physics, others suggest measurement error."},{"id":40616398,"author":"PartiallyTyped","month":"2024-06","story":"Gravity Without Mass: UAH Study Proposes Alternative to Dark Matter","stance":"rec","why":"Videos clarifying dark matter as observations.","quote":"I really enjoyed Dr Angela's videos on Dark Matter [1,2]","text":"IANAP (physicist) The only the matter portion of the universe is 85% dark matter (26.8% of mass-energy content of the universe). As for the question; I don't think it's right to assume that dark matter really is exotic matter. It's just a set of observations. It's something that behaves like matter (with respect to density scaling) but only seems to interact gravitationally. Some theories posit that it's some unknown particles, others that they are primordial black holes, and some that gravity just behaves differently (MOND). I really enjoyed Dr Angela's videos on Dark Matter [1,2] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":37895534,"author":"Pxtl","month":"2023-10","story":"Revisited mass of the Milky Way is much smaller than expectations from cosmology","stance":"rec","why":"Explains dark matter as a set of observations rather than a theory.","quote":"dark matter is not a theory, it's a set of observations.","text":"There's a good cosmology YouTuber who basically explains: dark matter is not a theory, it's a set of observations. Galaxies behave like there's a halo of invisible mass surrounding them, one that varies in density per-galaxy. The halos even perturb as expected in galaxy collisions. But nobody's got a coherent theory on what that halo is. [VIDEO]"},{"id":37895569,"author":"aeturnum","month":"2023-10","story":"Revisited mass of the Milky Way is much smaller than expectations from cosmology","stance":"rec","why":"Explains consistent observed excess mass effects.","quote":"If you're interested in learning more I recommend you watch \"dark matter is not a theory","text":"If you're interested in learning more I recommend you watch \"dark matter is not a theory\"[1]. We consistently, across many measures, observe the effect of substantially more mass than we expect using traditional understandings of dynamics. People have been aware of it, under different names, for about 90 years now. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":40968255,"author":"karmakaze","month":"2024-07","story":"Fitting an elephant with four non-zero parameters","stance":"reference","why":"More recent video by the physicist on dark matter.","quote":"Here's a more recent video by the physicist[0].","text":"Interesting that you should link that video. Its title card says \"Angela Collier\". Here's a more recent video by the physicist[0]. Re: where it says \"using simulations with dark matter\", we can't simulate DM because it doesn't have any properties beyond our observations. All we do is distribute amounts of it to match observations. It could be \"Dyson spheres with EM shields\" and the results would be the same. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":40677014,"author":"karmakaze","month":"2024-06","story":"Wild new study suggests gravity can exist without mass","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Dark matter is not a theory\"[0]","text":"\"Dark matter is not a theory\"[0] Dark matter, do we need it? what is it? where is it? how much? Full rap mp3[1] [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":40740222,"author":"ansible","month":"2024-06","story":"Rotation curves: still flat after a million light-years","stance":"reference","why":"Collier discusses dark matter and MOND.","quote":"Angela Collier talks about Dark Matter here","text":"I also don't know why MOND remains so popular. Angela Collier talks about Dark Matter here: [VIDEO] And talks more about how often MOND is brought up in comments on her own video: [LINK]"},{"id":40090077,"author":"SJC_Hacker","month":"2024-04","story":"Scientists say they have found evidence of an unknown planet in our solar system","stance":"reference","why":"Collier argues dark matter is an observation, not a theory.","quote":"takes the position that dark matter isn't an theory, but an observation","text":"Angela Collier (astronomy post-doc) takes the position that dark matter isn't an theory, but an observation [VIDEO]"},{"id":36809752,"author":"johnnyworker","month":"2023-07","story":"An Invitation to a Secret Society","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Doing something professionally just means doing it for money and with the authorization of others, not necessarily doing it well. We all know about professional software developers who are forced by their profession, that is, the fact that they do it under contract X for company Y managed by person Z, to do things that they know are complete crap. I mean, SAP is professional. Microsoft or Google lock-in? Highly professional! Shiny even. Supporting RSS? Not professional. A gimmick you're allowed, like what you do outside of work. Or even more brutal: Ignaz Semmelweis. That he was a professional didn't help him, the super mega cereal professionals undid his efforts without blinking, and without anyone even realizing it at the time. At the same time, people just make stuff up without any rigor, just take climate change denial and whatnot. So it's not that not being a professional is any positive indicator, either. I just think that if you do something others don't already do, they cannot authorize you, by definition. They don't know what they're even talking about, while thinking they do. Not their fault, not your fault, but if you don't do you, \"you\" will not be done, and the world will never know. Of course, in the end the results have to hold up, my point is not to give license to \"questioning everything\" as code for just spouting non-sequiturs. But I wouldn't dissuade \"randoms\" from pursuing scientific fancies because the professionals got it, because frankly, they don't. No"},{"id":40973605,"author":"andrewflnr","month":"2024-07","story":"Fitting an elephant with four non-zero parameters","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"I think she's trying to make some meta point but ends up just muddying the water.","text":"As far as I know it's from here: [VIDEO] As I said elsewhere in this thread, I think she's trying to make some meta point but ends up just muddying the water."}],"r":[["qS34oV-jv_A",2],["ZSRHeXYDLko",1],["pW-SOdj4Kkk",1],["34rdxDgpaaA",1],["L-x8DYTOv7w",1],["iRdwXQb7CfM",1],["AyORieDhpkg",1],["AZX6awZq5Z0",1],["EZ05e7EMOLM",1],["otAcmD6XEEE",1]]},"9crggox5rbc":{"wy":"Commenters recommend it as a clear, characteristically engaging Susskind lecture for people trying to grasp the entanglement and gravity connection.","q":{"x":"Mr.Susskind has a Stanford lecture on this topic detailing the physics in his own unique & awesome way","i":10625226,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":36672630,"author":"hughesjj","month":"2023-07","story":"The Physicist Who’s Challenging the Quantum Orthodoxy","stance":"rec","why":"Among lectures the commenter watched while learning gravity-entanglement ideas","quote":"I've been incessantly watching lectures in this space to try to beat an understanding into my dumb dumb brain because it's super, super fucking cool.","text":"I'm definitely into the \"it's an emergent phenomena\" camp. I think it's inherently relational, as that's a more efficient way to encode geometry rather than space itself being a quantized \"thing\". Afaik this theory is the \"leading\"/imho most promising theory of \"quantum gravity\", that being the \"gravity = entanglement\" conjecture and the related ideas of \"Complexity= action/volume/whatever\" that susskind and many others have been developing for the past 20 so years. All that said I'm nowhere near a physicist and am probably just spewing a total misunderstanding of the situation from my armchair. That said, I've been incessantly watching lectures in this space to try to beat an understanding into my dumb dumb brain because it's super, super fucking cool. - [LINK] - [LINK] - [VIDEO] - [LINK] - [LINK] I'm also fascinated by the idea of phase transitions, which seems to be how the laws of physics have \"evolved\" so far. It's crazy how much quantum computation is coming into play with this stuff, ex last year's Nobel prize with the bell inequality. That said I'm sure being a programmer I'm biased to think the universe is inherently computation/math."},{"id":19666247,"author":"philipov","month":"2019-04","story":"Travel through wormholes is possible, but slow","stance":"rec","why":"Susskind lecture on connectivity of space arising from entanglement","quote":"How about another lecture by Leonard Susskind?","text":"How about another lecture by Leonard Susskind? I'm just repeating his ideas! The gist of it is that the connectivity of space is related to the patterns of entanglement between particles in the quantum foam. If you were to break that entanglement, you could disconnect two regions of space. [VIDEO]"},{"id":18938220,"author":"ithkuil","month":"2019-01","story":"Strangeness of Black Holes","stance":"rec","why":"One of several insightful Susskind lectures","quote":"I found these lectures by Leonard Susskind very insightful","text":"I found these lectures by Leonard Susskind very insightful: - Leonard Susskind on The World As Hologram ( [LINK] ) - Leonard Susskind | \"ER = EPR\" or \"What's Behind the Horizons of Black Holes?\" ( [LINK] ) - Entanglement and Complexity: Gravity and Quantum Mechanics ( [VIDEO] )"},{"id":10625226,"author":"jharohit","month":"2015-11","story":"SLAC Theorist Lance Dixon Explains Quantum Gravity","stance":"rec","why":"Susskind's lecture explaining the physics in his own way","quote":"Mr.Susskind has a Stanford lecture on this topic detailing the physics in his own unique & awesome way","text":"Mr.Susskind has a Stanford lecture on this topic detailing the physics in his own unique & awesome way [VIDEO]"},{"id":15537058,"author":"philipov","month":"2017-10","story":"Newfound Wormhole Allows Information to Escape Black Holes","stance":"reference","why":"Source for complexity growth after thermal equilibrium and wormhole length","quote":"the complexity continues to increase long after thermal equilibrium","text":"A black hole is a fast scrambler and very quickly comes to thermal equilibrium (max entropy). However, the complexity continues to increase long after thermal equilibrium, and that's what is thought to be analogous to the length of the wormhole growing (at the speed of light, no less). [0]: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["bJczLlwp-d8",1],["NNnIGh9g6fA",1],["Y0Oa4Lp5fLE",1],["TwKpj2ISQAc",1],["PHiyQID7SBs",1],["mScpHTIi-kM",1],["KTzGBJPuJwM",1],["mceaM2_zQd8",1],["tISy7EJQPzI",1],["OBPpRqxY8Uw",1]]},"Hzb37RyagCQ":{"wy":"HN commenters point to it whenever the question is how far hardware can be trusted, including FPGAs, supply chain attacks and firmware TPMs.","q":{"x":"Here's an excellent talk that addresses your question","i":24962059,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":24962059,"author":"Confiks","month":"2020-11","story":"Bunnie's Precursor Crowdfunding Begins","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Excellent talk on trust problems in hardware","quote":"Here's an excellent talk that addresses your question","text":"Here's an excellent talk that addresses your question: Open Source is Insufficient to Solve Trust Problems in Hardware [1]. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":23054039,"author":"Confiks","month":"2020-05","story":"OpenTitan RTL Synthesis with Yosys","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Excellent talk on trust problems in hardware","quote":"Here's an excellent talk that addresses your question","text":"Here's an excellent talk that addresses your question: Open Source is Insufficient to Solve Trust Problems in Hardware [1]. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":35791012,"author":"kfreds","month":"2023-05","story":"AMD's Firmware TPMs Vulnerable to Hardware Attacks, Defeating Disk Encryption","stance":"rec","why":"Bunnie's talk on FPGAs and physical supply chain attacks","quote":"did an excellent talk on the subject","text":"As I'm sure you're aware FPGAs are nice because they can complicate things for an attacker in the physical supply chain. Andrew \"bunnie\" Huang did an excellent talk on the subject: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29076443,"author":"skinner927","month":"2021-11","story":"U.S. telecoms are going to start physically removing Huawei gear","stance":"reference","why":"Cited on the limits of trusting hardware","quote":"","text":"There’s really no way to ever be 100% sure of hardware. Sure we could use FPGAs for all hardware and then the hardware “source” is software, but who makes the FPGAs? There’s techniques even under X-ray to mask a rogue chip. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["qd_hEja6bzE",1],["J3FcbFqSoQY",1],["tybOi4hjZFQ",1]]},"_8bhtEgB8Mo":{"wy":"Recommended as a high-level but clear explanation of the black hole information paradox and competing theories, by a physicist known for making quantum mechanics approachable.","q":{"x":"explains the paradox and several different competing theories at a high level.","i":20800033,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":31289871,"author":"joncp","month":"2022-05","story":"Where do space, time and gravity come from?","stance":"rec","why":"Carroll makes quantum mechanics approachable","quote":"Sean Carroll is a master of making quantum mechanics approachable to non-physicists.","text":"Sean Carroll is a master of making quantum mechanics approachable to non-physicists. Here are a few of his talks: - Fermilab 2013: [LINK] - The Royal Institution 2014: [VIDEO] - The Royal Institution 2020: [LINK]"},{"id":24950122,"author":"andreareina","month":"2020-10","story":"The Black Hole Information Paradox Comes to an End","stance":"rec","why":"Fuller explanation of the information problem","quote":"There's a Royal Institute video featuring Sean Carroll and Jen Ouelette[1] that more fully explains the problem","text":"There's a Royal Institute video featuring Sean Carroll and Jen Ouelette[1] that more fully explains the problem, but as I understand it it comes down to a quantum mechanical principle called monogamy of entanglement---there's only so much entanglement to go around, and if two particles (e.g. the pair of Hawking radiation particles) are fully entangled then they cannot have any amount of entanglement with anything else (e.g. information about the black hole). The result in TFA breaks the entanglement between the Hawking pair, allowing the outgoing particles to encode the black hole's information. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":20800033,"author":"platz","month":"2019-08","story":"How do black holes destroy information and why is that a problem?","stance":"rec","why":"Explains paradox and competing theories at a high level","quote":"explains the paradox and several different competing theories at a high level.","text":"Also see Black hole Firewalls - with Sean Carroll (speaking at 20:25) and Jennifer Ouellette [VIDEO] which explains the paradox and several different competing theories at a high level."},{"id":13527536,"author":"platz","month":"2017-01","story":"Study reveals substantial evidence of holographic universe","stance":"rec","why":"Quick summary of holographic principle","quote":"Black hole Firewalls with Sean Carroll and Jennifer Ouellette (45m+50s quick summary of the holographic principle)","text":"Black hole Firewalls with Sean Carroll and Jennifer Ouellette (45m+50s quick summary of the holographic principle) [VIDEO] Leonard Susskind on The World As Hologram (more dense/verbose) [LINK]"},{"id":13981026,"author":"platz","month":"2017-03","story":"NIST Physicists Show Ion Pairs Perform Enhanced &#039;Spooky Action&#039;","stance":"reference","why":"Source on black hole firewalls","quote":"it's one of the only ways to avoid 'black hole firewalls' [1]","text":"locality is looking suspect for other reasons too, as it's one of the only ways to avoid 'black hole firewalls' [1] (the other way to avoid firewalls involves giving up conservation of information which would be bad for QM theory). [1] Black hole Firewalls with Sean Carroll and Jennifer Ouellette [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["RXJKdh1KZ0w",1],["gEKSpZPByD0",1],["5hVmeOCJjOU",1],["QZy4Yml3LTY",1],["fNpsgTIpODA",1],["ftcIcn8AmSY",1],["2DIl3Hfh9tY",1],["Agu6jipKfYw",1],["MEUWxNifJJ8",1]]},"52KLGqDSAjo":{"wy":"HN users point to it as a clear, evidence-based series to counter climate skepticism.","q":{"x":"There is a great video series on YouTube by \"potholer54\" that shows the scientific evidence for it better than I can.","i":21323546,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":21323546,"author":"itcrowd","month":"2019-10","story":"Antarctic ice cliffs may not contribute to sea-level rise as much as predicted","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Great series presenting scientific evidence; first video is 10 minutes","quote":"There is a great video series on YouTube by \"potholer54\" that shows the scientific evidence for it better than I can.","text":"It was my mistake to link to the summary, I was actually referring to the full report in the comments before. As for your quote, it needs to be read in the context of the full paragraph. Its written sloppily, but a better phrasing would be: \"We don't see the worldwide acceleration observed by Nerem et al. (2018) in the Netherlands' coast area , within the 1993-2017 record\". I.e. they are saying the global trend is not the same as the local (Dutch) trend. Perfectly reasonable. > I must say I'm currently on the fence about the role of CO2 in global warming. Well, I could imagine your doubts about sea level rise because they are difficult and uncertain. But there is absolutely no scientific doubt about the role of CO2 in global warming. In fact, CO2 as a control knob of the climate is the only way to explain climatic variations observed in (at least) the past 500 million years . What is uncertain is the predictions because they are influenced by action taken to curb emissions etc. There is a great video series on YouTube by \"potholer54\" that shows the scientific evidence for it better than I can. The first video is only 10 minutes and addresses the general points rather well. [VIDEO]"},{"id":25717134,"author":"imtringued","month":"2021-01","story":"A Polar Vortex collapse sequence has begun","stance":"rec","why":"Playlist on climate change from same creator","quote":"That same content creator also made a whole playlist concerning climate change","text":">There's also some evidence that historic rises in CO2 lagged temperature rather than the reverse. That's only true in some cases with strong emphasis on \"some\". If you want to know how much of that is true and how much is wrong just watch this video [LINK] I don't think I can do it justice with a butchered summary on HN but I'll keep it long enough to get you hooked. There are two phases during a deglaciation and CO2 lags in the first one and leads in the second one. We have basically skipped the first phase by emitting CO2 directly. If anything, this should worry us even more because there is evidence that increased temperatures drive CO2 emissions as well. So we haven't skipped the first phase at all, it's going to happen in the future. Feedback loops have been predicted by many scientists. What could happen is this: \"man-made CO2 -> higher temperatures -> more CO2 -> even higher temperatures\". I recommend you to watch the entire video. Not just the segment I linked. That same content creator also made a whole playlist concerning climate change: [VIDEO]"},{"id":6388487,"author":"quink","month":"2013-09","story":"Dialing Back the Alarm on Climate Change","stance":"rec","why":"Offered as the rebuttal to a skeptic video","quote":"Meanwhile, you go ahead and watch this","text":"OK, let's watch this. 0:23-ish: No one in the mainstream media in Australia has ever told anyone about Henry fucking Ergas? Well, this is full of shit already. He also ran Concept Economics. > Concept Economics, the consultancy that recently estimated the costs of the National Broadband Network outweighed the benefits by up to $20 billion, has gone into administration. Sheesh, what is it with these climate skeptics and their businesses going under? 0:48: Lack of action across the world? Most expensive carbon tax in the world? Kind sir, I have a graph to cure that ailment: [LINK] Austria is AUT. Australia is AUS, in case there's any confusion following the whiplash you just got by this fact-whipping. 1:18: Thank you for that idiocy. Australia is the world's highest CO2 per capita emitter with any population to speak of, apart from the UAE: [LINK] . It has the dirtiest power station in the entire OECD, which alone produce 1/1800th the greenhouse gas emissions on the planet: [LINK] You know what, I'm done. I will go on, but considering such a litany of error within a minute and a half, I can spend my time vastly better. But like I said, I'll watch it for amusement value. Meanwhile, you go ahead and watch this: [VIDEO] Edit: OK, just wanted to add that the carbon tax does not \"cost\" 10 billion a year by any means. That's near enough the revenue being raised by the government through carbon pricing and near enough all of it makes it back into the economy through various means. One"}],"r":[["bRcu-ysocX4",1],["Em7nqDqQ8oM",1],["uUmIdq0D6-A",1]]},"7JUs4c99-mo":{"wy":"HN points to it as an impressive look at how far WebAssembly can go, and as the explanation of the architecture behind CheerpX and its Flash support.","q":{"x":"This is honestly one of the coolest things I've seen","i":23532747,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":30687615,"author":"gavinray","month":"2022-03","story":"PartialExecuter: Reducing WebAssembly size by exploring all executions in LLVM","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Demo of C++ multiplayer game with client and server in browser.","quote":"There's a fantastic Meetup presentation given by one of them","text":"This company has an x86-to-WASM compiler that lets you execute arbitrary binaries in the browser. Also a JVM to WASM transpiler. There's a fantastic Meetup presentation given by one of them where they show running a C++ multiplayer game with both client AND server running in a browser, using WebRTC as a networking polyfill. Really mindblowing: [VIDEO]"},{"id":43536722,"author":"gavinray","month":"2025-03","story":"Browsercraft: Java Minecraft in the browser","stance":"rec","why":"Demo porting multiplayer C++ game to browser with WASM.","quote":"In it, they showed a functional demo of porting an OSS multiplayer C++ game entirely to the browser with WASM.","text":"5 years ago, I watched a YouTube recording of a meetup talk given by one of the CheerpX engineers: [VIDEO] In it, they showed a functional demo of porting an OSS multiplayer C++ game entirely to the browser with WASM. Afterwards, I read some of their engineering content and was blown away. It's been fascinating to watch the CheerpX/CheerpJ progress from afar over the years."},{"id":23532747,"author":"btown","month":"2020-06","story":"Adobe to remove Flash Player from web site after December 2020","stance":"rec","why":"Team talk on x86 virtualization in the browser.","quote":"This is honestly one of the coolest things I've seen","text":"Here's a talk by the team as well: [VIDEO] And their website: [LINK] This is honestly one of the coolest things I've seen, and given the amount of Flex/Spark software for enterprise out there, it could drastically change the legacy software landscape."},{"id":37984678,"author":"nanaian","month":"2023-10","story":"Ruffle: Flash Player Emulator","stance":"reference","why":"Explains CheerpX Flash architecture.","quote":"Yuri talks about CXFF's architecture here","text":"Developer here. CheerpX for Flash runs the Pepper API version of Flash, and you're correct about the license. However, we don't do full Linux emulation just to run Flash - we emulate PPAPI and run the Flash player in an x86 JIT (CheerpX). Yuri talks about CXFF's architecture here: [VIDEO] ...but if you wanted full Linux system emulation, we got it! [LINK]"},{"id":24864026,"author":"saagarjha","month":"2020-10","story":"iSH Shell","stance":"reference","why":"Work on faster WASM loading.","quote":"","text":"It's been on our minds for a while, the major issues with this is that IPC between the app process and WebKit is like 100µs, so having the interpreter run isolated from the app process is hard. Plus loading WASM is not \"free\" (that is, essentially an instruction cache flush) because you need to generate a bundle that WebKit will then load, which is slow (although there has been work done in this area: [VIDEO] ). So even if we did some sort of WASI thing where everything is going on in the browser, there are hurdles to overcome to make this viable. Plus the current interpreter almost certainly leaves performance on the table ;)"},{"id":23925499,"author":"apignotti","month":"2020-07","story":"Flash Game History","stance":"reference","why":"Presentation on architecture of CheerpX Flash system.","quote":"A presentation about the architecture of the system","text":"At Leaning Technologies, we are working on preserving _all_ flash content by running the original x86 Flash plugin via WebAssembly virtualization (CheerpX). If you want to read more: [LINK] [LINK] A presentation about the architecture of the system: [VIDEO] Feel free to drop me any question: [LINK]"}],"r":[["XehNK7UpZsc",1],["Qct6LKbneKQ",1],["5TFDG-y-EHs",1],["MiUHjLxm3V0",1],["fU9hR3kiOK0",1],["UQuIVsNzqDk",1],["ML4tw_UzqZE",1],["0bcZb-SsnrA",1],["dKx1wnXClcI",1]]},"JENdgiAPD6c":{"wy":"HN points people to it as a funny and sobering warning about why parsing, validating, or implementing email software is far harder than it looks.","q":{"x":"There is is awesome talk about E-Mail by Ricardo Signes","i":27266364,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":27266364,"author":"JoyfulPanda","month":"2021-05","story":"Complexities of e-mail validation logic","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Awesome, hilarious talk on email; first five minutes are Perl-specific","quote":"There is is awesome talk about E-Mail by Ricardo Signes","text":"There is is awesome talk about E-Mail by Ricardo Signes: [VIDEO] The first 5 minutes are perl specific, but the rest is email and just hilarious."},{"id":6348939,"author":"k33l0r","month":"2013-09","story":"Email Validation Rules","stance":"rec","why":"Primer on why email addresses are terrible to parse","quote":"excellent primer into why email addresses (and emails in general) are terrible to parse","text":"I found this talk by Ricardo Signes on email parsing to be an excellent primer into why email addresses (and emails in general) are terrible to parse: [VIDEO]"},{"id":3231947,"author":"AngryParsley","month":"2011-11","story":"Move over HTTP it's SMTP's Turn to Shine","stance":"rec","why":"Talk showing how complicated and crazy SMTP and email are","quote":"does a good job of scaring anyone thinking of writing an SMTP client or server","text":"The SMTP standard is complicated and crazy. RJBS from Pobox does a good job of scaring anyone thinking of writing an SMTP client or server: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21577208,"author":"q3k","month":"2019-11","story":"How to run your own mail server (2017)","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on how messy the email standards are","quote":"I'll let Ricardo Signes do the talking","text":"Fun is a very curious way of putting it, considering how batshit crazy IMAP is. The rest of the involved email standards are also a huge dumpster fire. I'll let Ricardo Signes do the talking: [VIDEO]"}]},"WEDIj9JBTC8":{"wy":"HN recommends it to beginners as a quick, clear overview of finance and investing fundamentals.","q":{"x":"An excellent crash course for the most critical concepts.","i":23953443,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":23953443,"author":"amix","month":"2020-07","story":"Ask HN: What are some good resources to learn business administration/finance?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Excellent crash course on the most critical finance concepts","quote":"An excellent crash course for the most critical concepts.","text":"William Ackman: Everything You Need to Know About Finance and Investing in Under an Hour [VIDEO] — An excellent crash course for the most critical concepts."},{"id":22867138,"author":"ipnon","month":"2020-04","story":"Ask HN: What are you reading to make sense of the economy?","stance":"rec","why":"Great follow-up covering the money-making Wall Street side","quote":"A great follow up in the","text":"A great follow up in the \"New York billionaires explain basics of the economy\" curriculum: William Ackman: Everything You Need to Know About Finance and Investing in Under an Hour | Big Think [0] Covers the money making, Wall Street side [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":10020780,"author":"kleer001","month":"2015-08","story":"Ask HN: Do you day trade in stock market? What are your investing strategies?","stance":"rec","why":"45 minute talk on investing basics","quote":"fun time 45 min talk on the basics","text":"tl;dr Know what you're buying, know your risk threshold, hold it as long as you can, invest frequently. It's going to sound like a stale and naive quip but buy low and sell high. I look for deals. Sometimes that's an IPO with a nice finance sheet. Sometimes it's a huge correction on a popular mega-corporation. Weather you're a value investor or a growth investor the goal is profit, right? What works for me is knowing what I'm investing in. I'm familiar with the products or services. It's a fun topic. There's lots to read out there. Sturgeons Law holds in full effect. 90% of all of it is total crap. All you need is probably at your local library. Here's some homework. Look up these terms: dead cat bounce, double top, exponential moving average, don't fight the tape, upgrades, channel, channel up, channel down, wedge, triangle, insider selling, dividends, oversold, ticker, forex, a falling knife has no handle, FUD, Irrational exuberance... fun time 45 min talk on the basics: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["kYYJlNbV1OM",1],["I8Xc2_FtpHI",1],["ipVxxxqwBQw",1],["snAhsXyO3Ck",1],["cQl6jUjFjp4",1]]},"Wx_kugSemfY":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a clear, short but in-depth explanation of harmonics and overtones, useful even for musicians.","q":{"x":"Music producer Andrew Huang does a fantastic job demonstrating the harmonic series concepts","i":27592136,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":27592136,"author":"auiya","month":"2021-06","story":"The Fine Art of Combining Harmonics","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Fantastic demonstration of the harmonic series","quote":"Music producer Andrew Huang does a fantastic job demonstrating the harmonic series concepts","text":"Music producer Andrew Huang does a fantastic job demonstrating the harmonic series concepts in a video from last year: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27852588,"author":"hug","month":"2021-07","story":"The Alexander: Why did you build such a long piano?","stance":"rec","why":"Short, simple but in-depth harmonic series explanation, even for musicians","quote":"This video does a good job of explaining it in a relatively short and simple way","text":"The harmonic series is a really neat thing to learn about -- one of those \"art is math\" kind of moments. This video does a good job of explaining it in a relatively short and simple way, but goes in-depth enough that even people with an understanding of music might learn something: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27078279,"author":"noxToken","month":"2021-05","story":"Bird song sonographs show distinct drawing patterns","stance":"rec","why":"Clear explanation of harmonics, overtones, and sonograms","quote":"Andrew Huang does a good job of explaining harmonics and overtones","text":"Andrew Huang does a good job of explaining harmonics and overtones[0]. You only need to watch until about the 4 minute mark (from the timestamp) to get an explanation of harmonics and what the sonograms represent. The short of it is that most natural sounds product a root tone plus a varying amount of related tones above it. Our ears hear the root tone, and the other tones above it are what give the sound its uniqueness. That's why a guitar, a clarinet and 3 singers can produce the same note while sounding distinct. Birds seem to produce a natural sound without a lot of the related tones above it. Their sound is, relatively speaking, much purer than most other natural sounds. That's very unique. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29063429,"author":"eru","month":"2021-11","story":"Fundamentals of Piano Practice","stance":"reference","why":"Harmonies built on harmonic series","quote":"a lot of harmonies are built on top of the harmonic series.","text":"Well, a lot of harmonies are built on top of the harmonic series. See [VIDEO] And tuning is all about 'numerical puns', similar to how eg 2^10 ~ 10^3, but for perfect fifths and octaves etc. Of course, our physiology and culture is a bit too complicated and convoluted to understand everything about music from first principles like this. So most of the time you are better off learning (and treating) music as music instead of as applied math or physics."}],"r":[["aIHj3qMRqqE",1]]},"cfwwHa-7Ux8":{"wy":"Recommended in threads about online hostility as a short reminder to treat strangers as human beings.","q":{"x":"This is the video, I want everyone on internet to watch","i":4181750,"y":"2012"},"m":[{"id":4181750,"author":"luckymurari","month":"2012-06","story":"How to beta test your mobile apps","stance":"strong_rec","why":"A video everyone online should watch","quote":"This is the video, I want everyone on internet to watch","text":"Feedback is awesome. But, I guess you could've reduced a bit of causticness in it. This is the video, I want everyone on internet to watch - \"A real person, a lot like you\" - [VIDEO] PS/Disclaimer: Not directly affiliated to them. Founders are my friends!"},{"id":5342198,"author":"networked","month":"2013-03","story":"Hello, I'm a Human Being","stance":"rec","why":"A short video on treating online people as human","quote":"There's a nice three-and-a-half-minute video based on it","text":"Derek Sivers makes a somewhat more extensive comment on the same point in \"Anything You Want\". There's a nice three-and-a-half-minute video based on it [1]. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":2836000,"author":"patio11","month":"2011-08","story":"Bassnectar responds regarding music piracy","stance":"rec","why":"Reminds viewers that online strangers are real people","quote":"I think the lesson is rather important.","text":"Hiya Slowpoke. You seem to be new to HN. That's cool. We're very much unlike some other Internet communities which you've spent time on. In particular, we place a wee bit of emphasis on treating people like they are people, even when interacting with them over HTTP. My name is Patrick. Pleased to meet you. I run a business which helps teach little kids to read, and spend a lot of time on HN as my hobby. A lot of HNers know me, have spoken to me, or have shaken my hand in person, because I'm a fairly personable guy. Most do not say \"Hello Patrick, pleased to meet you, I don't give half a fuck whether you starve to death.\" That would be a wee bit uncouth. We have guidelines here for the community's consensus on talking to each other: [LINK] I'd also like to leave you with a video from Derek Sivers, who also happens to be a HNer. I think the lesson is rather important. [VIDEO]"},{"id":4354909,"author":"vivekjishtu","month":"2012-08","story":"Ask HN: Where is the Hostility on HN Coming From? ","stance":"reference","why":"How we talk to real people versus online","quote":"how we normally talk to a real person compared to someone online","text":"Just saw a video about something similar about how we normally talk to a real person compared to someone online. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["HCUct4NlxE0",1],["otbnC2zE2rw",1],["BTHvs3V8DBA",1],["ds77e3aO9nA",1]]},"dF_9YcehCZo":{"wy":"Recommended as an amazing blind-debugging story with deep detail on 1970s spacecraft computing.","q":{"x":"The talk is amazing and goes through the computer architecture of the spacecraft","i":41520970,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":41520970,"author":"tverbeure","month":"2024-09","story":"NASA Pulls Off Delicate Thruster Swap, Keeping Voyager 1 Mission Alive","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Blind debugging of Voyager's memory fault, covering architecture and old documentation","quote":"The talk is amazing and goes through the computer architecture of the spacecraft","text":"2 weeks ago, Bruce Wagoner from the Voyager program gave a talk at !!Con about how they recovered from the CMOS memory issue that they had a year ago. It’s basically blind debugging with a latency of 45 hours. The talk is amazing and goes through the computer architecture of the spacecraft as well as the challenges of dealing with something that is so old, with so documentation that has conflicting information or unreadable etc. [VIDEO]"},{"id":41521054,"author":"tverbeure","month":"2024-09","story":"NASA Pulls Off Delicate Thruster Swap, Keeping Voyager 1 Mission Alive","stance":"strong_rec","why":"How the team reasoned through missing and conflicting low-level information","quote":"Check out this amazing talk","text":"When Voyager failed last year with a CMOS memory error, one of the big problems was that a bunch of low level information was gone or conflicting. For example, they sometimes had to guess assembler instructions because the code printouts were low quality photocopied pages. Or because there were handwritten comments or comments scratched out with pencil without any clue about why it was done. One saving grace was the fact that the architecture and the code space was simple enough so that they could reason through the symptoms and actions to take, something that would have been much harder with modern spacecraft. Check out this amazing talk: [VIDEO]"},{"id":43165791,"author":"asalahli","month":"2025-02","story":"Ask HN: What are you working on? (February 2025)","stance":"rec","why":"Suggested as inspiring for the commenter's project","quote":"You might like this and or find it inspiring, in case you haven't seen it already","text":"You might like this and or find it inspiring, in case you haven't seen it already: Saving Voyager 1! - Bruce Waggoner at !!Con 2024 - [VIDEO] Edit: Also, please do share when it's playable :)"},{"id":41998078,"author":"Ductapemaster","month":"2024-10","story":"NASA reconnected with Voyager 1 after a brief pause","stance":"rec","why":"Voyager source code is scattered across papers and computers","quote":"Check out this talk","text":"Check out this talk: [VIDEO] The source code isn't hiding in a repo somewhere for security reasons — it's spread around on various pieces of paper and computers over the last 50 years. There isn't a single source of truth. Adds a whole other level of wizardry to keeping the thing running."},{"id":41520340,"author":"Twisol","month":"2024-09","story":"NASA Pulls Off Delicate Thruster Swap, Keeping Voyager 1 Mission Alive","stance":"rec","why":"Keynote by a Voyager team member on saving the probe","quote":"I had the extreme pleasure of seeing Bruce Waggoner of the Voyager team give a keynote at !!Con just last month.","text":"I had the extreme pleasure of seeing Bruce Waggoner of the Voyager team give a keynote at !!Con just last month. The recording landed on YouTube just a couple days ago, so this is great timing: [VIDEO]"},{"id":48187409,"author":"ndiddy","month":"2026-05","story":"NASA still maintains some of the Voyager spacecraft code from the 70s era","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as example of crew lacking expertise on Flight Data Subsystem","quote":"Here's one example from a talk about how the crew fixed an issue with the Flight Data Subsystem","text":"> Regarding \"that almost nobody on Earth fully understands anymore\", I claim this is nonsense, and definitely not an obstacle. It seems like you aren't very familiar with the history of the Voyager or the computer systems it uses. The Voyager has three onboard computers, each of which has a custom ISA. Two of the three computers were only used on Voyagers. After the main Voyager mission ended in the late 1980s and they were repurposed to collect data on interstellar space, the probes were reprogrammed to only require limited commands on one of the computers, and no intervention whatsoever on the other two. They also got rid of the testbench for testing code on the ground, so the only working Voyager systems are billions of miles away from Earth. Since then, Voyager has been operated by a skeleton crew that has been shrinking over time as people retire. The result of all of this is that they legitimately do not have a full understanding of how the hardware and software operates. Here's one example from a talk about how the crew fixed an issue with the Flight Data Subsystem, one of the computers that hadn't needed any significant software changes since the interstellar mission began ( [VIDEO] ): > So our top priority was to figure out what the FDS was and how it worked. Because unfortunately the person who was the real expert had retired decades ago and the person who was their fill-in, their backup, had retired two years ago. So it was the worst place it could have hit us. The"}],"r":[["rsxCZAE8QNA",1],["h7apO7q16V0",1],["43XaZEn2aLc",1],["d4EgbgTm0Bg",1],["3HYw6vPR9qU",1]]},"xNzoerDljjo":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it as a clear, dense hour on parsers and parser combinators that holds the audience.","q":{"x":"Saša Jurić is fantastic at condensing lots of information in a 1 hour talk without losing the audience","i":21862856,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":21862856,"author":"muhic","month":"2019-12","story":"Ask HN: Best Talks of 2019?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Speaker condenses lots of information in great talk.","quote":"Saša Jurić is fantastic at condensing lots of information in a 1 hour talk without losing the audience","text":"+1 for both talks Saša Jurić is fantastic at condensing lots of information in a 1 hour talk without losing the audience, he gave another great talk this year called Parsing from first principles ( [VIDEO] )."},{"id":26723089,"author":"sandbags","month":"2021-04","story":"Parser Combinators in Elixir","stance":"rec","why":"Live-codes a simple Elixir parser combinator library.","quote":"live-codes a simple Elixir PC library","text":"This is a good article. Especially in that it talks a little bit about error handling which does not feel like a strength of NimbleParsec. Another good resource is this talk by Saša Jurić on Parsing from First Principles ( [VIDEO] ) where he talks about parser combinators and live-codes a simple Elixir PC library. That inspired me to build my own alternative to NimbleParsec . Partly because my use case makes error handling very important and I couldn't get a grip on how to do that with NP and partly as a decent problem to get to grips with Elixir. I'm currently figuring out lookahead. This is a great area to play in that has a lot of useful applications."},{"id":23346035,"author":"anarchyrucks","month":"2020-05","story":"What's in a Parser Combinator? (2016)","stance":"rec","why":"Writing a parser combinator in Elixir.","quote":"is a good video on writing parser combinator in Elixir","text":"Parsing from first principles by Saša Jurić [0] is a good video on writing parser combinator in Elixir. [0] [VIDEO]"}]},"N4UFC0y1tY0":{"wy":"HN recommends it when people ask why games aren't marketed by creator like films, and for the value of attaching your name to your work.","q":{"x":"Great talk on that from the GettingOverIt/QWOP dev","i":32368052,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":43178301,"author":"soulofmischief","month":"2025-02","story":"Freelancing: How I found clients, part 1","stance":"rec","why":"Value of attaching your name to your work.","quote":"Bennett Foddy and Zach Gage have a great video","text":"Thank you for bringing up such a great point. I've worked with people in the past who only call me by a handle, even face to face. Some people really could care less depending on the scene. Other people will want to know everything about you, and it's your preference where your boundaries lie. I've also hired anonymous engineers before, depending on the work. If it's confidential IP or user data, it's risky to work with anonymous employees, though I am fine with them being pseudonymous within the company as long as I have verifiable data on file in the event of a sudden disappearance, breach of contract or something else. Personally, I have a few handles as well as my company, and different engagements may go through me personally or through my company. I'm forthcoming about my name in less public spaces, but Hacker News is quite a public space. I still sometimes consider attaching my name, but for now I don't. I express my own opinion on this website and don't want it to affect others. Bennett Foddy and Zach Gage have a great video [0] about the value in prominently attaching your name to your life's work and brand, and I think they raise very valid points. There are pros and cons to every approach, and I just recommend letting your principles and personal boundaries guide you; if you're fine with your name being public or semi-public, go for it. If not, you can still carve a unique path through this industry with a healthy bit of networking and showing up with a shovel. I t"},{"id":32368052,"author":"Vermeulen","month":"2022-08","story":"Ask HN: Why aren't games marketed as the work of director X like movies are?","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on marketing games by the developer's name.","quote":"Great talk on that from the GettingOverIt/QWOP dev","text":"Still are a lot of indie games that are marketed based on the famous developer. Great talk on that from the GettingOverIt/QWOP dev, 'Put Your Name On Your Game' [VIDEO]"},{"id":22886973,"author":"ecdavis","month":"2020-04","story":"Game developers share what they're most proud of making","stance":"rec","why":"GDC talk by Bennett Foddy on his games.","quote":"the whole presentation is worth watching.","text":"> I wonder how many people swore at him for what they got put through ;) I heard him mention in an interview that he received a lot of abuse (cathartic, not mean, from the way he described it). I think the mobile version actually used text messages, which must've been fun for him after the game took off. EDIT: It's here, in a GDC presentation: [VIDEO] -- the whole presentation is worth watching."}],"r":[["AJdEqssNZ-U",1],["_bP80DEAbuo",1],["SaadsrHBygc",1],["VdqhHKjepiE",1]]},"S2Hp_1jqpY8":{"wy":"HN points to it as a simple diff-and-patch alternative to operational transforms and CRDTs for collaborative editing.","q":{"x":"The really nice bits, though, are that it is a simple algorithm","i":7905216,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":24619222,"author":"mhale","month":"2020-09","story":"CRDTs are the future","stance":"rec","why":"Promising alternative to OT that chains servers.","quote":"another approach I've run across that looks very promising is Differential Synchronization[1] by Neil Fraser","text":"I'm working on a project with some offline data synchronization needs, but haven't started implementation yet. I've been following CRDTs with interest. I also saw many of the same downsides mentioned in the OP, e.g. bloat (which apparently are being addressed remarkably well). Beyond OT, another approach I've run across that looks very promising is Differential Synchronization[1] by Neil Fraser. While it also relies on a centralized server, it allows for servers to be chained in such a way that seems to address many of the downsides of OT. I wonder why I rarely ever see Differential Synchronization mentioned here on HN? Is it due to lack of awareness or because of use-case fit issues or some fatal flaw I haven't seen? Or something else? [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":7905216,"author":"dahjelle","month":"2014-06","story":"Eventual Consistency in Real-time Web Apps","stance":"rec","why":"Simple algorithm for convergence using diff/patch.","quote":"The really nice bits, though, are that it is a simple algorithm","text":"I've not tried it, but you might also be interested in an approach called differential synchronization[1]. Basically, it's just a specific way of passing diffs around to guarantee convergence, though (as far as I can tell) it doesn't guarantee much else (as in do you lose data if a patch can't be applied?). The really nice bits, though, are that it is a simple algorithm and that it can be applied to anything that you have diff/patch algorithms for. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":4479347,"author":"raganwald","month":"2012-09","story":"JSON Patch, a proposed standard for synchronizing JSON structures","stance":"rec","why":"Explains patch-based sync for real-time collaborative editing.","quote":"Here's a Google Tech Talk on the subject","text":"One real-world use case is differential synchronization. Here's a Google Tech Talk on the subject: [VIDEO] This is for real-time collaborative editing or replication of structured data (unlike Operational Transformation, which is optimized for text). Sending patches back and forth is at the core of the protocol."},{"id":17537848,"author":"pg_bot","month":"2018-07","story":"Differential Synchronization (2009)","stance":"reference","why":"Author explains why frequent small changes suit the algorithm.","quote":"Neil goes into further detail in this google talk about why this is the case","text":"Differential synchronization does not require a fixed polling interval. You can have real time updates as each party types, in fact the algorithm prefers this setup as it is easier to process a lot of small changes rapidly. Neil goes into further detail in this google talk about why this is the case: [VIDEO] . The trick is sync after someone changes the document while including a short debounce so that you aren't overloading the system with superfluous edits."}],"r":[["Q3uvzcY2Xug",1],["nXCSloXZ-wc",1]]},"pyX8kQ-JzHI":{"wy":"HN recommends Susskind as a very good explainer who uses real math at an accessible level, and links this lecture series for self-learners.","q":{"x":"He is a very good explainer.","i":14172681,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":44912398,"author":"jackdoe","month":"2025-08","story":"Death and What Comes Next (2002)","stance":"rec","why":"Susskind lectures among resources for laypeople learning quantum mechanics.","quote":"Leonard Susskind","text":"Ignoring the moral question in the story, many people are satisfied by saying \"haha funny, the cat is both dead and alive\" and they just move on. Reading Pratchett for almost 30 years, it has fueled quite an interested in quantum mechanics in me, he subtly introduces QM teasers in almost every book I read. Particularly Mort. As I dropped out of highschool, I didnt have the luck to properly study physics, but in my 30s, I my existential crisis hit, as they say, a man dies twice, and the stupid half dead cat wouldn't leave me alone. And one night the omnipotent youtube algorithm recommended Susskind's lectures to me, then Feynman, Shankar, .. it just kept going. Now I watch and re-watch them but take screenshots and send them to claude when I am confused (which is still most of the time). If you are a layman like me and just want to know what is up with the cat, I encourage you to watch Sean Carroll's lectures (dont miss the Q&A videos) and it will for sure allow you to ask at least better questions. I love that cat so much. --- Sean Carroll: [LINK] Richard Feynman: [LINK] and [LINK] Leonard Susskind: [VIDEO] Ramamurti Shankar: [LINK] Barton Zwiebach: [LINK] Carl Bender: [LINK] If you struggle with calculus, I found Herb Gross to be partucularly good Herb Gross: [LINK] Of course watch 3blue1brown, alphaphoenix, styropyro etc as well"},{"id":32221312,"author":"kriro","month":"2022-07","story":"Ask HN: YouTube Channels for the Intellectually Curious","stance":"rec","why":"Playlist of Susskind physics lectures.","quote":"Playlist of all Susskind Physics lectures","text":"Not so much channels but interesting series: Playlist of all Susskind Physics lectures: [VIDEO] Sanderson Writing class (fantasy but applies to all genres): [LINK] Channels: Andreas Spiess aka Guy With the Swiss Accent for embedded / microcontrollers: [LINK] Hoopers Beta for a scienctific/medical POV about climbing: [LINK] IppSec for HackTheBox to learn security: [LINK] LiveOverflow for security: [LINK] Yannic Kilcher for ML news / overviews: [LINK] French Guy Cooking: [LINK]"},{"id":14172681,"author":"21","month":"2017-04","story":"Physicists demonstrate new way to violate local causality","stance":"rec","why":"Susskind explains concepts with real math at accessible level.","quote":"He is a very good explainer.","text":"You can also look at some Susskind talks. He is a very good explainer. They don't skim on the math, but it's followable for somebody with a high-school level of math, at least for the basic concepts like spin or bell inequality. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["-kFOXP026eE",1],["HI09kat_GeI",1],["VMj-3S1tku0",1],["hlrv7DIHllE",1],["vT1JzLTH4G4",1],["MFRWDuduuSw",1],["PqPGOhXoprU",1],["5MgBikgcWnY",1],["OSGv2VnC0go",1],["rcwngbUrZNg",1]]},"sCZJblyT_XM":{"wy":"Recommended as a brilliant demonstration of how to think in Unix pipelines.","q":{"x":"gave a great talk about practical problem solving with the unix shell","i":19161505,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":19161505,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2019-02","story":"Problem solving with Unix commands","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Demonstrates building unix pipelines interactively by iterative refinement.","quote":"gave a great talk about practical problem solving with the unix shell","text":"Gary Bernhardt[1] gave a great talk about practical problem solving with the unix shell: \"The Unix Chainsaw\"[2]. \"Half-assed is OK when you only need half of an ass.\" In the talk, he gives several demonstrations a key aspect of why unix pipelines are so practically useful: you build them interactively . A complicated 4 line pipeline started as a single command that was gradually refined into something that actually solves a complicated problem. This talk demonstrates the part that isn't included in the the usual tutorials or \"cool 1-line command\" lists: the cycle of \"Try something. Hit up to get the command back. Make one iterative change and try again.\" [1] You might know him from his other hilarious talks like \"The Birth & Death of JavaScript\" or \"Wat\". [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":11567097,"author":"agumonkey","month":"2016-04","story":"Programming blogs","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Shows how to use tiny unix tools as levers on themselves.","quote":"No absurd wit as in wat. Simply brilliant.","text":"From the same person: - The unix chainsaw, how to use tiny tools[1] as lever upon themselves. No absurd wit as in wat. Simply brilliant. [VIDEO] - Also listed above the wat page, [LINK] : boundaries, a whole new world. More abstract wishful thinking. Still brilliant."},{"id":29825367,"author":"btschaegg","month":"2022-01","story":"Ask HN: I’m New to Computer Science and Engineering. Any Advice?","stance":"reference","why":"Part of broad advice about exploring tools and shells.","quote":"","text":"1.) Be curious. Stay curious. Experiment, play with things. Your time during university is likely the best time for this and it will likely get harder later on when you're on the job and there's much more emphasis on finished work than on learning experiences. Also, I'm quite sure that it will be beneficial for you if you familiarize yourself with different ways of thinking (obviously programming languages, environments and paradigms, but that will likely apply to anything non-technical, as well). I gained this perspective from dealing with people who didn't really do that, but also from knowing that I personally also did this way too late (and from the benefits it brought me nonetheless). If you're only familiar with Windows, try Linux. Try BSD. Try OS X, if you want to. Try working in the terminal. Familiarize yourself with different editors (especially Vim and Emacs, maybe also newer ones like Kakoune). Learn bash, have a look at AWK and sed. Maybe learn Perl. Try out functional programming, logic programming (like Prolog) and so on and so forth. If you're feeling adventurous, play with niche systems like Haiku[1] or TempleOS[2], just to gain new insights on how things could work. Maybe try the ACME editor[3]. [1]: [LINK] [2]: [LINK] [3]: [LINK] Of course, there might be courses that will nudge towards some of those tools. If you visit some of those: Cool! But if there are no such courses (I didn't encounter many of them myself), try to explore these things nonetheless. Ev"},{"id":19951990,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2019-05","story":"Understanding the fork system call in Unix","stance":"reference","why":"Related unix talk context in a thread about process costs.","quote":"","text":"As others have already menti9oned, running 'bc' isn't actually that expensive. Comparing it to Bitcoin is ridiculous. Let's investigate the actual cost of that sum: $ cat foo.sh #!/bin/bash a=\"1.5\" b=\"0.5\" sum=\"$(echo \"${a} + ${b}\" | bc)\" echo \"The sum of ${a} and ${b} is ${sum}\" $ /usr/bin/strace -q -D -w -c ./foo.sh The sum of 1.5 and 0.5 is 2.0 % time seconds usecs/call calls errors syscall ------ ----------- ----------- --------- --------- ---------------- 55.53 0.001225 123 10 read 9.75 0.000215 215 1 execve 5.53 0.000122 7 18 mmap 4.03 0.000089 8 11 3 open 3.04 0.000067 7 10 mprotect 2.99 0.000066 5 13 rt_sigprocmask 2.54 0.000056 56 1 clone 2.45 0.000054 5 10 close 2.40 0.000053 5 11 rt_sigaction 1.81 0.000040 5 8 fstat 1.41 0.000031 8 4 lseek 1.22 0.000027 5 5 3 stat 0.91 0.000020 20 1 write That was 56us to clone(2) the process and 215us to execve(2) 'bc'. All together just 12.74% of the time spent running syscalls , and an even smaller portion of the total time to launch and execute this simple example. Most of the time is sent launching bash itself at the beginning. Using a language such as python or launching a custom binary will have similar costs to to launch the program. If we're talking about a single persistent process (e.g. an interactive shell), then we're still talking about paying just 0.25ms of CPU time to perform a useful calculation . Compared to intentionally wasting time by design maximizing hash prefix guesses to \"mine\" Bitcoin \"ain't the same fucki"},{"id":14617836,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2017-06","story":"Twice as happy customers means half the marketing spend","stance":"reference","why":"Source of the half-assed-is-ok quip.","quote":"Sometimes half-assed is ok, if it's the the right half of the ass.[1]","text":"I know I've had a few situations where the \"play around with the numbers\" part happened first. Usually once the actual goal was known the algebra (or similar details) would be obvious... but sometimes the inefficient/toy implementation was good enough. Sometimes half-assed is ok, if it's the the right half of the ass.[1] The wrong half becomes yet another type of technical debt to be refactored \"someday\". (this is not an excuse to ignore algebra or blindly assume the easiest/most-fun method is correct/useful/sane) [1] [LINK] via [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["lpu-3UF_b48",1],["5bQhGS8V6dQ",1],["2-oBNh0UkQc",1],["Mfsnlbd-4xQ",1],["NVEgyJCTee4",1],["vuIlsN32WaE",1],["p2LZLYcu_JY",1],["1AjhFZVfB9c",1],["f_0QlhYlS8g",1],["46ehrFk-gLk",1],["RqQhWitJ1As",1],["spUNpyF58BY",1],["nT-TGvYOBpI",1],["Bkm2Vfj42FY",1],["FfDa8tR25dk",1],["2gFsOoKAHZg",1],["7CgXvIuZR40",1],["5f9R9MtkpqM",1],["QEJ9HrZq7Ro",1],["Ylomy1Aw9Hk",1]]},"4VrLQXR7mKU":{"wy":"HN brings it up in threads about captchas, bots and AI agents as an excellent short film about being a robot.","q":{"x":"there is an excellent short movie online called \"I'm not a Robot\".","i":49245537,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":49245537,"author":"jjbinx007","month":"2026-08","story":"Convince an AI it's not alive in psychological horror game Prove You're Human","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Excellent short movie about being a robot","quote":"there is an excellent short movie online called \"I'm not a Robot\".","text":"If anyone hasn't seen it yet there is an excellent short movie online called \"I'm not a Robot\". [VIDEO] Well worth a watch."},{"id":43427913,"author":"Aachen","month":"2025-03","story":"FOSS infrastructure is under attack by AI companies","stance":"rec","why":"Dutch sci-fi short, special and Black Mirror-like","quote":"well, that was certainly special.","text":"Oh my, a Dutch film that actually sounds good?! I get to watch a movie that's originally in my native language for perhaps the second time in my life, thanks for linking this :D Edit: and it's on YouTube in full! Was wondering which streaming service I'd have to buy for this niche genre of Dutch sci-fi but that makes life easy: [VIDEO] Final update: well, that was certainly special. Favorite moment was 10:26–10:36 ^^. Don't think that comes fully across in the baked-in subtitles in English though. Overall it could have been an episode of Dark Mirror, just shorter. Thanks again for the tip :)"},{"id":44745527,"author":"falcor84","month":"2025-07","story":"OpenAI's ChatGPT Agent casually clicks through \"I am not a robot\" verification","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"you should also consider that you might in fact be a robot.","text":"As per the Oscar winning \"I'm not a Robot\" [0], you should also consider that you might in fact be a robot. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":44748399,"author":"tanseydavid","month":"2025-07","story":"OpenAI's ChatGPT Agent casually clicks through \"I am not a robot\" verification","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Seems like a mention of the 2025 Academy Award winner for Best Action Live-Action Short","text":"Seems like a mention of the 2025 Academy Award winner for Best Action Live-Action Short, called \"I am not a Robot\" is in order here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":44150970,"author":"aspenmayer","month":"2025-06","story":"The Future of Comments Is Lies, I Guess","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Next, I'm sure, you'll be telling me you're not a bot, Mr Marshall? [VIDEO] Previously (6 months ago but didn't trend, perhaps due for a repost?): [LINK]"}],"r":[["oponIfu5L3Y",1]]},"eUHBPuHS-7s":{"wy":"HN returns to it as an uncannily prescient prediction of tech consolidation and AI-written, personalized news.","q":{"x":"Rewatching it now gives me this uncanny feeling.","i":47785366,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":38790733,"author":"kragen","month":"2023-12","story":"NY Times copyright suit wants OpenAI to delete all GPT instances","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Accurate prediction of AI-written news and newspaper copyright suit","quote":"the accuracy is astounding","text":"this was predicted in the very influential epic 2014 video in 02004 [VIDEO] (the original is flash and has thus been consigned to the memory hole, so we are left with this poor-quality conversion) 36\": 'however, the press as you know it has ceased to exist' 40\": '20th-century news organizations are an afterthought; a lonely remnant of a not-too-distant past' 2'11\": 'also in 2002, google launches google news, a news portal. news organizations cry foul. google news is edited entirely by computers' 5'13\": 'the news wars of 2010 are notable for the fact that no actual news organizations take part. googlezon finally checkmates microsoft with a feature the software giant cannot match: using a new algorithm, googlezon's computers construct new stories, dynamically stripping sentences and facts from all content sources, and recombining them. the computer writes a new story for every user' 5'55\": 'in 2011 the slumbering fourth estate awakes to make its first and final stand. the new york times company sues googlezon, claiming that the company's fact-stripping robots are a violation of copyright law. the case goes all the way to the supreme court' they didn't get the details exactly right, but overall the accuracy is astounding however, that may be a hyperstition artifact in this timeline [LINK] (i thought epic 2014 might be the only flash video to hae a wikipedia article about it, but then i looked and found five others)"},{"id":47785366,"author":"duckmysick","month":"2026-04","story":"Google Broke Its Promise to Me. Now ICE Has My Data","stance":"rec","why":"Speculative video on personalized computer-written news","quote":"Rewatching it now gives me this uncanny feeling.","text":"Does anyone else remember Epic 2014? It was a video made years ago that speculated about the future of the internet and media, with the end game being personalized news written by a computer. The timeline is off but the brand names are mostly the usual suspects. Rewatching it now gives me this uncanny feeling. [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":40653039,"author":"kragen","month":"2024-06","story":"The Rise and Fall of BNN Breaking, an AI-Generated News Outlet","stance":"reference","why":"Predicted computer-generated personalized news stories","quote":"this was predicted in epic 2014 as a product of googlezon in 2010","text":"this was predicted in epic 2014 as a product of googlezon in 2010 [VIDEO] > Googlezon finally checkmates Microsoft with a feature the software giant cannot match. Using a new algorithm, Googlezon's computers construct news stories, dynamically stripping sentences and facts from all content sources, and recombining them. The computer writes a new story for every user. it took a bit longer than we thought it would in 02004"},{"id":25951735,"author":"pgrote","month":"2021-01","story":"Robinhood Play Store listing went from 329K reviews to 180K in few hours","stance":"reference","why":"Prescient video predicting a few tech companies dominating","quote":"there was a prescient video where there turned out to be only 4 tech companies left","text":"I wish I could find it, but 10 years ago, probably longer now, there was a prescient video where there turned out to be only 4 tech companies left. Something like amazgooglesoft or something. The narrator had a deep voice and I think it was funded by a media company. Edit: Found it through the keywords, \"old prediction video where amazon microsoft google buy everything\" lol [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["OyfBQmvr2Hc",1],["RuCnZClWwpQ",1],["a9xAKttWgP4",1]]},"oGTQ0Wj6yIg":{"wy":"Offered as a persuasion success story in threads about polarization and online toxicity.","q":{"x":"His episode on the JRE is really good and his stories keep you engaged.","i":24630348,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":24630348,"author":"jjice","month":"2020-09","story":"Social Cooling (2017)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Engaging stories from Daryl Davis, who does most of the talking.","quote":"His episode on the JRE is really good and his stories keep you engaged.","text":"Daryl Davis is a really interesting guy. His episode on the JRE is really good and his stories keep you engaged. Even if you don't like Joe Rogan, you should still be able to enjoy his episode because Davis does the majority of the talking. [VIDEO]"},{"id":23685342,"author":"bhawks","month":"2020-06","story":"Reddit shuts down subreddits including r/The_Donald and r/ChapoTrapHouse","stance":"rec","why":"Daryl Davis on persuading klansmembers to leave through conversation.","quote":"Look at Daryl Davis's amazing work getting over 200 klansmembers to turn away from the klan through honest conversation.","text":"The meme that you can't have a conversation with a person that changes their perspective and ideologies is a false and harmful one. Look at Daryl Davis's amazing work getting over 200 klansmembers to turn away from the klan through honest conversation. Is it easy? Of course not. But it's not impossible to reach people - it happens every day. [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":22205168,"author":"brlewis","month":"2020-01","story":"Ask HN: How do we stop the polarization/toxicicity filling the web?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Successful persuasion stories are always interesting to me","text":"Successful persuasion stories are always interesting to me, but at over 2.5 hours I'm unlikely to watch this whole thing. Can you please list some key takeaways? [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["WVDQEoe6ZWY",1],["4PaWFYm0kEw",1]]},"-ZSli7QW4rg":{"wy":"Cited as the go-to evidence that rudeness drives contributors away and that toxic people must be removed from projects.","q":{"x":"Assholes will ruin your project[1], you need to get rid of them","i":7205532,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":37506138,"author":"Modified3019","month":"2023-09","story":"Don't Use Discord as a Forum","stance":"rec","why":"Related video on toxic people in projects.","quote":"A good video related to this","text":"Good call. A good video related to this: \"Assholes Are Ruining Your Project. Donnie Berkholz (RedMonk)\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":7205532,"author":"justincormack","month":"2014-02","story":"The GNU C Library version 2.19 is now available","stance":"rec","why":"Argues toxic people ruin projects and must be removed.","quote":"Assholes will ruin your project[1], you need to get rid of them","text":"The glibc team are great people to work with; I managed to get a patch into this release which had been point blank refused by Ulrich Drepper when he was maintainer, and they were incredibly helpful and the process was really easy. So get your fixes in - glibc is becoming much more standards compliant. Assholes will ruin your project[1], you need to get rid of them, whether it is open source or not. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":34312508,"author":"Beltalowda","month":"2023-01","story":"Sourcehut will blacklist the Go module mirror","stance":"reference","why":"Best available evidence that rudeness drives contributors away.","quote":"The best I know of is \"Assholes are Ruining Your Project\"[1] from a few years back.","text":"> I've noticed that claims like this are never backed by any evidence of this improvement, or evidence of people who actually have been chased away by rudeness. I am one of them. I've seen other people claim the same. I did not keep a list, nor did I keep a list of all of his posts that I found egregious, and I don't really feel like spending a lot of time crawling through all posts to find them, so I guess this is all I have. It's hard to get \"hard evidence\" for these kind of things in the first place. Most people just disengage and don't come back. The best I know of is \"Assholes are Ruining Your Project\"[1] from a few years back. It would be interesting to check similar numbers for Go and other projects. I'm not sure if it's easy to get these kind of numbers from e.g. Slack or Reddit though. [1]: [LINK] / [VIDEO] > total exile is a big hammer that should be used more judiciously. It wasn't the first time he was banned, but I'm not privy to the exact details on this. Was total \"total exile\" proportional? I don't know: obviously I didn't see everything. I just wanted to say he didn't \"just\" get banned over a minor thing, but after many years of problematic behaviour that had been raised plenty of times."},{"id":19543047,"author":"jlokier","month":"2019-04","story":"On Being a Free Software Maintainer","stance":"reference","why":"Relevant to overwhelmed maintainers facing abusive demands.","quote":"Assholes are Killing Your Project\" (Donnie Berkholz)","text":"Even though bad faith criticism and complaints can be useful, I find there is a still an insurmountable problem sometimes: it can be too time- and energy-consuming to handle the sheer quantity and/or intensity. Sometimes, especially at \"political moments\" (like elections in a project I was involved in recently - and I wasn't even a candidate), it can feel like being overwhelmed by a tidal wave. If I only have 1 spare hour per day for a project, there is neither time nor emotional energy to handle emails that are so full of demanding questions that it would take 3 hours to process carefully and reply carefully. And if you don't reply carefully, they send you more, writing longer and getting more obsessive (and sometimes personal) as if they sense you'll engage them in a long conversation, so being careful in the first place is a net win. Even if it only took 1 hour, that's still used up all the time I was going to use on other productive things, the \"real work\". I am in awe of maintainers who seem to reply to an onslaught of demanding questioners, and somehow satisfy them. For me, these are relevant: \"Assholes are Killing Your Project\" (Donnie Berkholz) [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":13656206,"author":"JoshTriplett","month":"2017-02","story":"Brilliant Jerks Cost More Than They Are Worth","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"See also and .","text":"Exactly . You have to pick: either the jerks, or the people who the jerks drive away. And that doesn't take into account the people who aren't quite driven away but find work that much more unpleasant as a result. If you encounter many skilled jerks, consider why that might be, and why in the same environment you don't also encounter skilled people who are fun to work with. In such an environment, the skilled people who are fun to work with can afford to leave, while some of the non-skilled people may not have that option. So, it's natural that within such an environment (company, project, etc), it'll look like a correlation between jerkiness and skill. People then start assuming a causation, and thus contribute to that same cycle. The opposite environment, with many skilled people who are fun to work with, will tend to reject jerks regardless of skill. I've also noticed that such an environment tends to have a much higher degree of mentorship and collaboration, in addition to being more fun to work in, and boosting energy rather than draining it. See also [VIDEO] and [LINK] ."}],"r":[["KTzGBJPuJwM",1]]},"AcS3NOQnsQM":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as a favorite Lehrer song and invoke it as a joke whenever elements or science-themed wordplay come up.","q":{"x":"is the Tom Lehrer song that made the biggest impression on me.","i":34025355,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":34025355,"author":"nayuki","month":"2022-12","story":"Tom Lehrer has released all of his songs into the public domain","stance":"rec","why":"Song reciting chemical elements.","quote":"is the Tom Lehrer song that made the biggest impression on me.","text":"\"The Elements\" is the Tom Lehrer song that made the biggest impression on me. [LINK] , [VIDEO] There's antimony, arsenic, aluminum, selenium, And hydrogen and oxygen and nitrogen and rhenium, ..."},{"id":23224338,"author":"Diederich","month":"2020-05","story":"Google deletes “communist bandits” from comments on Youtube","stance":"rec","why":"Light-hearted song reciting chemical elements, with a surprise ending.","quote":"and his satire is top shelf.","text":"For those who aren't aware and/or won't look this reference: [LINK] As the other comenter noted, Tom Lehrer is indeed a national treasure, and his satire is top shelf. From the light-hearted \"The Elements\": [VIDEO] (watch for the surprise at the end) To the gallows comedy of \"We Will All Go Together When We Go\": [LINK] NB: I appreciate the irony linking to YouTube content in a 'WTF Google/YouTube' thread."},{"id":24283304,"author":"nayuki","month":"2020-08","story":"Tom Lehrer's Mathematical Songs (1951)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"The first work of Tom Lehrer's that I heard is","text":"The first work of Tom Lehrer's that I heard is \"The Elements\", where he recites chemical element names. [LINK] ; [VIDEO]"},{"id":36527146,"author":"Kaibeezy","month":"2023-06","story":"Researcher discovers new isotope of astatine","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Tom Lehrer sang about it.","text":"Don’t know much about astatine except it’s the rarest naturally occurring element and Tom Lehrer sang about it. [VIDEO]"},{"id":8860835,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2015-01","story":"Design of Lisp-Based Processors Or, LAMBDA: The Ultimate Opcode (1979) [pdf]","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"[Apologies to Tom Lehrer.]","text":"Jonathan Rees's a la carte menu of the elements of \"object oriented programming\" is a great example of that. [LINK] The Elements of Object Oriented Programming: Encapsulation, Protection, Ad Hoc Polymorphism, Parametric Polymorphism, Everything Is An Object, All You Can Do Is Send A Message, Specification Inheritance, Implementation inheritance/Reuse, Sum-Of-Product-Of-Function Pattern. These are the only ones of which the news has come to Harvard, and there may be many others, but they haven't been discarvered. ;) [Apologies to Tom Lehrer.] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["cZYNADOHhVY",1],["1Jwo5qc78QU",1],["dnHdqPBrtH8",1],["r1ZLGqL1FMo",1],["7J52mDjZzto",1],["q_vivC7c_1k",1],["P7GKK3liv8M",1],["7zrx-b2sLUs",1],["MFEHFsO-XSI",1],["43XKfuptnik",1],["9W74aeuqsiU",1],["kdz6FeQLuHQ",1],["d4EgbgTm0Bg",1],["dqQw05Mr63E",1],["Y99Wj-NStok",1],["-opBifFfsMY",1],["f7MzFfuNOtY",1],["HI09kat_GeI",1],["kTKn1aSOyOs",1],["ZXtimhT-ff4",1]]},"GCSXNGc7pfs":{"wy":"Cited in COVID-era threads as a physician's evidence-based argument that vitamin D is cheap, low-risk and worth considering.","q":{"x":"It's cheap and has minimal downside.","i":23189758,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":23189758,"author":"throwaway829","month":"2020-05","story":"Vitamin D and Inflammation: Potential Implications for Severity of Covid-19","stance":"rec","why":"Doctor recommending vitamin D, cheap with minimal downside.","quote":"It's cheap and has minimal downside.","text":"I doubt anyone here thinks it's some kind of magic, but if you've been following the pandemic from the beginning, you've probably seen these two doctors who both recommend vitamin D: - Dr. Seheult's: [LINK] - Dr John Campbell: [VIDEO] It's cheap and has minimal downside. Pre-covid it was already known to \"reduce symptoms of colds or influenza\"[0]. [0] Benefits of Vitamin D Supplementation - Journal of American Physicians and Surgeons [LINK]"},{"id":22901165,"author":"Zenst","month":"2020-04","story":"How the Anti-Vaccine Community Is Responding to Covid-19","stance":"rec","why":"Evidence-focused doctor's overview of Covid-19 and vitamin D.","quote":"A Doctor who has been doing a fair and most excellent overview with a critical mind","text":"Sure it's a correlation, in much the same way SAD syndrome more prevalent the further North you go. But some things may well have better papers or links, I was just pointing out what seems to be logical - however - watch this please: [VIDEO] A Doctor who has been doing a fair and most excellent overview with a critical mind upon this whole COVID-19 from early on and focused upon evidence. He covers things in a way that may hold more gravitas to that aspect. One of the many useful reference links from that video that I think covers all this fair and square. [LINK]"},{"id":29351680,"author":"andruby","month":"2021-11","story":"The Impact of Vitamin D Level on Covid-19: Systematic Review Meta-Analysis","stance":"reference","why":"Early discussion of vitamin D from April 2020.","quote":"He's actually been talking about Vitamin D since April 2020","text":"He's actually been talking about Vitamin D since April 2020 [0] and discussed the encouraging first clinical trial in September 2020 [1]. There are plenty of published studies on Vitamin D and its effects on Covid. I don't understand why healthcare guidelines don't mention or promote it more. The only reason I've come up with so far is that the pharmaceutical companies don't focus on it since there is no money to be made. Vit. D is basically free (€4 for a year's supply of 20 mcg/day). Without the push and lobbying from Pharma, I guess it doesn't get enough attention? [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":25261664,"author":"Zenst","month":"2020-12","story":"Vitamin D insufficiency may account for almost 90% of Covid-19 deaths","stance":"reference","why":"Evidence about vitamin D from April.","quote":"I remember this back from April and lots of evidence back then","text":"> I am honestly completely shocked that we haven't seen governments and the media increasing people's awareness of this issue. Same, and in the UK at least we got this: [LINK] I remember this back from April and lots of evidence back then: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23120795,"author":"fattire","month":"2020-05","story":"Vitamin D looks powerful, underutilized for Covid-19","stance":"reference","why":"One of several videos on vitamin D deficiency and mortality.","quote":"has put out a few videos from time to time related to vitamin D","text":"Dr. John Campbell, an educator in Northern England who's been doing daily Youtube updates on Covid news since January I believe, has put out a few videos from time to time related to vitamin D: [LINK] - A month ago [VIDEO] - 3 weeks ago [LINK] - 3 days ago He suggests vitamin D deficiency may play a partial role in explaining higher mortality rates among people with darker skin (other important social disparities notwithstanding)."},{"id":23170947,"author":"fattire","month":"2020-05","story":"Role of Vitamin D in the prevention of Coronavirus 2019 infection and mortality","stance":"reference","why":"One of several videos citing studies on vitamin D deficiency.","quote":"He offers published studies as evidence to suggest vitamin D deficiency may play a partial role","text":"Posted this previously when the subject came up, but John Campbell, an nurse and educator in Northern England who's been doing daily Youtube updates on global Covid news since January I believe, has put out a few videos from time to time related to vitamin D: [LINK] - 2 months ago [VIDEO] - 4 weeks ago [LINK] - 1 week ago [LINK] - 4 days ago [LINK] - today He offers published studies as evidence to suggest vitamin D deficiency may play a partial role in explaining higher mortality rates among people with darker skin (other important social disparities notwithstanding)."}],"r":[["DGscoaUWW2M",1],["KDpNQQqdSh8",1]]},"HfHgUu_8KgA":{"wy":"HN commenters treat it as the standard short answer whenever UK taps come up.","q":{"x":"Short video by Tom Scott on the subject for those interested.","i":26552093,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":26552093,"author":"SimonPStevens","month":"2021-03","story":"Rents for the rich are going down, rents for the poor are going up. Why?","stance":"rec","why":"Short explanation of UK water storage tanks and separate taps","quote":"Short video by Tom Scott on the subject for those interested.","text":"Short video by Tom Scott on the subject for those interested. [VIDEO] tldw; Older homes in the UK had a water storage tank in the roof space that fed the water heating tank. This couldn't be classified as drinking water so wasn't allowed to be combined with the safe mains water that fed the cold tap. These days modern houses mostly use combi-boilers with no storage tank, so mostly both hot and cold are drinkable and it's not an issue. But preferences still lean towards the separate taps."},{"id":9208819,"author":"rogerbinns","month":"2015-03","story":"Britain's housing crisis is a human disaster","stance":"rec","why":"Tom Scott explains why separate taps make sense","quote":"It makes a lot of sense having them separate. Tom Scott explains","text":"It makes a lot of sense having them separate. Tom Scott explains [VIDEO] Here in California showers and much other plumbing are required to have backflow valves so that hot and cold don't cross contaminate."},{"id":43640133,"author":"throw0101d","month":"2025-04","story":"BS 1363 British Plugs and Sockets","stance":"reference","why":"Obligatory Tom Scott video on UK separate taps","quote":"Obligatory Tom Scott video on the topic","text":"Obligatory Tom Scott video on the topic: * [LINK] Bonus: \"Why Britain Uses Separate Hot and Cold Taps\": * [VIDEO]"},{"id":37603888,"author":"crote","month":"2023-09","story":"Lead poisoning causes far more death, IQ loss than thought: study","stance":"reference","why":"Source for historical reasons behind separate taps","quote":"That's because it is primarily a historical artifact[0].","text":"That's because it is primarily a historical artifact[0]. Most modern plumbing heats water on-demand, which pretty much entirely avoids the risk of storage tank contamination or Legionella. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34638452,"author":"WirelessGigabit","month":"2023-02","story":"A manifesto on shower temperature control","stance":"reference","why":"Explanation of why the UK historically has two taps","quote":"Also mandatory explanation why UK historically has had 2 taps","text":"Mandatory Giphy: [LINK] Also mandatory explanation why UK historically has had 2 taps: [VIDEO] Which in the end is understandable. It's outdated. What isn't understandable is having the light switch OUTSIDE of the friggin' restroom."},{"id":31928726,"author":"cyounkins","month":"2022-06","story":"Controversy continues over whether hot water freezes faster than cold","stance":"reference","why":"History of UK separate taps and unsanitary cold water tanks","quote":"See also Tom Scott's video on Britain's history with separate hot and cold taps","text":"Hot water heaters frequently have sacrificial anode rods to prevent corrosion. The rods corrode over time and dissolve into the water. Lower cost aluminum rods have health concerns, not as much with magnesium rods. See also Tom Scott's video on Britain's history with separate hot and cold taps and why hot taps sometimes had unsanitary cold water tanks to supply them: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29078220,"author":"stevekemp","month":"2021-11","story":"Culture shock","stance":"reference","why":"Explains UK separate taps from water storage history","quote":"the UK typically has separate taps due to the way the water is stored, or used to be stored","text":"On the topic of mixer-taps in bathrooms, the UK typically has separate taps due to the way the water is stored, or used to be stored: [VIDEO] I was pleased when I remodelled my bathroom there and installed a mixer. So much more convenient."},{"id":28192211,"author":"msie","month":"2021-08","story":"Bottled water is 3,500 times worse for the environment than tap water","stance":"reference","why":"Explains why UK hot taps should not be drunk from","quote":"you shouldn't trust drinking from the hot tap","text":"At least in Britain, if there are separate taps for hot and cold, you shouldn't trust drinking from the hot tap: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["Ik1AK56FtVc",1],["RfdZTZQvuCo",1],["nBNTAmLRmUg",1],["y5UT04p5f7U",1]]},"ICp2-EUKQAI":{"wy":"Shared in Excel threads as a funny parody that treats spreadsheets like an e-sport.","q":{"x":"","i":40682633,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":40682633,"author":"Terr_","month":"2024-06","story":"The Microsoft Excel superstars throw down in Vegas","stance":"rec","why":"Parody videos treating Excel like an e-sport","quote":"these two great parody videos from ~5 years back, of Excel using e-sport tropes","text":"That makes me think of these two great parody videos from ~5 years back, of Excel using e-sport tropes, introduced but a (fictional) livestreaming competitor nicknamed Makro. \"Microsoft Excel stream highlights\" [LINK] \"XLOOKUP\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":23237593,"author":"somethoughts","month":"2020-05","story":"Microsoft announces Lists, a new Airtable-like app","stance":"rec","why":"Krazam parody of Excel streamers","quote":"had to put these videos by Krazam somewhere at some point on HN. Enjoy!","text":"Somewhat tangential (more Excel related) but had to put these videos by Krazam somewhere at some point on HN. Enjoy! MAKRO | Microsoft Excel Stream Highlights 3/19 [LINK] MAKRO | XLOOKUP: META BREAKER? | Microsoft Excel Stream Highlights 10/19 [VIDEO]"},{"id":32748290,"author":"DarkCrusader2","month":"2022-09","story":"New Text and Array Functions for Excel","stance":"reference","why":"Krazam sketch about competitive Excel","quote":"I watched a couple of videos by Krazam [0][1] on competitive Excel.","text":"A while ago I watched a couple of videos by Krazam [0][1] on competitive Excel. Thought it was just a sketch, didn't realize it was actually a thing. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":47588067,"author":"blitzar","month":"2026-03","story":"Seeing Like a Spreadsheet","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"tried to attract the party gamers by breaking the meta with xlookup?","text":"You just have to go to Ballmercon. Or are maybe they mean everyone is abandoning excel after they failed to patch out the cheeses and tried to attract the party gamers by breaking the meta with xlookup? [VIDEO]"},{"id":27511569,"author":"Thorrez","month":"2021-06","story":"Google Workspace for everyone","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":">or do they want to run a program for little kindergarteners to track how many times they poop their diapers every day? [VIDEO] Wow, Google literally demos almost exactly that: [LINK]"},{"id":24932647,"author":"gfodor","month":"2020-10","story":"Microsoft Overhauls Excel with Custom Data Types","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Ballmercon 2021 is gonna be lit AF.","text":"Wow, this totally blows up the scene. I'm sure there are gonna be haters, but Ballmercon 2021 is gonna be lit AF. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["aXQ2lO3ieBA",1],["u4L130DkdOw",1],["jbkSRLYSojo",1],["wVp5koAOu9M",1],["RWTic9btP38",1],["0MeRN7LE1LQ",1],["ydqReeTV_vk",1],["agzNANfNlTs",1],["jzMFoNZeZm0",1],["3dl9Ovwmnxw",1]]},"JAk448volww":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as the canonical joke about administrators multiplying regardless of the work, whenever threads turn to institutional or corporate bloat.","q":{"x":"a conversation between the minister & his private secretary where the latter explains why administrators are essential no matter what","i":30896853,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":30896853,"author":"maxwelldone","month":"2022-04","story":"Employees are returning to the office to sit on zoom calls","stance":"rec","why":"Scene explaining why administrators are essential in the empty hospital","quote":"a conversation between the minister & his private secretary where the latter explains why administrators are essential no matter what","text":"This reminds me of a great scene[1] from the British show \"Yes, Minister\". It's from an episode called The Compassionate Society where a minister for Department of Administrative Affairs learns about a newly built hospital which has some 500 employees but no doctors & patients. The scene I linked is a conversation between the minister & his private secretary where the latter explains why administrators are essential no matter what. [1] [VIDEO] Full episode: [LINK]"},{"id":28124525,"author":"YeBanKo","month":"2021-08","story":"New York Public Library’s Picture Collection Will Be Archived","stance":"rec","why":"Yes Minister dialog on administrator bloat","quote":"One of my favorite dialogs in Yes, minister!","text":"“Once the Picture Collection is relocated, its space in Room 100 will house administrators who were hired for educational outreach.” - inflation of administrative apparatus is a scourge of modern society. It is partially responsible for growing costs of education and healthcare. One of my favorite dialogs in Yes, minister! [VIDEO]"},{"id":42904093,"author":"frenchtoast8","month":"2025-02","story":"Macrodata Refinement","stance":"reference","why":"Yes Minister hospital with 500 employees as analogy for Severance","quote":"Like the hospital in Yes, Minister that is closed to the public but has 500 employees","text":"My theory is that all the severed employees are doing various maintenance tasks to keep the floor functioning. Like the hospital in Yes, Minister that is closed to the public but has 500 employees, all of them overworked.[1] Take O&D for instance, their full time job appears to be to just create art and handbooks used on the severed floor. Perhaps all the departments are like that. MDR could be doing some sort of ongoing maintenance for the severed system itself, like emotion or memory control of the employees on the floor. I believe the \"point\" of the severed floor is not the actual work that's being done, but the act of keeping them occupied while they are experimented on. Besides Mark S and Cold Harbor, I believe there are hints of other experiments being run. The dreams Irving B has during work seem unique to him, and it's uniquely affecting his outie as well. I also believe recent events in Season 2 with Dylan G could be the start of another experiment. ^1: [VIDEO]"},{"id":43009887,"author":"jodrellblank","month":"2025-02","story":"Teen on Musk's DOGE team graduated from 'The Com'","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> \"I do. Property in one thing that I think should be equally divided among individuals, with individuals being able to use/rent in whatever manner they please within certain confines (lots of assumptions here and lots of if and buts, but it's huge discussion).\" That's interesting, and rather goes up against the free markety ideas that the people with the most valuable use for land/property should be able to buy as much of it as they want from people with less valuable uses (who can sell it at market rates). I am interested and do wonder how that might work, and if it would work. Although I note that thing_you_like is allowed to be nuanced and complex, and thing_you_dislike must be bad because it's complex. > All that will disproportionately affect the poor not the rich who can actually afford the taxes. That happens when a speeding ticket is a fixed $100. Norway can give speeding fines up to 10% of the driver's annual salary. A bad property tax disproportionately hurts the poor, not all property taxes. Your parent comment \" Perhaps the most important question to ask is not who will gain - rather who stands to lose? \" is the entire reason taxes get complicated - because every change is a change that someone gains and someone else loses. Any thing which is paid for by taxes has an argument over who should pay the tax, and how much of it; do parents pay tax for schools? Society benefits from more educated people, so does everyone pay? Is it an income tax because people who bene"},{"id":25970210,"author":"waterhouse","month":"2021-01","story":"University of Leicester firing all pure math faculty","stance":"reference","why":"Yes Minister episode about a hospital with staff and no patients","quote":"has an episode about a hospital with 500 employees and no patients","text":"\"Yes, Minister\" has an episode about a hospital with 500 employees and no patients: [VIDEO] The show's creators said: \"After inventing this absurdity, we discovered there were six such hospitals (or very large empty wings of hospitals) exactly as we had described them in our episode, notably one in Cambridgeshire in which there was only one patient: the Matron (head of nursing staff) who had fallen over some scaffolding and broken her leg.\""}]},"JIQETrFC_SQ":{"wy":"HN points to it as an authoritative overview of AWS infrastructure, including what AZs and regions really mean.","q":{"x":"James Hamilton gives a good talk that goes over networking (and more).","i":12606353,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":12606353,"author":"dastbe","month":"2016-09","story":"An AWS Region is coming to France","stance":"rec","why":"Hamilton covers AWS networking and infrastructure at scale.","quote":"James Hamilton gives a good talk that goes over networking (and more).","text":"James Hamilton gives a good talk that goes over networking (and more). This is it from reinvent 2014. [VIDEO]"},{"id":9038307,"author":"MrZipf","month":"2015-02","story":"Introducing “6-pack”: the first open hardware modular switch","stance":"rec","why":"James Hamilton on building own hardware for clean-sheet software design.","quote":"The talk was on HN a little while ago, worth watching","text":"This looks great. They also have the advantage that James Hamilton mentioned AMZ got from building their own hardware - the get to do a cleansheet s/w design and not have to support all the gazillion options that Cisco does. The talk was on HN a little while ago, worth watching: [VIDEO] The scale at FB means they're probably saving hundreds of millions doing this."},{"id":22944964,"author":"ignoramous","month":"2020-04","story":"Now Open – AWS Africa (Cape Town) Region","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"have given pretty good overview over the years of the AWS infrastructure.","text":"James Hamilton [0][1] and others [2][3][4] have given pretty good overview over the years of the AWS infrastructure. [0] AWS Innovation at Scale (2014), [VIDEO] [1] Tuesday Night Live (2016), [LINK] [2] Operation of the AWS Global Network (2019), [LINK] [3] Foundations of AWS Infrastructure (2018), [LINK] [4] Exploring the AWS Global Network (2018), [LINK]"},{"id":12611486,"author":"idunno246","month":"2016-09","story":"New P2 Instance Type for Amazon EC2 – Up to 16 GPUs","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"I think this is the right talk by him","text":"[VIDEO] I think this is the right talk by him, but there's one where he implies some differences. For instance, that because they can promise exact temperature ranges they can clock them differently. My guess is same fab, just binned differently, or maybe different packaging."},{"id":10549429,"author":"Twirrim","month":"2015-11","story":"Backblaze Storage Pod 5.0","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"James Hamilton talks precisely what the definitions of AZs and regions are","text":"Around about here in the talk: [VIDEO] James Hamilton talks precisely what the definitions of AZs and regions are :)"},{"id":8647844,"author":"nandemo","month":"2014-11","story":"A peek at the massive scale of AWS","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Yes, Hamilton points that out in his talk","text":"> This suggests to me that running distributed databases in multiple AZ's has almost no latency penalty (e.g. you won't even paying eventual consistency / replication lag taxes that you might think are a danger). Yes, Hamilton points that out in his talk: [VIDEO]"}]},"NPOHf20slZg":{"wy":"HN threads on the Rabbit R1 point to it as an exposé on the company's history and operation.","q":{"x":"It's a bit clickbaity, but turns out Rabbit was a crypto-nft-ai buzzword company with a very shady history.","i":40965423,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":40965423,"author":"sadeshmukh","month":"2024-07","story":"The Rabbit R1 has been logging users' chats – with no way to wipe them","stance":"rec","why":"Rabbit's shady history as a crypto-NFT-AI buzzword company.","quote":"It's a bit clickbaity, but turns out Rabbit was a crypto-nft-ai buzzword company with a very shady history.","text":"See this video by Coffeezilla: [VIDEO] It's a bit clickbaity, but turns out Rabbit was a crypto-nft-ai buzzword company with a very shady history."},{"id":40993158,"author":"seanhunter","month":"2024-07","story":"Jailbreaking RabbitOS","stance":"rec","why":"Investigates claims about Rabbit's large action model.","quote":"I would encourage people to watch and and do whatever personal research and make their own minds up","text":"As I understand it, you can think of Rabbit's \"stack\" as a prompt to a standard llm (I think they just used openAI but I could be wrong) to understand what you want, and then a bunch of (brittle) selenium scripts to go call various websites to do the thing. The llm is there to understand that you've said \"what's the weather?\" not, say \"call me an uber\", and then to collate the responses from potentially several calls to websites and produce a natural language response. There is a layer of upsell/hustle/scam[1] on top of that where they said they had a \"large action model\" which learned your preferences and understood how all these websites worked sematically so the actions would be robust etc and a bunch of other stuff which turned out not to be true. [1] depending on your point of view. I would encourage people to watch [VIDEO] and [LINK] and do whatever personal research and make their own minds up"},{"id":40589543,"author":"stmonty","month":"2024-06","story":"Rabbit R1 It's a Scam","stance":"rec","why":"Exposes more about the R1 and its operation.","quote":"seem relevant for exposing more about the R1 and its operation","text":"These two videos by the YouTuber CoffeeZilla seem relevant for exposing more about the R1 and its operation: [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":40796875,"author":"VTimofeenko","month":"2024-06","story":"Rabbit data breach: all r1 responses ever given can be downloaded","stance":"reference","why":"Claims Rabbit's earlier projects were questionable.","quote":"the r1 is not their first questionable project","text":"If claims from this[1] video by Coffezilla are to be believed, the r1 is not their first questionable project. [VIDEO]"},{"id":40443336,"author":"ffsm8","month":"2024-05","story":"HumaneAI pin maker selling itself for $1B","stance":"reference","why":"Coffeezilla video on the company and its CEO.","quote":"Coffezilla made a video on this company and their CEO.","text":"Coffezilla made a video on this company and their CEO. It makes total sense in that context. (That they're trying to make a quick exit before further things come to light) [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["kya_LXa_y1E",1],["fNk_zzaMoSs",1],["lG2dXobAXLI",1]]},"S-VAL7Epn3o":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it as an accessible, non-technical look at real game pathfinding problems from a developer who built them.","q":{"x":"Ars Technica had a series of 10 minute videos where they interviewed developers about technical challenges.","i":44612131,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":44612131,"author":"saithound","month":"2025-07","story":"The year of peak might and magic","stance":"rec","why":"Developer interview on pathfinding and CD video in Westwood RTS games.","quote":"Ars Technica had a series of 10 minute videos where they interviewed developers about technical challenges.","text":"You're probably looking for something more detailed and implementation-focused, but Ars Technica had a series of 10 minute videos where they interviewed developers about technical challenges. E.g. they had one on pathfinding and CD video in second generation Westwood RTS games [1] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":23256362,"author":"imjasonmiller","month":"2020-05","story":"EA will be releasing the C&C Tiberian Dawn and Red Alert source code under GPL3","stance":"rec","why":"Great video on pathfinding challenges at the time.","quote":"there is this great video on some of their challenges at the time","text":"With regard to pathfinding, there is this great video on some of their challenges at the time [1]. 1. [VIDEO]"},{"id":47007631,"author":"shoo","month":"2026-02","story":"Age of Empires: 25 years of pathfinding problems with C++ [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Non-technical overview for a general audience.","quote":"(2019, non-technical, for a general audience)","text":"see also: \"How Command & Conquer: Tiberian Sun Solved Pathfinding\" (2019, non-technical, for a general audience) [VIDEO]"},{"id":28338682,"author":"huachimingo","month":"2021-08","story":"Now that machines can learn, can they unlearn?","stance":"reference","why":"Anecdote about making a bot make mistakes.","quote":"sometimes making a bot do mistakes is harder than making a correct bot.","text":"Tangentially related, sometimes making a bot do mistakes is harder than making a correct bot.See Command and Conquer's pathfinding anecdote: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["qgG5Jhi_Els",1]]},"h7Sv_pS8MgQ":{"wy":"Pointed to in 2020 testing threads for a visual explanation of why rapid antigen tests still serve a purpose.","q":{"x":"Medcram has a good video talking about this.","i":25755305,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":25755305,"author":"devy","month":"2021-01","story":"U.S. to require negative Covid-19 tests for international air passengers","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why rapid tests still serve a purpose despite lower accuracy.","quote":"Medcram has a good video talking about this.","text":"> But if the rapid tests are as unreliable in general as they were for me, it’s sort of no wonder COVID is spreading like wildfire. Many rapid tests are indeed not accurate[1] but they still serve a purpose, depending on the sensitivity and specificity of the tests[2]. For the rapid tests with high sensitivity and you get a positive result, it's highly likely you have COVID. For the rapid tests with high specificity and you get a negative result, it's highly likely that you don't have COVID. And the public health measures of testing / contact tracing ONLY works if testing results come back quick. PCR tests are neither fast nor cheap, so those are not as effective in prevention of spreading because you don't do those all the time (slow + expensive). Medcram has a good video talking about this. [3] [1]: [LINK] [2]: [LINK] [3]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24101848,"author":"jdc","month":"2020-08","story":"Interview with Bill Gates on Covid, social media","stance":"rec","why":"One of several MedCram lectures on at-home rapid testing.","quote":"Coronavirus Pandemic Update 98: At Home COVID-19 Testing - A Possible Breakthrough (~ 15 min)","text":"Right - see the MedCram lectures on the topic: How to Beat COVID-19 with At-Home, Rapid Result Testing: 5 Min. Summary by Dr. Mina (Antigen Tests) [LINK] Coronavirus Pandemic Update 98: At Home COVID-19 Testing - A Possible Breakthrough (~ 15 min) [VIDEO] At Home, Cheap, COVID-19 Tests with Results in 15 Minutes: How to Fix Testing with Dr. Michael Mina (~ 1 hr) [LINK]"},{"id":23947635,"author":"acqq","month":"2020-07","story":"Saliva based Covid test: results in 45 minutes","stance":"reference","why":"Visual explanation of rapid at-home antigen testing concepts.","quote":"The visual explanation of the ideas and concepts presented there","text":"The visual explanation of the ideas and concepts presented there, also mentioned in the later podcast: Medcram: “Coronavirus Pandemic Update 98: At Home COVID-19 Testing - A Possible Breakthrough” [VIDEO] Also, regarding the Ct value, from: “Interpreting the COVID-19 Test Results: a Guide for Physiatrists” [LINK] In PCR tests “the diagnosis is made based on the threshold cycle (Ct) value. Ct is defined as the cycle number when the sample fluorescence exceeds a chosen threshold above the calculated background fluorescence.5 In other words, the lower the Ct value of a specific gene, the more the gene exists in the sample. However, the problem with a Ct-based diagnosis is that there is no absolute or constant Ct cut-off value, and Ct cut-off values are different for each diagnostic reagent even for the same gene.”"}]},"lpu-3UF_b48":{"wy":"Offered as a pragmatic, convincing counter to the idea that Perl 6 is too strange to use.","q":{"x":"Here's a talk I gave at FOSDEM explaining how day-to-day Perl 6 is fairly straightforward and easier than people think","i":9491310,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":10342757,"author":"agumonkey","month":"2015-10","story":"Larry Wall Unveils Perl 6.0.0","stance":"rec","why":"Talk found convincing about Perl 6 pragmatism.","quote":"I found this talk pretty convincing about pragmatism","text":"The typing and grammar mechanism aren't common, it may influence other languages. Also, Perl managed to survive even without mainstream relevance so the community will probably keep enjoying it. ps: I found this talk pretty convincing about pragmatism [VIDEO] also: [LINK]"},{"id":9491310,"author":"Ovid","month":"2015-05","story":"Three Tales of Second System Syndrome","stance":"rec","why":"FOSDEM talk showing day-to-day Perl 6 is straightforward.","quote":"Here's a talk I gave at FOSDEM explaining how day-to-day Perl 6 is fairly straightforward and easier than people think","text":"Perl 6 isn't as complex as people make it out to be. I have friends who are race car enthusiasts who could go in to excruciating detail about cars and if you don't know how to drive, it could seem daunting. The reality is that most of us get in to our car and just drive. That's part of the issue with Perl 6: its proponents get very excited about it and write about all the really cool stuff you can do with it, but for those who don't know the language, it sounds intimidating. Here's a talk I gave at FOSDEM explaining how day-to-day Perl 6 is fairly straightforward and easier than people think: [VIDEO]"},{"id":9791211,"author":"raiph","month":"2015-06","story":"The Three Great Virtues of a Programmer: Laziness, Impatience, and Hubris","stance":"reference","why":"Talk comparing basic math and string handling across languages.","quote":"From a recent talk[1], what are x, y, z in the following?","text":"> [lang X] doesn't handle [basic math] correctly among other things. From a recent talk[1], what are x, y, z in the following? x = 7 ÷ 2; y = -7 ÷ 2; z = .1 + .2 - .3 x y z 3.5 -3.5 0 # human 3 ? tiny number # c89; ? depends on compiler 3 -4 tiny number # ruby 3 -4 tiny number # python 2/3 3 -3 0 # bc 3.5 -3.5 tiny number # perl5 3.5 -3.5 0 # perl6 ---- It isn't just basic math. How \"long\" is the following string? ปฏิบัติจริง 7 characters # human len(\"ปฏิบัติจริง\") = 33 # python 2 len(\"ปฏิบัติจริง\") = 11 # python 3 \"ปฏิบัติจริง\".length = ? # ruby (For Ruby, I've found \"the core language does not provide a way to count [human perceived characters]\". [3] Is that still correct or out of date?) ---- > Perl suffers from having too many magical operators that makes it hard to reason about a snippet of code. I feel Python strikes the right balance. To quote Larry Wall, \"Perl 6 ... has been rethought and rebalanced on every level\".[4] It might be interesting for you to pick a couple Perl 5 operators that you consider too magical; then we could look at the merits and demerits of those ops in Perl 5 and their equivalents in Python and Perl 6. > It's very easy to express ideas I have in mind without worrying about the details [of Python, now, and Perl, back in the day] This, of course, has explicitly been a deliberate focus of all the Perls. Part of the point of Perl 6 was to remove the bits in Perl 5 that were problematic (including some \"too cute/weird\" stuff) and to introduce much ni"}],"r":[["5bQhGS8V6dQ",1],["2-oBNh0UkQc",1],["sCZJblyT_XM",1],["Mfsnlbd-4xQ",1],["NVEgyJCTee4",1],["vuIlsN32WaE",1],["p2LZLYcu_JY",1],["1AjhFZVfB9c",1],["f_0QlhYlS8g",1]]},"p-I8GDklsN4":{"wy":"HN cites it as the go-to documentary on how car-industry interests undermined US transit.","q":{"x":"I think the documentary Taken for a ride(1996)","i":10123752,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":18779479,"author":"caravaggi0","month":"2018-12","story":"Fundamental rule of traffic: building new roads makes people drive more (2015)","stance":"rec","why":"Documentary on powerful interests undermining public transport.","quote":"Seen this?","text":"and building trains makes people ride trains. The fundamental rule is path of least resistance, literally and figuratively. It also matters who has the powerful interest. Seen this? Taken for a Ride - The U.S. History of the Assault on Public Transport in the Last Century [VIDEO]"},{"id":10123752,"author":"myth_buster","month":"2015-08","story":"The Uber endgame: privatized public transit","stance":"rec","why":"1996 documentary on the assault on US public transport.","quote":"I think the documentary Taken for a ride(1996)","text":"I think the documentary Taken for a ride(1996) [0][1] which showed up on /r/Documentaries [2] is perhaps relevant to this discussion. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":42827116,"author":"wonder_er","month":"2025-01","story":"How far can you get in 40 minutes from each subway station in NYC?","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for GM buying and shutting down streetcars.","quote":"most us cities did have subways (or streetcars), then GM bought 'em all and shut them down.","text":"most us cities did have subways (or streetcars), then GM bought 'em all and shut them down. [0] [0] Taken for a Ride - The U.S. History of the Assault on Public Transport in the Last Century: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32038140,"author":"jlarocco","month":"2022-07","story":"Why BART uses a nonstandard broad gauge","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as evidence car company profits killed rail.","quote":"It had less to do with NIMBYism and more to do with car company profits.","text":"It had less to do with NIMBYism and more to do with car company profits. [1] Here in the Denver metro area, we used to have trains running all over the place, and 100 years ago, there were daily trains between Denver, Boulder, Golden, and Longmont. Then they ended service and tore up most of the tracks. Fast-forward to 2022, and now they're trying again to put rail between Boulder and Denver, but it's a $1+ billion boondoggle that hasn't gone anywhere in 15 years. [2] [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":22115490,"author":"rasengan0","month":"2020-01","story":"The Cruise Origin","stance":"reference","why":"Linked as history of GM and transit.","quote":"","text":"$7.25 Billion - [LINK] Yep, same GM, now add Honda and Son-sama and we're ready for act 2: [VIDEO] Just in time for global warming Anecdotally, Cruise has been cruising around the city for years like they're helping rebuild Apple Maps or something. Now we know, hard to miss the big launch, getting out of the 47 Van Ness and seeing the usual special event at SVN West. How ironic it was at the old Honda dealership and less than 1/2 block away from the homeless navigation center. Seeing as homeless folk ride free in MUNI, I have to wonder will they get to cruise in Origin, too? Maybe good Breed PR gesture. Probably not. Wrong use case. If they crash and burn hopefully the IP will be worth something. Otherwise seppuku time. All the data from tooling around the city has got to be worth something."}],"r":[["3qHkcs3kG44",1],["taaEzHI9xyY",1]]},"xn76r0JxqNM":{"wy":"HN recommends it for first-hand accounts of how early 3D games like Doom worked within tight hardware limits.","q":{"x":"Some interesting related stuff in this talk","i":29127641,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":29494692,"author":"rasz","month":"2021-12","story":"Building a PS1 style retro 3D renderer","stance":"rec","why":"Pioneers explain how Doom and Wolfenstein avoided heavy math.","quote":"Wolfenstein and Doom are 100% correct because Carmack 'cheated' by deciding to never look down/up or draw slopes :-)","text":"From the horses mouths (minus Abrash/Carmack) 'HandmadeCon 2016 - History of Software Texture Mapping in Games' [VIDEO] Wolfenstein and Doom are 100% correct because Carmack 'cheated' by deciding to never look down/up or draw slopes :-) so whole game is drawn with 'lines of constant Z'. Or as they put it Chris Hecker (Microsoft/Maxis/etc): that's a classic Carmack thing which is like Fuck those general problems, Im gonna solve this other problem perfectly John Miles (ORIGIN/Miles Design/etc): Its all about not doing the math, we were still at a point in time when you won by not doing the math"},{"id":29127641,"author":"cma","month":"2021-11","story":"Ask HN: How were video games from the 90s so efficient?","stance":"rec","why":"Veterans discuss software texture mapping optimizations in 90s games.","quote":"Some interesting related stuff in this talk","text":"Some interesting related stuff in this talk: HandmadeCon 2016 - History of Software Texture Mapping in Games [VIDEO] I think they say at one point it went from 14 to 8 instructions, and then the Duke Nukem guy (Ken Silverman) got it down to around 4. Quake would do something where it only issued a divide every 8 pixels or something, and then only interpolate when inbetween and the out of order execution on pentium pro (I think?) would let it all work out."},{"id":30979390,"author":"corysama","month":"2022-04","story":"3D Software Rendering Technology of 1998's Thief: The Dark Project (2011)","stance":"reference","why":"Devs of early software 3D games recall working without references.","quote":"gathered a group of devs from different games who all wrote some of the first software rasterization 3D games.","text":"Garret’s buddies at HandmadeCon gathered a group of devs from different games who all wrote some of the first software rasterization 3D games. The general theme was “We had no idea what we were doing. There were no references!” [VIDEO]"},{"id":29533638,"author":"thoughtFrame","month":"2021-12","story":"Tacit knowledge is more important than deliberate practice","stance":"reference","why":"Example of teams accumulating tacit knowledge about texture mapping.","quote":"Those teams built up that kind of tacit knowledge like in this video about software texture mapping","text":"I guess Michael Abrash's Graphics Programming Black Book fits the bill, though no amount of literature replaces getting your hands dirty in late 80s-early 90s level hardware+software. However, while you can do this, you're still missing the things you learned by being part of a team (of the kind which there are probably in the low hundreds right now). How much I'd give to be part of Nintendo or Id software or some other era-appropriate team figuring out how to make the early big 3D games like Mario 64 or its contemporaries on the PSX. Of course you can bust out an SDK and try it out but it probably won't be the same. Those teams built up that kind of tacit knowledge like in this video about software texture mapping [VIDEO]"},{"id":29632275,"author":"rasz","month":"2021-12","story":"War stories: how Crash Bandicoot hacked the original Playstation (2020)","stance":"reference","why":"Source for how Doom rendered with constant-z planes.","quote":"doom doesnt operate on pixels, it operates on constant-z planes (lines)","text":"- it didnt, at least not Intel x86 MHz - doom doesnt operate on pixels, it operates on constant-z planes (lines), you can scale linearly inside those (cheap 2D operation). How Carmack did it? He cheated by deciding to never look down/up or draw slopes :) 'HandmadeCon 2016 - History of Software Texture Mapping in Games' [VIDEO] Chris Hecker (Microsoft/Maxis/etc): that's a classic Carmack thing which is like Fuck those general problems, Im gonna solve this other problem perfectly John Miles (ORIGIN/Miles Design/etc): Its all about not doing the math, we were still at a point in time when you won by not doing the math"}],"r":[["vYA0f6R5KAI",1],["tUAX0gnZ3Nw",1],["8b7erU_DOfE",1],["bJ5ppf0po3k",1],["3MghYhf-GhU",1]]},"hNRO7lno_DM":{"wy":"Held up as the peak of demoscene hardware hacking, especially for its finale and for how good the PC speaker sound gets.","q":{"x":"The 8088MPH demo also sounds surprisingly good.","i":26591722,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":13019388,"author":"i336_","month":"2016-11","story":"The BASIC Issue with Retro Computers","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Live cheering at the demo's finale; modern tooling in credits","quote":"Piece de resistance","text":"You've probably seen these but I thought I'd mention them anyway. [LINK] [LINK] Piece de resistance: [LINK] (live cheering FTW) / [VIDEO] (capture card copy) The credits in the 3rd video are an excellent example of using modern tooling. (How this was done has been documented online and is easy to find; it did take some cheating to do it but it's still awesome.) Hrm, the SID music in [LINK] is also amazing (this demo was on two floppies, I particularly like the music in the 2nd part which uses a distinctly different theme) and very obviously produced by modern tools too. (Note, that video is a 50fps capture and uses a real SID.)"},{"id":26591722,"author":"amiga-workbench","month":"2021-03","story":"The Sound of 1-bit: Musical Creativity on the 48k Sinclair ZX Spectrum (2017)","stance":"reference","why":"Demo sounds surprisingly good on PC speaker","quote":"The 8088MPH demo also sounds surprisingly good.","text":"I don't remember it being widely used for music, but some games did a pretty good job with it. [LINK] The 8088MPH demo also sounds surprisingly good. [VIDEO]"},{"id":19356744,"author":"PavlovsCat","month":"2019-03","story":"Daydreaming about the future instead of doing work today","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> It's like a forum full of carpenters that are using Black and Decker tools to build houses. In that analogy the end result is the same, but the process to get there more complicated. In reality, the difference is between a house that has stuff like insulation and faucets that don't leak, versus than something slapped together in 5 minutes for $50 that costs 50 times more to live in, for as long as you live in it. It's less effort for the programmer, more effort for every machine it gets run on, every time it gets run . And that stuff compounds in non-linear ways, too: The more data has to be read from disks, the more useless the disk cache becomes, the more data has to be shuffled around in RAM, the more useless the CPU cache becomes. Using gigantic frameworks for programs that use 0.1% of them, which still set up and maybe even poll for all sorts of stuff, not knowing it's not needed, is not \"wrong\" (certainly not ever when it's open source), but it's more wasteful for what it achieves . I just checked, and my Directory Opus with 3 windows open, one of them having 2 panes, and all the custom buttons etc. is taking up 100 MB of memory. The list of features is huge. Another thing I have to think of is audio software. Sure, there's also VST that come with gigantic bitmaps or whatever, but generally, efficiency is king. They know their code doesn't run in a vacuum and they are dealing with customers (even when talking about free software) who pay keen attention to such things,"},{"id":16992835,"author":"jammi","month":"2018-05","story":"C64 Keyboard Prototype","stance":"reference","why":"Demo ending shows PC beeper sounding good via optimization","quote":"like shown at the end of the 8088 MPH demo","text":"A PC beeper can sound just as good, when used on a fast enough PC and the beeper output is connected to something better than the built-in piezo speaker. Even on the original IBM PC, you could make it sound almost as good by very careful optimization and close to 100% of allocation of CPU cycles, like shown at the end of the 8088 MPH demo: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["6orsmFndx_o",1],["LA-gNoxSLCE",1]]},"-E6vHCT0wpw":{"wy":"Cited in threads on writing systems and CJK characters as an entertaining test of how much Japanese people can understand of Chinese.","q":{"x":"I found these videos quite entertaining","i":26578873,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":26578873,"author":"scaladev","month":"2021-03","story":"Japanese typography: Kilograms of sinograms","stance":"rec","why":"Entertaining look at Japanese people reading Chinese words with mismatched meanings.","quote":"I found these videos quite entertaining","text":">there's a bunch of stuff that kind of looks like it would make sense when reading a Chinese word in Japanese, but turns out there's a lot of meanings are very different As someone who knows nothing of the Japanese language beyond the very basics, I found these videos quite entertaining: [VIDEO] [LINK] It looks like many characters are familiar to his respondents, but their combinations make absolutely no sense."},{"id":39242384,"author":"bane","month":"2024-02","story":"History of Vietnamese Typography","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I've thought about this a little bit and wonder if the result CJK readers have ended up with is the result of a few interesting historic quirks. Assuming that homophones are simply going to exist in the languages, there's probably other alternatives. For example, in the case of modern Korean readers, the Hanja can supposedly used to help clarify the meaning of a homophone where context doesn't provide clarity. But in practice, most Korean readers don't use Hanja enough to remember more than a small percentage of what they learn in school, so they work in effect as an index into a lookup table, where they look the Hanja up in a dictionary, find the definition written in Hangul, and use that to determine the meaning. Support Japanese was reformed to entirely use one or both Kana in the way that modern Koreans use mostly Hangul. Then Kanji would be used to help determine meaning in the same way. As most people wouldn't encounter Kanji with enough frequency and distribution to remember the bulk of them, only the most common would be remembered. These are unlikely to be the difficult to discern homophones as contextual clues would clarify. So the Kanji would again also end up as indexes for looking up standardized definitions. In modern Japanese, this doesn't happen because Kanji is still used with enough frequency and variety that most people sustain some level of memory about the system. The question is how well do they remember it? [1][2][3][4][5] 1 - [LINK] 2 - [LINK] 3 - [LIN"},{"id":24766380,"author":"moogleii","month":"2020-10","story":"What Chinese looks, feels and sounds like when you're from Korea or Japan (2009)","stance":"reference","why":"Related challenge video on Japanese people reading Chinese.","quote":"That one's more fun because it's a little more challenging for the Japanese folk","text":"That one's more fun because it's a little more challenging for the Japanese folk, but the original is a bit more fair since it's vs traditional Chinese characters (and spoken Cantonese is a bit closer to the version that was imported by Japan): [VIDEO]"},{"id":24069203,"author":"anilakar","month":"2020-08","story":"Blissymbols","stance":"reference","why":"Answers whether Japanese speakers can read Chinese characters.","quote":"you can guess, but don't expect it to be exactly correct","text":"You are not the first one who has asked that question. In case you can't watch videos right now, the short answer is \"you can guess, but don't expect it to be exactly correct\". [VIDEO] [LINK]"}],"r":[["rzJqXd-1dEU",1]]},"1vpepaQ-VQQ":{"wy":"HN cites it as broad, accessible evidence that cheap Android TV boxes often come loaded with malware.","q":{"x":"found many cheap Android TV boxes were infested with malware","i":36022122,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":37562067,"author":"vosper","month":"2023-09","story":"Apple TV, now with more Tailscale","stance":"rec","why":"Warns cheap Android TV boxes ship with malware.","quote":"Beware that a lot of cheap Android TV boxes come pre-loaded with heaps of malware.","text":"Beware that a lot of cheap Android TV boxes come pre-loaded with heaps of malware. You don't want them in your network. Linus Tech Tips has a video about it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35768860,"author":"rektide","month":"2023-05","story":"The situation with malware on Android TV ROMs is ridiculous","stance":"reference","why":"LTT covered malware on many cheap Android TV boxes.","quote":"LTT didn't go super deep, but they did go pretty wide, & looked at a bunch of boxes","text":"This has been well covered by Linus Tech Tips very recently. [VIDEO] There's a great submission a couple hours ago on why LTT has been so effective covering it. [LINK] LTT cites some other major sources that were already publishing the absurd shit packaged on the various boxes. LTT didn't go super deep, but they did go pretty wide, & looked at a bunch of boxes, & were like, yup, all horrible, just absolutely deplorable garbage! So sad that arm is such a lowly immature ecosystem. If these were more like the computers we know love & take for granted we'd just do a fresh install of a regular OS like Debian & these would be potentially useful interesting boxes. Instead they're just garbage forever, unless you are an embedded kernel hacker. It's crazy that arm has been around so long & is still nowhere near being a viable software platform, that the whole stack is a giant deck of cards from flakey weird bootloader, to janky forked kernels with hacked together blobs & shitty kernel code, to godforsaken abominable user lands riddled with junkware. It's incredible to contrast to the PC world, where interoperability lead to choice lead to making things better. ARM as an ecosystem has had no need to deal straight with it's users & that's why it's been such a rotting cesspool of tech for so many decades now."},{"id":36021034,"author":"supriyo-biswas","month":"2023-05","story":"Potentially millions of Android TVs and phones come with malware preinstalled","stance":"reference","why":"LTT showed Chinese TV boxes with malware, supporting the report.","quote":"LTT did a video[1] on the same topic showing a few Chinese smart TV boxes with malware.","text":"Because unlike cybercriminals operating out of their home who can't consider a lawsuit because it'd only serve to surface their presence to law enforcement, companies can and do sue others on libel and similar grounds, even when there is no merit to the lawsuit. LTT did a video[1] on the same topic showing a few Chinese smart TV boxes with malware. There is no reason to believe the report is illegitimate. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":36022122,"author":"dghughes","month":"2023-05","story":"Potentially millions of Android TVs and phones come with malware preinstalled","stance":"reference","why":"LTT found many cheap Android TV boxes infested with malware.","quote":"found many cheap Android TV boxes were infested with malware","text":"Linus Tech Tips YouTube channel also did a review and found many cheap Android TV boxes were infested with malware. [VIDEO] It was perfect timing since I am trying to find some simple solution to my old Roku I use to bypass my \"smart\" TV. But Roku is probably just as invasive privacy-wise."},{"id":35578369,"author":"HnUser12","month":"2023-04","story":"Why is ‘juice jacking’ suddenly back in the news?","stance":"reference","why":"Evidence that Amazon-sold devices may be compromised.","quote":"we don’t know if the stuff itself is compromised","text":"The problem with buying stuff off Amazon is, we don’t know if the stuff itself is compromised[1][2]. I wish some well-known manufacturer would offer something like this. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] (Youtube)"}]},"2RQrKP9a0XE":{"wy":"HN uses it as visual evidence that cycling can move huge numbers of commuters in little space.","q":{"x":"how much space would you need if every single cyclist was driving a large SUV?","i":42205605,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":18410332,"author":"iknowstuff","month":"2018-11","story":"Ford Buying San Francisco-Area E-Scooter Startup Spin","stance":"rec","why":"Shows heavy bike commuting is feasible in Utrecht.","quote":"","text":">Truly - what % of people could actually commute using these things? It's very small. Of those, who would actually do it? In winter? Up and down hills? Are they a little bit too old for this, or maybe not so open minded? [VIDEO] Potentially: Everyone, winter to summer (look up his other videos), up and down hills (they're electric, hills don't matter), regardless of age. America is not the only country in the world, but even the US has cities far denser than the spread out, gridlocked hell of LA like San Francisco or NYC."},{"id":42205605,"author":"crote","month":"2024-11","story":"A common urban intersection in the Netherlands (2018)","stance":"reference","why":"Shows cycling infrastructure moves many people in little space.","quote":"how much space would you need if every single cyclist was driving a large SUV?","text":"On the other hand, the reduction in cars due to people switching to cycling makes the infrastructure incredibly cheap . Look at the video in [0]: how much space would you need if every single cyclist was driving a large SUV? Look how smooth the traffic flows through the intersection, how many flyovers would you need to achieve this with cars? Yes, cycle infrastructure does indeed take up a nonzero amount of space. But it easily pays for itself by reducing the need for far more space-consuming car infrastructure. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35198734,"author":"actionablefiber","month":"2023-03","story":"xkcd: Will _____ Destroy Whole Industries? (Simple Answers)","stance":"reference","why":"Example of a busy cycle path for judging commuting alternatives.","quote":"look at major commuting thoroughfares where the people are not in cars to begin with, like busy sidewalks and cyclepaths.","text":"I think an easy way to answer this question is to look at major commuting thoroughfares where the people are not in cars to begin with, like busy sidewalks and cyclepaths. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["lJ3NC-R3gSI",1],["xSGx3HSjKDo",1],["XuBdf9jYj7o",1]]},"9jMONZMuS2U":{"wy":"HN mentions it mostly as a condensed summary of that roundtable, in threads about COVID debate and content moderation, and not as a consensus recommendation.","m":[{"id":30221227,"author":"loceng","month":"2022-02","story":"Spotify deletes 70 Joe Rogan episodes","stance":"rec","why":"38-minute summary of a Senate roundtable on pandemic response","quote":"The 38 minute compilation is a great, easily consumable summary","text":"Have you seen this yet? \"COVID-19: A Second Opinion\" - [VIDEO] \"On January 24, 2022 Senator Ron Johnson invited a group of world renowned doctors and medical experts to the U.S. Senate to provide a different perspective on the global pandemic response, the current state of knowledge of early and hospital treatment, vaccine efficacy and safety, what went right, what went wrong, what should be done now, and what needs to be addressed long term. This 38 minute video highlights the 5-hour discussion.\" The 38 minute compilation is a great, easily consumable summary - however here's the link to the full 5+ hours of testimony - [LINK] - which obviously has far more depth to it. The reason I ask if you've seen it is Joe's had multiple of these highly credentialed, front-line workers (doctors, researchers, etc. with expert domain knowledge and experience), on his podcast - some multiple times. It's going to get harder to ignore these people and what they have to say the more formal the setting the information is shared. Another video everyone should watch is Maddie De Garay's mother giving testimony for 10 minutes of their experience of her daughter being in Pfizer's 12-15 year old vaccine clinical trial. The limitations designed into the clinical trial reporting app might be most obvious to UX designers, but I think everyone will be able to understand the design implications: [LINK]"},{"id":46524811,"author":"alex1138","month":"2026-01","story":"Google Co-Founder Sergey Brin's Unretirement Is a Lesson for the Rest of Us","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"here's it on Youtube which is the highlights","text":"So if we're linking stuff (and please don't scream at me for Rumble, Youtube loves removing videos) here's one [LINK] I was initially trying to make a point that the ideological lines that people have drawn have made it so they automatically think RFK is anti-science and they as a consequence have a whole host of assumptions for which I don't even blame them if they haven't spent time reading up about it. I apologize for not countering every single point and going to covid but it's kind of worth pointing out that RFK a) raises some very substantive charges against Fauci for frankly war criminal like actions throughout his unfortunate history of practicing medicine and b) it wasn't \"mismanagement\". They did, seriously, the opposite of good practice (both accepted good practice and what was discovered in 2020 going forward) in just about every case If you're not sympathetic to that then of course you're going to disagree and you might think that the only reason someone like Musk bought X (and please don't think this is me Musk fanning, I dislike him for several reasons) is just to have a joyride (which is still possible); they used to ban so many people and real doctors for information that they didn't like and it was a serious problem and if they could do that for covid then they also did it for other things Edit: (see, I do know how to edit comments) here's it on Youtube which is the highlights [VIDEO]"},{"id":30231592,"author":"loceng","month":"2022-02","story":"Spotify deletes 70 Joe Rogan episodes","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"It's quite telling that your standards are so low, and that you think an app purposefully designed to prevent reporting of adverse events is somehow me expecting \"perfectionism.\" That component on its own is not a complex problem - it's blindingly obvious. You're trying to gaslight to categorize something as simple and obvious is a \"conspiracy theory\" is \"classic\" attempt to lazily smear and demonize, meanwhile you actually avoid addressing my specific points. So let's address 1 of my specific points shall we, and not claim that I'm merely being a perfectionist to dismiss discussing it? Is it a problem, yes or no, that the adverse events that could be reported by participants in at least the Pfizer mRNA clinical trial for 12-15 year olds limited what could be reported by highly specifically creating a relatively short list of relatively minor potential adverse events? Yes or no? Now, please brainstorm and extrapolate to the potential consequences of the app that participants were provided as the only means of reporting adverse events, and write out your thoughts here. It's obvious you don't care much for integrity as you dismiss crucial structure for an apparatus meant to and necessary to capture the truth, the whole truth, and nothing but the truth - but interestingly the operators and designers of the clinical trial decided to design the app to not allow any free-form writing option - like you and I are able to do here? Surely you're smart enough and have enough integrity t"},{"id":30089157,"author":"twofornone","month":"2022-01","story":"Pfizer board member suggests end to mask, vaccine mandates","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"dozens of hours of testimony on behalf of researches and clinicians before congresspeople is simply being ignored[0].","text":"I don't think you understand how many assumptions are baked into the current conclusions that vaccines and masks are effective. This relies on accurate reporting of vaccination status and accurate reporting of breakthrough cases. Meanwhile in the real world covid is spreading unabated, record case counts, regardless of vaccination rate or mask compliance. Clearly something isn't adding up. Meanwhile the rosy estimates of safety and effectiveness have been repeatedly and consistently walked back. I think it's time to recognize that our institutions have had their analyses and recommendations tainted by strong and pervasive political and social biases, from the laypeople who have no incentive to report breakthrough cases, all the way up through the chain past academics and policymakers who have their careers riding on the validity of 2 years of covid research and policy that cannot be contradicted without trashing credibility. That's not to mention the career and social stigma associated with speaking out or publishing negatively against the vaccines. It doesn't help that dozens of hours of testimony on behalf of researches and clinicians before congresspeople is simply being ignored[0]. Add media to the rabidly biased groups and you're getting closer to reconciling what is being published and communicated about the vaccines and virus with what is happening in the real world. 0. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["tIuj-oMN-Fk",1],["_b4qKv1Ctv8",1],["pwaWilO_Pig",1],["q5SRrsr-Iug",1],["Z7rd04KzLcg",1]]},"AqDdWEiSwMM":{"wy":"Shared as the place to see where the Zig project is heading, especially on compiler performance.","q":{"x":"that is basically all about compilation speed","i":35996794,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":35996794,"author":"rudedogg","month":"2023-05","story":"Zig now has built-in HTTP server and client in std","stance":"rec","why":"Roadmap talk focused on compiler performance","quote":"that is basically all about compilation speed","text":"> To write a fast compiler, it has to be fast from the start. The focus on compilation speed is one of the reasons I'm so interested in Zig. The compiler uses an interesting data-oriented design to represent the AST nodes, etc. You can read about it here: - [LINK] - [LINK] The creator of Zig also has a talk from Handmade Seattle talking about some of the performance gains they've had using these techniques: - [LINK] And a talk \"Zig 2023 Roadmap\" that is basically all about compilation speed: - [VIDEO]"},{"id":31484246,"author":"kristoff_it","month":"2022-05","story":"How Uber Uses Zig","stance":"reference","why":"Project direction and roadmap for Zig","quote":"You might also be interested in reading about the project to see if you like where we're going","text":"The worst part is that it's not done yet :^) This means that you will not find super complete introductory materials about every aspect of programming with Zig. The language reference is very good but for example the standard library doesn't have docs yet as we're still working on the autodoc tool. On the other hand reading the stdlib source code is very easy in good part thanks to the reasons you already mentioned in your post. [LINK] You might also be interested in reading about the project to see if you like where we're going (aside from technical details of the language). [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO] You can find links to learning materials in the \"learn\" section of the official website: [LINK] Last but not least, at the moment we compensate the lack of learning materials by having a community that it's very good at helping newcomers, so make sure you join one of the communities."},{"id":31052234,"author":"AndyKelley","month":"2022-04","story":"Zig self hosted compiler is now capable of building itself","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on upcoming Zig compiler milestones","quote":"The talk recording about upcoming compiler milestones is uploaded now","text":"Hello HN! Here is some context to decorate this announcement: The Zig self-hosted compiler codebase consists of 197,549 lines of code. There are several different backends, each at varying levels of completion. Here is how many behavior tests are passing: LLVM: 1101/1138 (97%) WASM: 919/1138 (81%) C: 740/1138 (65%) x86_64: 725/1138 (64%) arm: 490/1138 (43%) aarch64: 411/1138 (36%) As you might guess, the one that this milestone is celebrating is the LLVM backend, which is now able to compile the compiler itself, despite 3% of the behavior tests not yet passing. The new compiler codebase, which is written in Zig instead of C++, uses significantly less memory, and represents a modest performance improvement. There are 5 more upcoming compiler milestones which will have a more significant impact on compilation speed. I talked about this in detail last weekend at the Zig meetup in Milan, Italy[1]. There are 3 main things needed before we can ship this new compiler to everyone. 1. bug fixes 2. improved compile errors 3. implement the remaining runtime safety checks If you're looking forward to giving it a spin, subscribe to [this issue]( [LINK] ) to be notified when it is landed in master branch. Edit: The talk recording about upcoming compiler milestones is uploaded now [1] [1]: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["TlLIOgWYVpI",1],["3LVeEjsn8Ts",1]]},"Cn4vC80Pv6Q":{"wy":"HN points to it as a look at an early GUI that did a lot with little learning curve, often to contrast it with modern software.","q":{"x":"Seeing it in action gives you a much more complete picture of the system.","i":2447411,"y":"2011"},"m":[{"id":2447411,"author":"thought_alarm","month":"2011-04","story":"Pictures of the first GUIs from Xerox","stance":"rec","why":"Demo shows the Star system in action","quote":"Seeing it in action gives you a much more complete picture of the system.","text":"There are a few demos of the Xerox Star floating around on YouTube. Seeing it in action gives you a much more complete picture of the system. [VIDEO] The \"look\" was obviously hugely influential, the \"feel\" not so much. It is quite an odd beast, as the mouse is actually only used for selecting objects. The Lisa GUI prototypes are also and interesting bit of GUI trivia. It's easy to see the Star's influence on the final shipping version of the Lisa GUI compared to its prototypes. [LINK]"},{"id":42091236,"author":"aresant","month":"2024-11","story":"Apple Macintosh before System 7","stance":"reference","why":"Bonus Xerox Star UX demo alongside early Mac system versions","quote":"Bonus round - Xerox Star UX (1982) -","text":"FYI if interested in visuals for the earlier system OS System .85! - [LINK] (December 4, 1983) System 1 - [LINK] System 2 - [LINK] System 3 - [LINK] System 4 - [LINK] System 5 - [LINK] System 6 - [LINK] (April 1988) Bonus round - Xerox Star UX (1982) - [VIDEO]"},{"id":33076344,"author":"GeekyBear","month":"2022-10","story":"Xerox PARC – Office Alto Commercial (1979) [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Star was ahead of its time and expensive","quote":"They were too far ahead of their time.","text":"> How did IBM and Apple take over and Xerox end up as a has-been also ran? They were too far ahead of their time. The component costs needed to come down first. In 1981, the \"starter kit\" for their office system consisted of one Xerox Star for a user, a second Star to act as a file and print server, and a laser printer for about a quarter of a million dollars in today's money. Each additional networked Star that you added was about $50,000 in today's money. Even at those prices, the system wasn't exactly snappy. I do wonder what would have happened if they had kept iterating on the product until faster, cheaper components arrived. [VIDEO]"},{"id":21607062,"author":"gambler","month":"2019-11","story":"Designing Windows 95's User Interface (2018)","stance":"reference","why":"Windows as views onto system objects with unified interactions","quote":"the original windowed interface on Xerox machines was a view into underlying system objects","text":"Worth remembering: the original windowed interface on Xerox machines was a view into underlying system objects . It was designed around a unified vocabulary of interactions that allowed user to message those objects and also direct inter-object communication: [VIDEO] Xerox -> Apple -> Microsoft interface transfer preserved nothing of those core concepts. UI became a crutch developers grudgingly added to the system for \"those stupid users\". Thus, most software engineers today are still convinced that a teletype emulation is the best possible interface to the underlying OS that can possibly exist. Also, normal users are treated as second-class citizens in their own systems."},{"id":18176099,"author":"gambler","month":"2018-10","story":"12 Factor CLI Apps","stance":"reference","why":"Icons as live objects in the first modern UI","quote":"And this isn't the best UI, it's simply the first modern UI.","text":"Most people who claim that command line is the best tool for power users know nothing about history of GUIs. They think that laughable garbage that Windows calls user interface is \"how it's supposed to work\", and proceed to smugly lecture everyone on how command line is the only interface that can be composed and easily recorded. (The following paragraph is not directed at the author of the parent post. It's fully rhetorical.) Did you know that icons were supposed to be live representations of in-memory objects? That objects were more fundamental for the OS than files? Did you know that windows were views onto those objects? Did you know that interactions to and between icons were synonymous with OOP polymorphism? [VIDEO] And this isn't the best UI, it's simply the first modern UI."},{"id":16325891,"author":"romaniv","month":"2018-02","story":"Designing Windows 95’s User Interface","stance":"reference","why":"Xerox Star demo undermines claim of huge leap forward","quote":"After seeing some old demos of Xerox Star, I'd question that.","text":"> At the time, it was a huge leap forward in desktop computing. After seeing some old demos of Xerox Star, I'd question that. [VIDEO]"},{"id":15644735,"author":"romaniv","month":"2017-11","story":"Almost everything on computers is perceptually slower than it was in 1983","stance":"reference","why":"Star enabled complex tasks with almost no learning curve","quote":"Star allowed people to perform complex tasks with almost no learning curve.","text":"The thing that horrifies me about all of this is that instead of criticizing or fixing issues most people adapt what they do and even how they think to match all the deficiencies in software. Often, users simply can't imagine systems that work fundamentally differently and better. Often, when deficiencies are pointed out people start defending the mess and pointing out all the clever (half-assed, really) workaround and hacks they came up with. They are proud of all the time they spent (wasted) learning about obscure and counter-intuitive software functionality. They are proud that barely-working plugin/extensions/add-on they found and set up. All of that to do thing that should be trivial to begin with. One counter-point, though. > I make no secret of hating the mouse. If you look at the original uses of the mouse it was great. Especially in systems like Xerox Star. Star allowed people to perform complex tasks with almost no learning curve. [VIDEO] (Note how they weren't shy of using keyboard either. There are dedicated hardware buttons for standard commands like copy, find, repeat, \"properties\" and even for common text editing actions. Meanwhile, our keyboards don't have dedicated keys for undo, redo, cut, copy and paste - operations that are used in almost every application today.) Trouble is, we lost most of the driving ideas behind Xerox-style interfaces. Using a predefined set generic, powerful commands. Object-oriented UI. Uniformity of representations. Modern system ha"},{"id":13962395,"author":"gizmo","month":"2017-03","story":"How much your computer can do in a second","stance":"reference","why":"Contrasts Xerox 1982 interface with today's software","quote":"it's pretty clear to me we messed up big time somewhere along the road","text":"Way too much to cover in depth. In short, it's all terrible. We're mostly using tools from the 70s, and we're still to this day plagued by use-after-free and out-of-bounds errors, nullpointer exceptions, awful debuggers, race conditions, slow compilation times, bad primitives for multithreaded work, just to name a few. We've known for decades that we can do better and ways to do better have been studied in depth by academia. That's just the basics. Then there is futuristic things: Why can't we move running processes from one physical machine to another? Where are the debuggers that allow you to go back and forward in time? Why can't we visualize the data structures in our program and trivially change the data layout to see how that affects performance? When I look at the demo of the Xerox Sparc interface (1982) and compare that to where we are today it's pretty clear to me we messed up big time somewhere along the road: [VIDEO]"},{"id":13601283,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2017-02","story":"Grappling with Go","stance":"reference","why":"Star 8010 was programmed in Mesa, inspiring Modula-2","quote":"the Xerox Star 8010 system, which was programmed in Mesa, the language that inspired Niklaus Wirth to create Modula-2.","text":"Faire enough, so prepare for an history lesson. :) Mesa/Cedar was created at Xerox PARC, as an extension of the Xerox Star 8010 system, which was programmed in Mesa, the language that inspired Niklaus Wirth to create Modula-2. [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK] Mesa evolved to Mesa/Cedar, which allowed for automatic memory management via reference counting and a tracing collector for the cycles. [LINK] The environment had a REPL and debugger inspired by the Smalltalk and Lisp systems at Xerox. Think of Swift Playgrounds, kind of. This system inspired Niklaus Wirth to create Oberon, which you can read about in his book, about how the OS was built. [LINK] In 2013, Niklaus Wirth revised the language and updated the book for a FPGA based computer. [LINK] There were several generations of the Oberon OS, the last one before it stop being relevant, the Bluebottle OS coded in Active Oberon variant. [LINK] It had a strange mix of UI, using a mix of text and mouse input. Basically all applications were dynamic modules and the procedures/functions with a special signature could be used from the UI. So you could select text or objects from applications, and invoke such operations on the selection. The Oberon System 3 and Oberon V4 with their Gadgets framework were the best experience, before AOS was designed. Most relevant OS in Modula-3 was SPIN OS, [LINK] There were a few others, this is just a small sample."},{"id":7920840,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2014-06","story":"Web Starter Kit","stance":"reference","why":"One of several videos of historic interactive systems","quote":"","text":"Self [LINK] Xerox Star User Interface [VIDEO] [LINK] Tektronix 4404 Smalltalk [LINK] The Smalltalk-80 Programming System [LINK] The Final Demonstration of the Xerox 'Star' computer [LINK] Symbolics Hypertext Document [LINK] Warren Teitelman work at Xerox PARC [LINK] The Mesa/Cedar programming environment [LINK] Steve Jobs internal demo of NeXTSTEP 3, [LINK] Alan Kay on Xerox PARC work [LINK] [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":7565751,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2014-04","story":"Shadow DOM","stance":"reference","why":"One of several videos of historic interactive systems","quote":"","text":"Mainly videos Self [LINK] Xerox Star User Interface [VIDEO] [LINK] Tektronix 4404 Smalltalk [LINK] The Smalltalk-80 Programming System [LINK] The Final Demonstration of the Xerox 'Star' computer [LINK] Symbolics Hypertext Document [LINK]"},{"id":6011394,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2013-07","story":"Tangle: a JavaScript library for reactive documents","stance":"reference","why":"Xerox Star among presentations of early interactive environments","quote":"Sometimes I wish people would learn about computing history.","text":"Except the original Lisp and Smalltalk environments are much more than a simple REPL. Sometimes I wish people would learn about computing history. Presentation from Kalman Reti about Lisp Machines, check the interactivity starting at 00:44:00 and how to introduce mouse sensitive images at 01:00:00 [LINK] Xerox presentations about Smalltalk-80 [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":3240154,"author":"cooldeal","month":"2011-11","story":"Steve Jobs biography -- Isaacson blew it","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Not sure if I am replying to a troll(plus much of the above is copy/paste or very similar to an earlier comment from you). >they've just heard the lie that Apple stole the GUI from Xerox (impossible since there was no GUI at Xerox at the time, in fact) The Apple haters also seem to uploading fake videos of a old Xerox GUI on Youtube! /sarcasm [LINK] [VIDEO] > Both Apple and Google release the operating system as open source, and both Apple and Google keep as closed source the application layer where their proprietary apps live. For Apple, that's the UI, for google that's the Google Apps Sorry, Google Apps is not an application layer, however you wish it would be."},{"id":3262310,"author":"Pewpewarrows","month":"2011-11","story":"Peter Thiel: \"I Don’t Consider [The iPhone] To Be A Technological Breakthrough\"","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Yep, Xerox never invented the GUI. This entire product and all promotional videos and memories of it existing are a complete fabrication: [VIDEO] You've made similar posts in the past, and they've been similarly filled with rambling inaccuracies. I'll just leave it at that."}],"r":[["bdJKjBHCh18",1]]},"FyK4P7ZdOK8":{"wy":"Cited in discussions of cryptocurrency, KYC/AML and financial surveillance as a framing of money as a tool of control.","q":{"x":"Andreas Antonopoulos is a good person to listen to on this topic.","i":24538602,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":24538602,"author":"joshfraser","month":"2020-09","story":"FinCEN Files show trillions in tainted dollars flow freely through major banks","stance":"rec","why":"Explains money as a political tool of control and surveillance","quote":"Andreas Antonopoulos is a good person to listen to on this topic.","text":"All money is dirty. Most people don't question the provenance of their money, but every bill in your wallet contains trace amounts of cocaine. Every dollar you have has been involved in some sort of grey or black market transaction. Your money has also been used to fund an enormous amount of legally sanctioned atrocities around the world. You're not a bad person for accepting it. The money itself is amoral. When people talk about cracking down on \"tainted dollars\", they're really talking about power. In 1970, Richard Nixon signed the Bank Secrecy Act and turned money into a system of control, the system of control that attempts to use money as a political tool in order to control who is able to send and receive it, who they are able to send money to, and to have complete surveillance of all financial transactions. Your money isn't really yours if an authoritarian leader can dictate how you can spend it or seize it at any time. Andreas Antonopoulos is a good person to listen to on this topic. [VIDEO]"},{"id":15994802,"author":"tacon","month":"2017-12","story":"Why you can’t cash out pt 2: Bitcoin and KYC/AML","stance":"reference","why":"Quotes the talk's framing of money as a system of control","quote":"But what happens when a fourth use of money is introduced, one which subordinates all the other characteristics: money as a system of control?","text":"\"But what happens when a fourth use of money is introduced, one which subordinates all the other characteristics: money as a system of control?\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":15400872,"author":"AlexCoventry","month":"2017-10","story":"Ask HN: Resources to learn blockchain and smart contracts?","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Here's a somewhat banana-pants talk he recently gave.","text":"> is this heavy on marketing, cheerleading and kool-aid? I haven't looked at the book, but the author is, a bit. Here's a somewhat banana-pants talk he recently gave. [VIDEO]"}]},"G-lGCC4KKok":{"wy":"HN mostly cites it as evidence that YouTube was built on Python and as background on how it scaled.","q":{"x":"YouTube (owned by Google) is mainly Python.","i":4586919,"y":"2012"},"m":[{"id":4586919,"author":"gitarr","month":"2012-09","story":"We need more PyCon US 2013 submissions","stance":"rec","why":"PyCon 2012 talk on YouTube's Python architecture","quote":"YouTube (owned by Google) is mainly Python.","text":"YouTube (owned by Google) is mainly Python. Here a talk from Pycon 2012, the technical part starts around 10 min. in: [VIDEO]"},{"id":40411099,"author":"teitoklien","month":"2024-05","story":"Ask HN: Video streaming is expensive yet YouTube \"seems\" to do it for free. How?","stance":"reference","why":"Background on how YouTube scales video serving","quote":"","text":"They improve their stack on multiple fronts 1 - They use their own custom hardware encoders now optimised for their scale (previously they’d use a ton of intel cpu for encoding) This is often not possible for smaller startups to have their own custom hardware, but one could setup their own Xilinx FPGA based hardware accelerated encoding servers, Xillinx Alveo Boards are popular for media acceleration, they support both ffmpeg and gstreamer for acceleration needs. Nvidia Nvenc encoding is decent too but mostly for the most popular encoding types, and honestly an fpga based accelerator is probably better after cpu unless you have gpus lying around. 2 - Much lower bandwith costs, a lot of startups build their streaming applications on top of AWS, but that’s insanely expensive due to the insanely expensive price of AWS itself As a streaming service you could immediately cut down costs by shifting to ovhcloud, hetzner etc, they often have dedicated gameserver like offerings which are beneficial for streaming usecases too. That itself should allow you to bring down costs by 10x or even more (you have to choose the plan correctly on other tho, ovh, hetzner etc say you have unlimited bandwith on some packages but there is always a catch or they throttle you later, need to be careful about the offering you choose but otherwise a lot better) 3 - Having colocation hosts with your own servers setup and each video encoded at different sizes for different media devices also help only sendi"},{"id":39361706,"author":"Leftium","month":"2024-02","story":"Ask HN: Can I create a mobile and Web App using a Python/Python Framework?","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as evidence YouTube runs on Python","quote":"YouTube runs on Python","text":"For reference, YouTube runs on Python[1,2,3]: > 1. Python and Django: YouTube’s backend is predominantly written in Python, offering a balance of performance and readability. > 2. Google Cloud Platform... > 3. Java and C++: YouTube also utilizes Java and C++ for specific backend services, as they provide better performance for certain tasks. --- A long time ago, I looked into these Python frameworks: - [LINK] - [LINK] [1]: [LINK] [2]: [LINK] [3]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28498206,"author":"Jugurtha","month":"2021-09","story":"Ask HN: When to switch startup backend from Python to Golang?","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on how YouTube scaled its Python stack","quote":"","text":"Scalability at YouTube: [VIDEO] The Python is the less likely think that will suffocate you. What did you suffer from in past projects with each of these languages?"},{"id":5557700,"author":"tbatterii","month":"2013-04","story":"Security incident update","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for YouTube being built in Python","quote":"youtube was always done in python as far as I understand.","text":">Facebook and YouTube were both created later, and in PHP youtube was always done in python as far as I understand. [VIDEO] But yeah, back in 2002-3 coldfusion was an option."},{"id":3748825,"author":"gitarr","month":"2012-03","story":"Poll: What's Your Favorite Programming Language?","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as evidence YouTube relies on Python","quote":"services like Dropbox[1] or YouTube[2], both arguably very complex systems, are relying on Python as their language of choice.","text":"The real beauty of Python is the fact that it is easy for beginning programmers, yes, but it has also a lot to offer for experienced coders concerning all the advanced things they might need. It's no accident that services like Dropbox[1] or YouTube[2], both arguably very complex systems, are relying on Python as their language of choice. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"}]},"JaF-fq2Zn7I":{"wy":"HN mostly cites it in nuclear power debates as Gates making the case that nuclear is needed for clean energy, with a few critical references to his views on population.","q":{"x":"Here's Bill Gates explaining why nuclear is not optional","i":2401819,"y":"2011"},"m":[{"id":12641826,"author":"vayarajesh","month":"2016-10","story":"Ask HN: What's your favorite tech talk?","stance":"rec","why":"One of several favorite talks listed","quote":"TED talk - Bill gates (Innovation to Zero)","text":"TED talk - Elon musk - [LINK] D10 conference - Steve jobs and Bill gates - [LINK] TED talk - Bill gates (Innovation to Zero) - [VIDEO]"},{"id":31093489,"author":"carlhjerpe","month":"2022-04","story":"Utilities Want to Convert Coal Plants to Nuclear; Skeptics Abound","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Your comment loses it's value when you're trying to poke all the holes. > Storage is also plunging in cost, and additional technologies are already in commercial use (iron flow, for example.) > HVDC transmission is spreading, with higher capacity, lower cost, and higher efficiency for moving electricity greater distances (in other words, it's less of a deal if it's really windy in one spot and not in another, and ideally-suited wind sites can be further from where electricity is needed.) How do you transform DC up and down? By converting it to AC. Running it through a transformer and converting it back, HVDC isn't gonna take over the world, certain stretches might be HVDC where beneficial but not all over the world. The difference with nuclear is that anyone can build and operate next-gen storage, so you're commercial right out the door. I could hook my UPS up to the grid and feed back, commercial success! > Wind and solar are as distributable and scaleable as you want. Not without storage, which is harder to scale than production of other kinds. > You don't need to worry about geological stability with wind and solar. I don't see the USP, there are plenty of geologically stable areas in most countries. > Wind and solar don't require tens or hundreds of megawatts of cold-start / post-operation (cooling) power. They have the added downside of only producing power when the weather guy says so. > Wind/solar don't generate waste that is weaponizeable. Good point, for now. > You d"},{"id":2401819,"author":"Devilboy","month":"2011-04","story":"Nuclear Power is Safest Way to Make Electricity","stance":"reference","why":"Bill Gates argues nuclear is not optional for clean energy","quote":"Here's Bill Gates explaining why nuclear is not optional","text":"None of those can replace coal / nuclear. EDIT: Here's Bill Gates explaining why nuclear is not optional: [VIDEO]"},{"id":45873485,"author":"huijzer","month":"2025-11","story":"Building a 2.5kWh battery from disposable vapes to power my workshop [video]","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"he says we need to limit climate change by limiting population growth","text":"Sorry, I didn’t double check indeed. I posted the wrong TED talk from him, see [1] at 4:21. There he says we need to limit climate change by limiting population growth. I think this is quite a hypocritical stance from someone who flies private jet all year. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15019482,"author":"givan","month":"2017-08","story":"Gates Makes Largest Donation Since 2000 with $4.6B Pledge","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"his foundation uses the money for vaccines and health services which surprisingly has as target","text":"Instead of using the money to research renewable or more efficient energy production, reduce pollution etc his foundation uses the money for vaccines and health services which surprisingly has as target, as he says in a ted video (link below), to decrease human population which he sees it as the major problem of today. [VIDEO] \"The world today has about 6.8 billion people. That's headed up to about 9 billion. Now if we do a really great job on new vaccines, health care, reproductive health services, we could lower that by, perhaps, 10 or 15%\""}]},"RhsUHDJ0BFM":{"wy":"HN uses it as a quick, memorable illustration of how money laundering works in discussions of crypto seizures and mixers.","q":{"x":"Here is a short educational video on the topic of money laundering","i":42016701,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":42016701,"author":"Shatnerz","month":"2024-11","story":"Suits, money laundering, and linear programming","stance":"rec","why":"Short educational video on money laundering.","quote":"Here is a short educational video on the topic of money laundering","text":"> This is called \"layering\" I believe it is actually \"structuring\". Layering is a step in money laundering where layers of legitimacy are added as money moves around. Structuring is breaking larger transactions into smaller transactions in order to avoid detection. Smurfing is also similar to structuring and honestly I don't understand the difference well enough to explain, but they are often used interchangeably in my experience. edit: Here is a short educational video on the topic of money laundering: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35565447,"author":"nostrademons","month":"2023-04","story":"An Update on Tornado Cash","stance":"reference","why":"Used as example of protected speech describing laundering.","quote":"","text":"Poor analogy. Speech and ideas are constitutionally protected in the U.S; accommodations are not (well, other than quartering of soldiers). A better analogy is that the government can prosecute people for doing the actions described in the Breaking Bad scene on money laundering [1], but they cannot prosecute the writers of Breaking Bad, the networks it's shown on, YouTube, or people who link to it. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":31153814,"author":"objclxt","month":"2022-04","story":"The candy stores of Oxford Street","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates the laundering mechanism via a cash-heavy storefront.","quote":"","text":"> Why do you think it money laundering? What’s the exact mechanism? [VIDEO] Replace \"nail salon\" with \"candy\" and you're there. Candy is a great product for this because it's high-margin, and very low value."},{"id":30264102,"author":"openknot","month":"2022-02","story":"Feds arrest couple, seize $3.6B in hacked Bitcoin funds","stance":"reference","why":"Saul Goodman explains money laundering; used in lectures.","quote":"this scene where the character Saul Goodman explains money laundering in Breaking Bad","text":"That's right; your comment brings to mind this scene where the character Saul Goodman explains money laundering in Breaking Bad (this clip is supposedly shown as part of university lectures): [VIDEO]"}]},"ZZ3F3zWiEmc":{"wy":"Used to explain why contemporary art is expensive for money reasons rather than art reasons.","q":{"x":"Good time as ever to plug Wendover's recent video about how the art world works.","i":28889027,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":28889027,"author":"scrollaway","month":"2021-10","story":"Sotheby’s Metaverse curated NFT marketplace","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how the art world works and why prices are inflated.","quote":"Good time as ever to plug Wendover's recent video about how the art world works.","text":"Good time as ever to plug Wendover's recent video about how the art world works. [VIDEO] Seeing Sotheby's attach its name to NFTs confirms every suspicion i ever had about NFTs."},{"id":33058507,"author":"marginalia_nu","month":"2022-10","story":"How a Stable Diffusion prompt changes its output for the style of 1500 artists","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for claim that contemporary fine art is not about art.","quote":"It's not a particularly well hidden secret that the contemporary fine art is really not about the art or the artist.","text":"> The people doing contemporary fine art with their audience are unaffected. It's not a particularly well hidden secret that the contemporary fine art is really not about the art or the artist. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29566927,"author":"Balero","month":"2021-12","story":"Bitcoin could become ‘worthless’, Bank of England warns","stance":"reference","why":"Supports argument that art prices reflect money, not art.","quote":"Art is expensive for Money reasons, not art reasons.","text":"I mean you are right, but that texture is doing a lot of work, since the Mona Lisa is about $850 million, and my solution is $0.0001 million, and indistinguishable at 15 meters. But either way, this doesn't distract from the main thrust of my point. Art is expensive for Money reasons, not art reasons. [VIDEO] [LINK]"}],"r":[["9OfBpQgLXUc",1],["wOlcB-JxkFw",1],["ytpRnRke6I0",1],["i70wkxmumAw",1],["9zinZmE3Ogk",1],["7J52mDjZzto",1],["Pae2AMnmUVA",1],["rwhUpu9MfZ0",1],["7NfX0vlCa4k",1],["PQccNdwm8Tw",1],["kYHTzqHIngk",1],["90NsjKvz9Ns",1],["NVGuFdX5guE",1],["nSKp2StlS6s",1],["7Pq-S557XQU",1],["FjA0EQPeUGM",1]]},"jbp3kdJZ1_A":{"wy":"Cited as a rigorous radio-engineering analysis of what radar capability Starlink could plausibly have.","q":{"x":"Check out this video that goes into a very deep technical explanation","i":48117591,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":47989143,"author":"cmrx64","month":"2026-05","story":"America's Expanding Domestic Surveillance","stance":"rec","why":"Radio engineering breakdown of Starlink radar capabilities.","quote":"radio engineering breakdown of starlink radar capabilities","text":"and soon from space? radio engineering breakdown of starlink radar capabilities, it’s a pretty impressive bird if you were designing it only for that: [VIDEO]"},{"id":48117570,"author":"Enginerrrd","month":"2026-05","story":"Starship V3","stance":"rec","why":"Analysis of maximum plausible radar capability.","quote":"I found this video[1] to be a pretty convincing analysis of the absolute max capability you could expect","text":"Yeah, I agree. A massive radar network, passive or active is the most likely possibility I have come across. You'd need a LOT of compute at each node to get the most out of the network. I found this video[1] to be a pretty convincing analysis of the absolute max capability you could expect, and it would indeed be impressive. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":48117591,"author":"yellowbkpk","month":"2026-05","story":"Starship V3","stance":"rec","why":"Deep technical explanation of satellites as SAR.","quote":"Check out this video that goes into a very deep technical explanation","text":"Check out this video that goes into a very deep technical explanation about how the satellites can be used as a Synthetic Aperature Radar to build a realtime representation of the entire globe at meters of resolution: [VIDEO]"},{"id":49163823,"author":"defrost","month":"2026-08","story":"Battle of the Beams","stance":"reference","why":"Linked alongside another source on radar tech.","quote":"","text":"The US, UK, AU, and Russia have been back and forth on leadership relative to some metric or another wrt Over the Horizon systems throughout the post war period. > the world's finest radar and navigation tech Hadn't realised the UK had a sat nav constellation up (nav tech) and .. are we counting Starlink as radar tech? * [LINK] * [VIDEO]"},{"id":48869577,"author":"defrost","month":"2026-07","story":"SpaceX wants to launch 100k more Starlink satellites – for 100x the bandwidth","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for Starlink as radar with larger resolution increase.","quote":"","text":"Think of it less as a comms system with a small increase in bandwidth, and more as a radar system with a larger increase in resolution. * [VIDEO]"},{"id":48565556,"author":"dakolli","month":"2026-06","story":"SpaceX to buy Cursor AI coding agent operator Anysphere for $60B","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as support for Starlink being a military radar.","quote":"","text":"More like a giant military radar larping as an space based ISP. [VIDEO] Elon has always been a Department of Defense/intelligence asset larping as a capitalists. You can't exactly go to congress and get a quarter of the government budget to do globe spanning surveillance from space, or convince them to let you fund, build and maintain a platform for psyopping your own civilian population (x) So you go get your illegal projects funded through capital markets with through an asset like Elon. As soon as the department of defense started focusing on subterranean warfare and how its the future of wars Elon starts a tunneling company. Elon is just Howard Hughes v2, they're running the same script again."},{"id":48120262,"author":"msuniverse2026","month":"2026-05","story":"Starship V3","stance":"reference","why":"Timestamp cited to support the radar constellation claim.","quote":"See 1:38:00","text":"The data centres are for the secret radar constellation masquerading as an ISP company that is Starlink You are watching the slave grid being erected over your head [VIDEO] See 1:38:00"},{"id":48120580,"author":"mapt","month":"2026-05","story":"Starship V3","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for orbital SAR idea.","quote":"","text":"On the military side, \"Starlink V3 with data center\" looks a hell of a lot like an orbital Synthetic Aperture Radar asset, which requires both a big antenna and a good deal of onboard computation to mitigate bandwidth requirements. See [VIDEO] and specifically for the economics of AI vs surveillance - [LINK]"}]},"kdz6FeQLuHQ":{"wy":"Pointed to as a good rant on car-brained design and as evidence that country size is not what drives car dependence.","q":{"x":"Not Just Bikes did a great rant on the carbrain of The Bahamas","i":43029738,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":43029738,"author":"nayuki","month":"2025-02","story":"The Prophet of Parking: A eulogy for the great Donald Shoup","stance":"rec","why":"Rant on car-brained Bahamas traffic","quote":"Not Just Bikes did a great rant on the carbrain of The Bahamas","text":"> moved from the Caribbean, where public transportation was nonexistent, and traffic and parking were a constant nightmare Not Just Bikes did a great rant on the carbrain of The Bahamas: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36754878,"author":"TomK32","month":"2023-07","story":"Germany Achieves Record 57.7% Renewable Energy Share for First Half of 2023","stance":"reference","why":"Used to argue weather is not the barrier to cycling","quote":"","text":"Oh you picked the wrong guy for your comment: I worked 10km away the winter before and got out the door at 6am, cycled to work and left a 5pm to cycle back home, often when it was dark again. It's only a question of the right outfit, not the weather, otherwise everyone on the Bahamas would cycle [VIDEO]"},{"id":35519962,"author":"kiliantics","month":"2023-04","story":"Dashcam Footage Shows Driverless Cars Clogging San Francisco","stance":"reference","why":"Example of dedicated bus lanes improving transit in the Bahamas","quote":"explained in this video with the extreme example of the Bahamas","text":"According to the NYC MTA[1], there are close to 6k buses in the fleet with 1.2M riders per day. That's over 200 riders per bus per day. With each paying a $2.75 fare, you are making about enough to cover your estimated costs. I think the problem is not the cost of the bus but the organisation of the city's infrastructure. The bus could be a viable option for a lot more people -- far more than 200 per bus per day -- they just don't structure the roadways to maximise bus use but rather to minimise it. If there isn't a dedicated lane for buses (physically separated since NYPD cannot or will not enforce painted separation) then buses will go at least as slow as traffic, along with regular stopping, making it a less attractive option to driving or cabbing. It's pointless to operate the system this way and very cheap to fix it, where fixing it would make everyone's lives so much better as explained in this video with the extreme example of the Bahamas [2] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["cZYNADOHhVY",1],["1Jwo5qc78QU",1],["dnHdqPBrtH8",1],["r1ZLGqL1FMo",1],["7J52mDjZzto",1],["q_vivC7c_1k",1],["P7GKK3liv8M",1],["7zrx-b2sLUs",1],["MFEHFsO-XSI",1],["43XKfuptnik",1],["9W74aeuqsiU",1],["d4EgbgTm0Bg",1],["dqQw05Mr63E",1],["Y99Wj-NStok",1],["-opBifFfsMY",1],["AcS3NOQnsQM",1],["f7MzFfuNOtY",1]]},"sLFaa6RPJIU":{"wy":"HN points to it in superconductor and LK-99 threads as evidence that YBCO can be made in a modest home lab.","q":{"x":"Here's his video of making YBCO for anyone that hasn't seen it","i":36966276,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":36966276,"author":"eco","month":"2023-08","story":"Electronic Structure of LK-99","stance":"rec","why":"Ben Krasnow's video of making YBCO.","quote":"Here's his video of making YBCO for anyone that hasn't seen it","text":"I think he might be working on it. On Twitter he teased a homemade device for making very fine powder for, say, superconductors. Here's his video of making YBCO for anyone that hasn't seen it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36976524,"author":"uyhgr","month":"2023-08","story":"LK99: Team of Southeast University observed zero resistance below 110 K","stance":"reference","why":"Close-up of crystal imperfections enabling flux pinning.","quote":"where he shows a close up of the crystal’s cross section that shows the imperfections that allow the magnetic field through for the pinning effect","text":"The term is “flux pinning”, and it only applies to the “quantum lock” effect. That is the specifically static hovering effect. The diamagnetism, importantly this means repulsion of both poles simultaneously and equally (this is how you can have these magnets spin, a regular magnet repels same poles and attracts opposites, diamagnets repel both poles), is simply a characteristic of the superconductor, but it alone would just repel the object off. Here is a timestamped link to NileRed’s YBCO video that visually describes the flux pinning: [LINK] And here’s a timestamped link to Ben Krasnow’s Applied Science YBCO video where he shows a close up of the crystal’s cross section that shows the imperfections that allow the magnetic field through for the pinning effect: [VIDEO]"},{"id":33901528,"author":"gus_massa","month":"2022-12","story":"Publicity Stunt Fallout","stance":"reference","why":"Shows high temperature superconductor made in a good personal lab.","quote":"The process is not so complicated, and can be done at home.","text":"Most of it, but there are some corners. For example, high temperature superconductivity was discovered in 1986 [1]. The process is not so complicated, and can be done at home. Well, at least in a very good personal lab, for example see this video by Applied Science. [VIDEO] I think the original team made like 100 samples using different metals and different oxygen proportion. Discovering the first one requires some brute force that needs a small team, and is too much for a single person. Anyway, it doesn't look too far away from something a single person can discover. [1] That's 36 year ago. I'm feeling old..."},{"id":26549167,"author":"gus_massa","month":"2021-03","story":"Cold Fusion or Low-Energy Nuclear Reactions, U.S. Navy Researchers Reopen Case","stance":"reference","why":"Shows YBCO can be made with a modest lab.","quote":"now you can also see the recipe in a video in YouTube by \"Applied Science","text":"The problem of Cold Fusion is the lack of a reliable experiment. Compare this with high temperature superconductivity. If you get a time machine and go to 1985 and try to promote high temperature superconductivity, nobody will take you seriously. But a team discover one material and published a clear recipe to built it. Other team tried some variations, and discovered an easier recipe to build them and published it. So soon everybody and their dog was making new variants, and new variants, and new variants... A few years ago, they gave one in the physics' lab for undergraduate students, and we made a few measurements for a month carefully because they don't tolerate humidity. Until someone put it in a glass with water and ice to measure the properties at 0°C. Also, now you can also see the recipe in a video in YouTube by \"Applied Science\": [VIDEO] , the guy has some advanced lab, but nothing too advanced. The problem of Cold Fusion is the lack of a reliable experiment."}],"r":[["bbyr7jZOllI",1],["fCbAafKLqC8",1]]},"sQ56ve39l2I":{"wy":"Cited as evidence against the assumption that thin modern phones are fragile.","q":{"x":"take a look at the latest Jerry Rig Everything video where it exceeded his typical scratch test resits and he was unable to bend it with his hands.","i":45316809,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":45316809,"author":"thepryz","month":"2025-09","story":"Vapor chamber tech keeps iPhone 17 Pro cool","stance":"rec","why":"Shows the iPhone Air exceeding scratch tests and resisting bending.","quote":"take a look at the latest Jerry Rig Everything video where it exceeded his typical scratch test resits and he was unable to bend it with his hands.","text":"I think you'd be surprised by how durable the newer phones are, especially the iPhone Air. It's hard to completely protect a glass screen from shattering since its a crystalline material encased in a metal enclosure that is more likely to dent than flex, absorb the impact and return to it's shape, but they keep getting better and I think most people could go without a case if they really wanted to. If you're curious about just how durable the iPhone Air is, take a look at the latest Jerry Rig Everything video where it exceeded his typical scratch test resits and he was unable to bend it with his hands. [VIDEO]"},{"id":46419253,"author":"tstrimple","month":"2025-12","story":"China Is Banning Tesla-Style Retractable Door Handles over Safety Concerns","stance":"reference","why":"Evidence rebutting claim that modern phones are fragile.","quote":"If you think it's fragile, you're completely ignorant to the current state of phone durability.","text":"If you think it's fragile, you're completely ignorant to the current state of phone durability. Literally you have zero idea what you're talking about and just commenting out of your ass based on pre-conceived bias. [VIDEO] Quite frankly you are part of the reason that the modern internet is garbage. You present your \"alternative-facts\" as if they are truth despite having zero bearing on the truth. You're just a fucking disinformation bot like the MAGA crowd with just as much supporting evidence. You should be ashamed. But you're a green account created to respond so you don't have to show your true feelings on your real account. So you have no shame. You can't even commit to your own opinions on your own account. Despicable."},{"id":45586468,"author":"kristianp","month":"2025-10","story":"Google Pixel 10 Pro Fold explodes during JerryRigEverything's durability test","stance":"reference","why":"Shows the iPhone Air could not be broken by hand.","quote":"The Iphone air couldn't be broken by hand either","text":"The Samsung Z Fold 7 fares better. He's able to bend it back, but unable to cause it to crack by bending it backwards. [LINK] Not sure if I see the point of using a craft knife to scratch the case though. Nor the point of using a lighter to kill pixels on the screen. More for the sheer horror of it than anything. The Iphone air couldn't be broken by hand either: [VIDEO]"}]},"x1H-323d838":{"wy":"Linked as the go-to hands-on demonstration of how transparent wood is made and why scaling it to large pieces is hard.","q":{"x":"NileRed on youtube has a cool video detailing the steps to create transparent wood.","i":24667180,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":24667180,"author":"valine","month":"2020-10","story":"Transparent wood could be the window of the future","stance":"rec","why":"Detailed walkthrough of steps to make transparent wood.","quote":"NileRed on youtube has a cool video detailing the steps to create transparent wood.","text":"NileRed on youtube has a cool video detailing the steps to create transparent wood. His result isn’t quite as clear as in the article but the process seems similar. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28983888,"author":"robbedpeter","month":"2021-10","story":"Scientists have found a way to harden wood to make a knife that rivals steel","stance":"reference","why":"Covers the lignin removal chemistry for treating wood.","quote":"The NileRed YouTube channel goes into the lignin removal chemistry here","text":"This looks like it can be accomplished with minimal basic chemistry and a hydraulic press. I'd love to see what type of acoustic guitars could be created using this material. Super thin tops and super rigid bodies are exciting. The NileRed YouTube channel goes into the lignin removal chemistry here [VIDEO] There's also great progress in using nanocrystalline cellulose to produce super strong composite materials. Embedding graphene or carbon nanotubes gives you super light, super tough material that is suitable for replacing aluminum and steel structures."},{"id":26052412,"author":"jrockway","month":"2021-02","story":"Solar-assisted fabrication of large-scale, patternable transparent wood","stance":"reference","why":"Shows making transparent wood by soaking in epoxy under vacuum.","quote":"NileRed did a video about preparing this from a different paper","text":"> Not sure how they \"infuse\" it. That sounds like driving it deeper. NileRed did a video about preparing this from a different paper: [VIDEO] It seems that you just soak the thing in epoxy in a vacuum. The results were not great, though."},{"id":26052964,"author":"adrianN","month":"2021-02","story":"Solar-assisted fabrication of large-scale, patternable transparent wood","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"As you can see from the video it is basically impossible to scale this process to large pieces of wood.","text":"Nile Red has a video where he makes this: [VIDEO] As you can see from the video it is basically impossible to scale this process to large pieces of wood. Discussions about replacing windows with this are pretty pointless I think."},{"id":25481393,"author":"grenoire","month":"2020-12","story":"Super Wood (2018)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Process-wise I think this is an industrial version of what NileRed [0] and prior AvE [1] tried.","text":"Process-wise I think this is an industrial version of what NileRed [0] and prior AvE [1] tried. Pretty cool, if it's truly a durable alternative. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":24681379,"author":"riffraff","month":"2020-10","story":"A clear, strong, and thermally insulated wood for energy efficient windows","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"there's a few videos from years ago attempting earlier versions of this[0].","text":"there's a few videos from years ago attempting earlier versions of this[0]. In the end, it is very little \"transparent wood\" and more \"plastic with wood bits in it\". [0] e.g. [VIDEO]"}]},"zU8alMWUmDI":{"wy":"HN mentions it in threads on the OP-1 and gear acquisition syndrome, as a look at the OP-1's feature set and a cheaper alternative.","q":{"x":"You may enjoy this video about the OP-1's feature set","i":35907650,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":35907650,"author":"mandmandam","month":"2023-05","story":"TP-7","stance":"rec","why":"Video about the OP-1's feature set and its limits.","quote":"You may enjoy this video about the OP-1's feature set","text":"They are ridiculously overpriced. They are terribly limited, which is marketed as a bonus - I don't see it that way. I wouldn't go so far as to call them trash - they're very expensive toys. You may enjoy this video about the OP-1's feature set, even without the background knowledge to know everything he's saying is 100% true (which it is) - [VIDEO] I like Apple, quite possibly too much. I put a lot of value on user experience. But I would never buy a Teenage Engineering product with my own money. I find that 'I only drink $500 bottles of wine' kind of thing deeply uncool."},{"id":41183533,"author":"woolion","month":"2024-08","story":"Gear Acquisition Syndrome","stance":"reference","why":"Introduced commenter to GAS therapy amid OP-1 pricing debate.","quote":"I just discovered GAS therapy [0]","text":"I just discovered GAS therapy [0], as there was some controversy about the OP-1 pricing (also from Teenage Engineering). To summarize both sides, software is pretty cheap and limitless. Device are limited which can spark creativity by constraints, their tactile form factor feel great, and their portable nature can be a great advantage. However, there are other arguments that typically revolve around how \"software cannot replicate vintage sounds\" where the holy wars really begin. Nobody has ever proven that, but the myth endures. Recently enough [1] showed all that basically with an eq, a compressor and a distortion you can reproduce any vintage amp sound, you just need to know how it deviates from a flat sound response. How expensive the vintage or high-end hardware is might be a deterrent for some but a very attractive proposition for others. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":32519312,"author":"diimdeep","month":"2022-08","story":"Ask HN: What are you Watching? (Educational)","stance":"reference","why":"Listed among educational videos being watched.","quote":"","text":"- Using the C++ Standard Library for Real-time Audio - Timur Doumler - ADC21 - [LINK] - How to replicate Teenage Engineering OP-1 and OP-1 Field for free. | GAS Therapy #35 - [VIDEO]"},{"id":36268805,"author":"diimdeep","month":"2023-06","story":"Teenage Engineering OP-1: How a Micro Synth Made a Massive Impact in Music Tech","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Impact on pocket cash for sure.","text":"Impact on pocket cash for sure. But seriously: How to replicate Teenage Engineering OP-1 and OP-1 Field for free. | GAS Therapy #35 [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["tybOi4hjZFQ",1],["ne6tB2KiZuk",1]]},"5J3H8--CQRE":{"wy":"HN uses it almost entirely as evidence in criticism of Tesla's marketing claims, repeating that Tesla never ran the quarter mile it advertised.","q":{"x":"Analysis by Engineering Explained","i":40396202,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":40396202,"author":"peutetre","month":"2024-05","story":"Rematch: Tesla Cybertruck vs. Porsche 911 Drag Race (This Time It's Not Rigged)","stance":"reference","why":"Engineering Explained's analysis of the Cybertruck vs Porsche race.","quote":"Analysis by Engineering Explained","text":"Jointly done by MotorTrend, Engineering Explained, and Out of Spec Motoring. Analysis by Engineering Explained: [VIDEO] Out of Spec Motoring's video: [LINK]"},{"id":47895975,"author":"breve","month":"2026-04","story":"Tesla (TSLA) discloses $2B AI hardware company acquisition buried","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Tesla never ran that quarter mile","text":"Why do you want the articles to be \"positive\" or \"negative\"? Why do you not want them to simply be the fact of the matter? If you want more good news about Tesla then perhaps Tesla should be better run. Perhaps Tesla should abandon their policy of constantly lying. Tesla's been lying continuously about full self-driving for a decade. Tesla lies about dumb things there's no need to lie about like how fast the Cybertruck is: [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO] Tesla never ran that quarter mile, a lie which the lead Cybertruck engineer pathetically tried to defend. When even your engineers can't achieve basic honesty then you've got a sick company culture: [LINK] What \"positive\" spin do you want to see on these lies?"},{"id":47096059,"author":"breve","month":"2026-02","story":"Tesla to pay $243M judgement over Autopilot crash","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"That's not evidence, that's advertising.","text":"Dude. That's not evidence, that's advertising. You're taking at face value a claim made by a company that has lied continuously for over a decade about the self-driving capabilities of their cars: [LINK] [LINK] Remember how there were supposed to be 1 million robotaxis on the road by 2020: [LINK] Lying is part of the company culture at Tesla. They even lie about dumb things there's simply no need to lie about, but they go ahead and lie anyway: [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO] It's not rational to swallow Tesla's lies hook, line, and sinker. If you can't produce independent verification of Tesla's claims then there's nothing to talk about."},{"id":46497698,"author":"breve","month":"2026-01","story":"Tesla's 4680 battery supply chain collapses as partner writes down deal by 99%","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Here are some bad arguments and false claims from Tesla about how fast the Cybertruck is","text":"> I strongly believe that criticising bad arguments and correcting false claims is especially important You're against bad arguments and false claims? Cool! Here are some bad arguments and false claims from Tesla about how fast the Cybertruck is: [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO] I look forward to your criticisms of Tesla's bad arguments and corrections of Tesla's false claims. But maybe we should just call them what they are: lies."},{"id":45334553,"author":"breve","month":"2025-09","story":"Tesla influencers tried coast-to-coast self-driving, hit debris before 60 miles","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"They never even ran that quarter mile.","text":"Tesla doesn't cultivate an engineering culture. Tesla encourages a culture of lying. Some engineers have become so corrupted by it that they're willing to lie about things that there's no need to lie about, like quarter mile times of the Cybertruck: [LINK] [VIDEO] The lead engineer on the Cybertruck sadly tried to defend the lie: [LINK] They never even ran that quarter mile."},{"id":45057971,"author":"breve","month":"2025-08","story":"Neuralink 'Participant 1' says his life has changed","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"like quarter mile times of the Cybertruck","text":"The work of many talented engineers at Tesla is dismissed by Musk: [LINK] Some engineers have become so corrupted by the culture of lying of Tesla that they're willing to lie about things that there's no need to lie about, like quarter mile times of the Cybertruck: [LINK] [VIDEO] The lead engineer on the Cybertruck sadly tried to defend the lie, while admitting that they never even ran that quarter mile: [LINK]"},{"id":43316122,"author":"zfg","month":"2025-03","story":"Tesla created secret team to suppress driving range complaints (2023)","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Tesla never ran the quarter mile that they claimed to.","text":"> How can Tesla advertise a “more accurate” number if they are required by regulation to use EPA estimate? By also providing the worst case scenario numbers in addition to the EPA numbers. Tesla could simply do a highway range test at 70mph, ideally in Winter: [LINK] They could also show towing range: [LINK] There's nothing stopping Tesla showing these things. The one time Tesla did a towing demonstration those numbers turned out to be lies. Tesla never ran the quarter mile that they claimed to. When even your engineers lack basic honesty you've got a sick company culture: [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":43520270,"author":"zfg","month":"2025-03","story":"My Day Inside America's Most Hated Car","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"There's no value in believing Tesla's lies","text":"> It can apparently outrace a Porsche 911, while simultaneously towing a Porsche 911. There's no value in believing Tesla's lies: [LINK] [VIDEO] It's such a dumb thing to lie about but lying is just part of the company culture at Tesla. Here's the lead engineer of the Cybertruck trying to rationalize the lies: [LINK] When not even your engineers possess basic honesty then you've got a sick company culture."},{"id":42902603,"author":"zfg","month":"2025-02","story":"NTSB forces reporters to get plane crash updates on X","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Tesla never even ran that quarter mile.","text":"Musk has no commitment to the truth or even reality. He lies constantly. He lies about things there's simply no need to lie about. He can't help himself. He's spent 10 years lying about Tesla's full self-driving: [LINK] [LINK] He lies about being good at video games: [LINK] He lies about Cybertruck quarter mile times: [LINK] [VIDEO] Tesla never even ran that quarter mile. When your engineers don't have commitment to basic honesty then you've got a rotten company. And the fish rots from the head: [LINK] He enjoys and promotes conspiracy theories: [LINK] He dabbles in antisemitism: [LINK] He stokes partisan division over the Los Angeles fires: [LINK] And so on and so on. X is an endless swamp of falsehoods with Musk himself spreading as much of it as he can. Without facts America will have no past and no future. America will be stuck in an eternal present, just like Russia. This is Musk's attempt at a Russification of America. America's in trouble."},{"id":41807525,"author":"peutetre","month":"2024-10","story":"Tesla Robotaxi","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Tesla never ran that quarter mile.","text":"The problem is Tesla and Musk have been lying about full self-driving for years: [LINK] In 2016 Tesla said that \"as of today, all Tesla vehicles produced in our factory – including Model 3 – will have the hardware needed for full self-driving capability at a safety level substantially greater than that of a human driver.\" That was a lie: [LINK] Tesla even lies about things as dumb as Cybertruck quarter mile times: [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK] Tesla never ran that quarter mile. And the worst thing about it was the Cybertruck's lead engineer trying to rationalize the lie: [LINK] When your engineers lack commitment to basic honesty then you've got a sick company culture. Lying is just too much a part of the culture at Tesla. Musk clearly doesn't value honesty or credibility."},{"id":41892651,"author":"peutetre","month":"2024-10","story":"Optimus Navigating Around [video]","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Tesla lied about the Cybertruck towing a Porsche over a quarter mile.","text":"Lying is part of the company culture at Tesla. For example, Tesla lied about the Cybertruck towing a Porsche over a quarter mile. It's a dumb thing to lie about, there was absolutely no need to lie about it, and yet Tesla went ahead and lied: [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO] Tesla never ran that quarter mile. And the worst thing about it was the Cybertruck's lead engineer trying to rationalize the lie: [LINK] When your engineers lack commitment to basic honesty then you've got a sick company culture. You can't take anything Musk or Tesla says at face value."},{"id":40676184,"author":"peutetre","month":"2024-06","story":"Tesla shareholders re-approve Elon Musk's 2018 pay package","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Maybe it's because Tesla lies about things as dumb as quarter mile times.","text":"Or maybe it's because Musk runs around calling people pedophiles: [LINK] Maybe it's because his behavior is increasingly erratic and he doesn't seem to care about Tesla anymore: [LINK] Maybe it's because Tesla employees describe him as a pigeon CEO because \"he comes in, shits all over us, and then leaves.\": [LINK] Maybe it's because he advocates for the submission of Taiwan to China: [LINK] And for the submission of Ukraine to Russia: [LINK] Maybe it's because he says \"China rocks\" while the US is full of \"complacency and entitlement\": [LINK] Maybe it's because he pledged his commitment to China's core socialist values: [LINK] Maybe it's because he called California's covid measures \"fascist\": [LINK] But praised China because \"Shanghai's COVID-19 measures helped lay the foundation for the city's future development\": [LINK] Maybe it's because in 2016 Tesla claimed \"that, as of today, all Tesla vehicles produced in our factory – including Model 3 – will have the hardware needed for full self-driving capability at a safety level substantially greater than that of a human driver\" . That was a lie: [LINK] Maybe it's because Tesla lies about things as dumb as quarter mile times. There's simply no need to lie about it, but lying is just too much a part of the company culture: [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK] And so on and so forth. There's a lot not to like."},{"id":40834535,"author":"peutetre","month":"2024-06","story":"EV charging is so bleak in US that 46% consider going back to gas-powered cars","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Tesla never even ran the quarter mile.","text":"The plug is just a plug. The more important part is the charging protocol and standard. That's CCS going forward. CCS now supports three plug types: Type 1 combo, Type 2 combo, and J3400. Older Teslas can't charge on these chargers because they don't have CCS support. Those cars will need to get a CCS retrofit. In general you shouldn't take anything Tesla says at face value. Lying is just too much a part of the company culture at Tesla. Tesla lies about things as dumb as quarter mile stunts. There was absolutely no need to lie about it, yet they just couldn't help themselves and they went ahead and lied about it anyway: [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK] Tesla never even ran the quarter mile. And the worst thing about it is the Cybertruck's lead engineer trying to rationalize the lie: [LINK] When your engineers lack commitment to basic honesty then you've got a sick company culture."},{"id":40472069,"author":"peutetre","month":"2024-05","story":"Tesla's once-stellar reputation is running out of juice","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Tesla went ahead and lied about it","text":"> You also have to remember that Tesla doesn't advertise. It's funny how persistent this myth is. Tesla has always advertised. Here's an ad from almost 5 years ago. Tesla's YouTube channel is nothing but ads: [LINK] Tesla ads to benefit Twitter: [LINK] [LINK] Tesla's advertising to try to convince shareholders to approve Musk's pay package: [LINK] It's a shame that Tesla has a tendency to lie in its ads, especially about things as trivial as quarter mile times where there's simply no need to lie. But Tesla went ahead and lied about it: [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":40540638,"author":"peutetre","month":"2024-05","story":"Lucid Boss Says Tesla Has Lost Its \"Sense of Purpose\"","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"The Cybertruck didn't outperform a Porsche 911 while towing a 911. Tesla lied to you.","text":"The Cybertruck didn't outperform a Porsche 911 while towing a 911. Tesla lied to you. Elon lied to you: [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK] You have swallowed Tesla's advertising hook, line, and sinker. There's no point in repeating Tesla's lies."}]},"9LLZBVTid4I":{"wy":"Cited in energy-crisis threads as evidence that Germany's dependence on Russian energy was flagged years before 2022.","q":{"x":"They've been warned about this danger for years.","i":32663892,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":37599536,"author":"hn_throwaway_99","month":"2023-09","story":"Germany went from envy of the world to worst-performing major developed economy","stance":"reference","why":"Trump's remarks on German reliance on Russian energy proved correct","quote":"Cheap Russian energy was the drug that Germany got hooked on.","text":"I'm definitely no fan of Trump, but if you separate the messenger from the message here, he's actually totally correct here: [VIDEO] Cheap Russian energy was the drug that Germany got hooked on. Every viewer of after-school specials knows you shouldn't get hooked on the drug dealer's freebies."},{"id":32663892,"author":"TrispusAttucks","month":"2022-08","story":"How Bad Europe’s Energy Crisis Is","stance":"reference","why":"Germany was warned in 2018 about Russian energy dependence","quote":"They've been warned about this danger for years.","text":"Yep. They've been warned about this danger for years. Germany 'Totally Controlled By Russia' (2018) [VIDEO]"},{"id":32109689,"author":"ibeckermayer","month":"2022-07","story":"Looming natural gas shortages has the EU scrambling for solutions","stance":"reference","why":"Trump's 2018 warning on German dependence on Russian energy","quote":"German bureaucrats laugh at Trump after he says the country could become totally dependent on Russian energy","text":"Related content \"Trump: Germany 'Totally Controlled By Russia'\" [VIDEO] German bureaucrats laugh at Trump after he says the country could become totally dependent on Russian energy: [LINK]"}]},"IVpOyKCNZYw":{"wy":"HN cites it as shorthand for the long-running frustration of the Linux community with Nvidia's drivers, often as a joke or background.","q":{"x":"See things like this infamous clip from Torvalds[0] for more context on the community sentiment around nvidia in general.","i":31345592,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":31345592,"author":"lucideer","month":"2022-05","story":"Nvidia releases open-source GPU kernel modules","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"See things like this infamous clip from Torvalds[0] for more context on the community sentiment around nvidia in general.","text":"This is a good technical summary of the impact but I think misses the somewhat emotive history here. See things like this infamous clip from Torvalds[0] for more context on the community sentiment around nvidia in general. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":24457621,"author":"syshum","month":"2020-09","story":"SoftBank set to sell UK’s Arm Holdings to Nvidia for $40B","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Nvidia has been the single worst company we have ever delt with so Nvidia F_ck You!\" -- Linus Torvalds","text":"You are not wrong \"Nvidia has been the single worst company we have ever delt with so Nvidia F_ck You!\" -- Linus Torvalds [1] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":14728593,"author":"erikb","month":"2017-07","story":"What happened to Steam Machines?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"For quite some time it was graphics drivers support","text":"What's really stopping us here? For quite some time it was graphics drivers support: [VIDEO] Is this still the case? I hear from many people that at least on Ubuntu that's no problem anymore. Are the graphics cards still overheating? Any experience in that area? And yes, Valve could've done things better, but it's not that bad, that you couldn't do anything with at. As said, at least on Ubuntu with Steam gaming should be fine. And if we got one thing from Steam it's that more and more games are deployed for Linux as well. Really, why hasn't it lifted off now? Is it really that users are so stupid that they don't see a $0 offer is better than a $100 offer for an OS? Hard to believe."},{"id":13144860,"author":"bostik","month":"2016-12","story":"AMD responds to Linux kernel maintainer's rejection of AMDGPU patch","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Linus gave a VERY public, VERY brutal rant on NVIDIA and their drivers four years ago.[0]","text":"You're asking a question with a very complex answer. I'll start with a short answer - AMD wants to have at minimum a reasonably good baseline functionality on linux, out of the box. If that answer doesn't make much sense, please read on for the complex bits. To provide some historical context, Linus gave a VERY public, VERY brutal rant on NVIDIA and their drivers four years ago.[0] At the time, NVIDIA's closed drivers were an opaque blob which basically reimplemented the entirety of OpenGL. These collided with everything else. The open drivers (nouveau) were slow and far behind in features. The situation was bad enough that you couldn't necessarily even install a linux distro on a system with NVIDIA chip because the open drivers wouldn't work well enough for X and/or desktop environment to initialise properly, let alone remain up and functional. In effect, if you wanted to run linux, you were best off without NVIDIA. Fast forward year and a half. NVIDIA had come out and committed to improving the linux driver situation. They still couldn't open source their current-generation drivers, but they had realised the horrible reputation of their hardware on linux was going to be a persistent PR nightmare, and thus an existential threat to their growing mobile division. A space where they were up against Imagination Technologies. (Don't get me started on ImgTec, please...) NVIDIA needed to get their hardware and software processes aligned in a way that they would work reliably out of"},{"id":12885708,"author":"squarefoot","month":"2016-11","story":"Nvidia  adds telemetry to latest drivers","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Some background.","text":"Some background. [VIDEO] Shouldn't be a problem anymore under Linux as most distros today install Nouveau drivers by default. [LINK]"},{"id":6497726,"author":"kleiba","month":"2013-10","story":"Nvidia Removed Linux Driver Feature Due to Windows","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"NVidia has a history of not playing nice with Linux.","text":"NVidia has a history of not playing nice with Linux. Allow me to point to a small comment Linus Torvalds made on Nvidia a while ago (NSFW): [VIDEO]"},{"id":19950784,"author":"klingonopera","month":"2019-05","story":"AMD Immune to MDS Vulnerabilities","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"That's the reason there's an infamous photo of Linus showing the finger to nVidia","text":"Yeah, if you even need dedicated GPUs at all, as usually it's Gamers who are looking for those and they're usually on Windows systems anyways (really looking forward to installing Windows 7 on the laptop I mentioned if I do get it this year - kinda mean that both ironic and not-ironic...). The built-in Radeon Vega 8 or 10 solutions on all mobile Ryzens are usually better and more efficient than current Intel counterparts, so if you don't need a dGPU, then that's one more reason to root for Team Green. EDIT: In case anyone reading isn't aware, nVidia is a biaaatch when it comes to drivers for Linux. Anyone looking for powerful GPUs in Linux machines, no matter desktop or laptop, has an easier life with AMD solutions. That's the reason there's an infamous photo of Linus showing the finger to nVidia: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18836783,"author":"apatters","month":"2019-01","story":"Chromium blacklists Nouveau","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"FWIW there's Linus' famous Nvidia F-bomb :)","text":"YMMV I suppose. Some people seem to have good experiences with their driver but mine have been terrible. I've used Ubuntu as my daily driver for 7 years now and Nvidia's driver has been the #1 source of crashes and configuration problems (half of the time I can't even get 3d accel working with it turned on, spend hours trying different versions that are reported on some forum to have fixed some problem but they don't, can't figure out why things aren't working, upon upgrading from 16.04 to 18.04 it borked the whole system and I had to switch to Nouveau, this list of Nvidia driver pain goes on forever, this is across multiple installs of Ubuntu). All my experience has been on laptops and there are lots of reports of Optimus related problems on Linux so there's that. I gave up on the hope of having Optimus actually work years ago. I would like to just be able to enable the latest version of Nvidia's driver and see my laptop start using the discrete gpu. That would be a big step forward from the past 7 years. FWIW there's Linus' famous Nvidia F-bomb :) \"Nvidia has been the single worst company we ever dealt with\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":6050959,"author":"D9u","month":"2013-07","story":"Linus on \"acting professionally\"","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"All I can think of is when Linus said told Nvidia \"F... you!\" and flipped the bird.","text":"All I can think of is when Linus said told Nvidia \"F... you!\" and flipped the bird. [VIDEO] This isn't just an isolated incident either, but I'm no shining example of acting professionally."}]},"NCmTJkZy0rM":{"wy":"HN cites it as proof of how much food a very small urban plot can produce.","q":{"x":"You can grow a surprising amount on even 0.1 acres.","i":22712721,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":24704768,"author":"kyuudou","month":"2020-10","story":"Why I am building permapeople.org","stance":"reference","why":"Example of high yield from tiny plot.","quote":"","text":"To add to this, folks like this family claim (or were when that video[1] was done) to harvest 6,000 lbs of food a year from a 1/10th of an acre and still have enough left over to sell to local chefs to the tune of about $20,000. The more I read about this the more I started doubting all the doom about sustainability. Granted, I think they were vegetarian (still had live birds for eggs) but.. I think the combination of pooling together ancient techniques with modern technology could provide better results than the \"if GMO & Roundup are eliminated the WORLD WILL STARVE\" type of propaganda that, the more I read up about things like Agent Orange and, well, propaganda, the more I want to hear from people like the Dervais kids (RIP Jules) and less from people who have never farmed before or maybe grew some herbs one year. 1: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22765451,"author":"kyuudou","month":"2020-04","story":"People are planting more vegetable gardens","stance":"reference","why":"Family of three producing 6000 pounds of food yearly.","quote":"This family of 3 produces 6000 pounds of food a year on 1/10 of an acre","text":"This family of 3 produces 6000 pounds of food a year on 1/10 of an acre that is organic, non-GMO and vegetarian: [VIDEO] They have enough surplus to sell to local restaurants to the tune of about 20,000$/year to supplement their income. They use a lot of techniques handed down from antiquity, apparently. Shh, don't tell the eugenicists masquerading as environmentalists and conservationists!"},{"id":22712721,"author":"broahmed","month":"2020-03","story":"From Spain to Germany, farmers warn of fresh food shortages","stance":"reference","why":"Family grows 6000 pounds on about 4000 square feet.","quote":"You can grow a surprising amount on even 0.1 acres.","text":"You can grow a surprising amount on even 0.1 acres. Here's a video where a family grows 6000 pounds on about 4000 square feet. [VIDEO] Granted, they have the whole family pitching in with the labor and it's a full-time job for them."},{"id":9266402,"author":"msandford","month":"2015-03","story":"Destroy the soil and we all starve","stance":"reference","why":"Family grows huge amount of food on a tenth acre.","quote":"Here are people in LA that grow a huge amount of food in their backyard, 1/10 of an acre","text":"Agreed, but industrial agribusiness is predicated on huge automation and the idea that one farmer can farm hundred or thousands of acres. That's an efficient use of labor, but not of acres. We might be getting to the point that land is more valuable than labor and thus it makes sense to spend more human effort per acre than in the past. Here are people in LA that grow a huge amount of food in their backyard, 1/10 of an acre: [VIDEO] Here's a guy in Canada that makes a legit living urban farming 1/3 of an acre: [LINK] There are tons of additional examples on youtube of other people doing very similar things. The point, though, is that it's very intensive on a labor per acre basis. You can't do this kind of farming with big tractors over thousands of acres with just a few people, you need several people per acre for it to work. But it does work! Yes there is a limit eventually, but the point isn't as low as you think."}]},"RAGcDi0DRtU":{"wy":"Mostly posted as a joke or amusing aside in threads about English, language learning, and swearing.","m":[{"id":21146702,"author":"michael_j_ward","month":"2019-10","story":"Is “Ass” the Most Complicated Word in English?","stance":"reference","why":"Full Conan clip of the Ass routine.","quote":"For those that want a link to the whole thing","text":"The article contains clips of the \"Ass\" version. For those that want a link to the whole thing: [VIDEO]"},{"id":41707503,"author":"nvader","month":"2024-10","story":"Comedy Theory (2022)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I love his with on pointing out the incongruities in surface level features of English.","text":"For the curious, I suspect this was Ismo. I love his with on pointing out the incongruities in surface level features of English. [VIDEO]"},{"id":31349772,"author":"PrimeDirective","month":"2022-05","story":"Some notes on 'asshat' (2018)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"There's a Finnish stand-up comedian who also pondered on the many possibilities of the English word \"ass\".","text":"There's a Finnish stand-up comedian who also pondered on the many possibilities of the English word \"ass\". This is him on Conan's talk show: [VIDEO] Funny enough, he mentions the same things the article does, especially the end of it."},{"id":27220785,"author":"biztos","month":"2021-05","story":"Nonnative English speakers share their gripes about speaking English","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Thanks to the magic of the innernets I have recently discovered a Finnish comedian named Ismo who talks about this","text":"I'm a native English speaker who's lived in other countries a lot and speaks three languages, now trying to learn a fourth. The weirdness of \"English\" is something I think about often. For starters, you have to live in it before you can really speak it. And our spelling is the least rational, least consistent thing I've ever seen. In addition to the weird way we spell a lot of things, many of the sounds are not pronounced at all, or pronounced differently, in different countries. And there are dialects that are pretty much incomprehensible to people who grew up in the mainstream dialect (true of many other languages as well) but then we add weird things like Americans hearing race in accent and dialect, while the British can hear class but for the most part the opposite is not true. I think simple English is one of the easiest languages to learn, because of its ubiquity and in most places the material advantage of speaking some; while good English is one of the hardest. Thanks to the magic of the innernets I have recently discovered a Finnish comedian named Ismo who talks about this: [VIDEO] (Arguably NSFW if you work in a kindergarten.)"},{"id":27230308,"author":"irjustin","month":"2021-05","story":"Native speakers are hard to understand in a lingua franca situation","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Ismo, the comedian sums it up in great style[0] [1].","text":"Ismo, the comedian sums it up in great style[0] [1]. \"I've got shit to do\" means anything but actually going to the bathroom. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":23230446,"author":"kaitai","month":"2020-05","story":"Uber cuts 3000 more jobs, closes 45 offices","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Makes me think of Ismo and the word \"ass\"","text":"Speaking of dissonant, as a woman it's always a bit difficult to figure out when \"man\" means \"human\" and when \"man\" means \"not you, lady\". \"Men's room\" = \"not you, lady\"; \"man-years\" = \"of course I'm talking about you because I'm certainly not sexist; man means human, of course, except when it explicitly doesn't, like when you didn't have the right to vote because the right to vote was for all men who are created equal, haha\". Makes me think of Ismo and the word \"ass\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":21825780,"author":"ubermonkey","month":"2019-12","story":"The hardest aspect of learning English as a second language","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"my favorite comments about English as a second language come from Finnish comedian Ismo","text":"Lately, my favorite comments about English as a second language come from Finnish comedian Ismo, regarding his take on the most complicated word in the English language: Ass. [VIDEO] (Reasonably SFW; the set is from a normal TV talk show.)"}]},"SIVKmwzWSuc":{"wy":"HN links it as a concrete example of the scale and speed of automated recycling sorting.","q":{"x":"Mixed recycling goes in, and aluminum, ferrous metal, glass cullet sorted by color, paper, and trash come out.","i":19196940,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":19196940,"author":"Animats","month":"2019-02","story":"National Sword","stance":"reference","why":"Shows how a materials recycling facility sorts mixed recycling","quote":"Mixed recycling goes in, and aluminum, ferrous metal, glass cullet sorted by color, paper, and trash come out.","text":"Separating the stuff after it gets dumped in a truck in one big mix?? ABSURD. Nope. Routine.[1] There are shakers, screens, magnets, blowers, crushers, and optical sorters. It's not that complicated. Mixed recycling goes in, and aluminum, ferrous metal, glass cullet sorted by color, paper, and trash come out. Separation is working fine. The problem is that the markets for waste paper, glass cullet, and plastic are weak, even when fully sorted. Waste paper, especially. Paper mills in North America have been closing for years. Plastic bottle recycling is coming along well. CarbonLite has a huge plant near LA, and plants in Denver and Pennsylvania. Recycled plastic bottles go in, and plastic pellets ready for injection molding into new bottles come out. This is the area where things are getting better. Glass cullet is in trouble. Fewer manufacturers are using glass containers and there's a cullet glut. All this is at best marginally profitable and tends to need subsidies. Aluminum is the only real moneymaker. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":17842829,"author":"Animats","month":"2018-08","story":"Recycling in the United States is in serious trouble. How does it work?","stance":"reference","why":"Typical modern UK mechanized recycling plant","quote":"Here's a typical modern plant in the UK.","text":"Automated recycling is coming along well. Here's a typical modern plant in the UK.[1] Sorting is mechanized, with tumble drums, vibratory sorters, and magnets doing most of the work. Permanent magnets pull ferrous metal out. AC magnets pull aluminum out. Vision systems, often multispectral, look at the presorted materials as they go by on a fast belt and use air jets to kick out certain types of items. This is standard technology. There's still some manual picking involved in most plants. Robots are taking that over at a few plants.[2] Aluminum is the moneymaker. Plastic bottles can go to a plant that turns them back into pellets for making new bottles.[3] A huge plant now serves southern California. Not clear this makes money. Waste paper isn't that valuable. \"Peak paper\" was a while back, and US paper mills have been closing for years. It's not that sorting is hard, it's that the product is so low-value. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":15860160,"author":"visarga","month":"2017-12","story":"Drowning in Garbage","stance":"reference","why":"Example of automated trash sorting","quote":"This is an example of automated trash sorting (optical sorting) from 4 years ago.","text":"AI can help us here. This is an example of automated trash sorting (optical sorting) from 4 years ago. I expect today we can sort trash even more efficienty: [VIDEO] Another idea would be an army of cleaning bots that can be let loose in a forest, on a beach or on roadside to methodically collect all trash. Even underwater drones could be used to clean up beaches. I think cleaning bots is the way forward."},{"id":11781151,"author":"jarvist","month":"2016-05","story":"A Rock-Sorting Robot","stance":"reference","why":"Scale and speed of recycling plant sorting","quote":"An info vid on a recycling plant, showing the sort of scale and speed of sorting:-","text":"I believe WEEE (Waste Electrical and Electronic Equipment) recycling plants use computer vision directly. Recycling plans having multiple optical units with compressed air to then redirect the route of identified items, but I don't know whether this is image recognition or Raman / IR to identify the material. Certainly to differentiate plastics, it will be Raman / IR. An info vid on a recycling plant, showing the sort of scale and speed of sorting:- [VIDEO]"}]},"USKD3vPD6ZA":{"wy":"Mostly invoked as a joke in threads about monkeys or darts beating professional stock pickers, and about loose definitions of AI.","m":[{"id":35000858,"author":"SuoDuanDao","month":"2023-03","story":"The Inverse Jim Cramer ETF Has Officially Arrived","stance":"reference","why":"Humorous video of a fish picking stocks, tracking WSB sentiment","quote":"I came across a humorous video of someone getting his fish to invest in stocks","text":"I came across a humorous video of someone getting his fish to invest in stocks [VIDEO] He was essentially teaching algo trading, and also tracked wallstreet bets sentiment analysis. He went so far as to pitch his investment strategy to serious tech investors (it was ahead of the nasdaq at the time). His Wallstreet bets tracker had been tanking so he focused on the fish investing fund, but I was waiting for one of the investors to suggest he inverse wallstreet bets and make a lot of money."},{"id":46154932,"author":"Bender","month":"2025-12","story":"We gave 5 LLMs $100K to trade stocks for 8 months","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Spoiler, the fish did great vs wall street bets .","text":"This experiment was also performed with a fish [1] though it was only given $50,000. Spoiler, the fish did great vs wall street bets . [1] - [VIDEO] [video][15 mins]"},{"id":41091969,"author":"Modified3019","month":"2024-07","story":"Fish in remote tank made into fractals with Video Feedback Device [video]","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Fish were an excellent choice. They are very versatile","text":"Fish were an excellent choice. They are very versatile: [VIDEO] (“I Gave My Goldfish $50,000 to Trade Stocks”)"},{"id":38374610,"author":"dilippkumar","month":"2023-11","story":"FTC authorizes compulsory process for AI-related products and services","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"This camera tracking a goldfish to pick stocks is AI by this definition","text":"It doesn’t even have to be software. This camera tracking a goldfish to pick stocks is AI by this definition: [VIDEO] Update: A coin toss meets this definition."},{"id":31676287,"author":"LinuxBender","month":"2022-06","story":"A Hacker News comment about entrepreneurship, darts, and wealth goes viral","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"The fish did pretty good in my opinion.","text":"Is this in line with the concept of pitting r/wallstreetbets vs a fish? [1] The fish did pretty good in my opinion. [1] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":31259523,"author":"LinuxBender","month":"2022-05","story":"Buffett would bet on monkeys throwing darts to outperform financial advisers","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"A similar technique was implemented and produced results using a Goldfish.","text":"A similar technique was implemented and produced results using a Goldfish. [1] [1] - [VIDEO] [video] [warning:language]"}]},"cIQ0yIZgQeE":{"wy":"Cited in threads about physical controls and how diesel-electric locomotives actually work.","m":[{"id":44376198,"author":"sandworm101","month":"2025-06","story":"Battery-electric \"Infinity Train\" will charge itself using gravity","stance":"reference","why":"How diesel locomotives work","quote":"virtually all diesel locamotives already employ regenerative braking.","text":"Fyi, virtually all diesel locamotives already employ regenerative braking. They just send the electricity to resistors, turning it into heat. The amount of energy is simply staggering. No battery tech can absorb the power fast enough. Its funny that this train is in australia. Only on a very flat run will braking be so slow that power can be stored efficiently. [VIDEO]"},{"id":34290758,"author":"rawgabbit","month":"2023-01","story":"Study Finds That Buttons in Cars Are Safer and Quicker to Use Than Touchscreens","stance":"reference","why":"Shows locomotive cab controls as an ideal physical UI","quote":"A diesel locomotive has my ideal user interface[1].","text":"A diesel locomotive has my ideal user interface[1]. (a) For non critical operations, it has smart displays similar to an airplane cockpits. Physical function keys perform different things depending on the screen the smart display is presenting. (b) For critical operations, physical switches and levers are clearly labelled to show what they do,. I would say the best UI is one where you can talk someone through an emergency over the phone. e.g., \"Mayday. Help. The locomotive engineer just passed out and I don't know how to stop this thing.\" \"Are you sitting on the right seat? The operator's seat? Look to your left, you will see two blue levers marked throttle and dynamic braking etc.\" [1] [VIDEO] (skip to the 20 minute mark)"},{"id":37594645,"author":"devoutsalsa","month":"2023-09","story":"Tesla buys German railway line, now hosting passenger trains","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"At least the diesel engine powers the electric motors at the wheels!","text":"At least the diesel engine powers the electric motors at the wheels! How a Diesel-Electric Locomotive Works -- [VIDEO]"}]},"cXhZvyGtMrk":{"wy":"HN cites it as a striking demonstration that fluorescent tubes glow under power lines without wiring.","q":{"x":"You can light up fluorescent tubes by placing them on the ground under high voltage power lines","i":28344746,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":28344746,"author":"fy20","month":"2021-08","story":"Police find electronic signal-jamming device inside California home","stance":"reference","why":"Fluorescent tubes light up under high voltage lines.","quote":"You can light up fluorescent tubes by placing them on the ground under high voltage power lines","text":"I heard of a similar story when taking a tour of a local transmition station as a kid. You can light up fluorescent tubes by placing them on the ground under high voltage power lines, so I'd assume something like that is how this would work: [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":11082321,"author":"DanBC","month":"2016-02","story":"What 50,000 watts of RF energy sounds like through a jumper cable","stance":"reference","why":"Fluorescent tubes glowing under power lines.","quote":"If I lived close enough I'd be trying to work out some kind of trickle charger for phones.","text":"There are a few \"art installations\" where people put fluorescent tubes under power lines. [VIDEO] [LINK] If I lived close enough I'd be trying to work out some kind of trickle charger for phones."},{"id":3996125,"author":"Retric","month":"2012-05","story":"Nikola Tesla Wasn't God And Thomas Edison Wasn't The Devil","stance":"reference","why":"Fluorescent bulbs lit under power lines; people walking disrupt them.","quote":"you can see people walking around the field prevents the bulbs from lighting up.","text":"Tesla did not invent Unlimted wireless electricity. He got low efficiency wireless power transfer working, but never worked out that inducing a current acts as a drain on the source aka you can use a high tension power line to light a few light bulbs just fine but the power company can measure the drain on the line. IMO, his problem was he was getting such low efficiency transfers that the drain was not obvious. PS: If you still think his idea of charging the ionosphere could work, watch this video. [VIDEO] But, you can see people walking around the field prevents the bulbs from lighting up. Just, ignore the audio on this as they don't know what their talking about."}]},"dEeyaAUCyZs":{"wy":"Quoted whenever someone needs Jobs's own words that Apple sees itself as a software company.","q":{"x":"The money is in the hardware but the value is in the software.","i":27221988,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":45593730,"author":"ViktorRay","month":"2025-10","story":"Apple M5 chip","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs says Apple is a software company","quote":"Steve Jobs himself said that Apple sees itself as a software company","text":"Steve Jobs himself said that Apple sees itself as a software company [VIDEO] The above link is a video where he mentions that. It is true that Apple’s major software products like iOS and MacOS are only available on Apple’s own hardware. But the Steve Jobs justification for this (which he said in a different interview I can’t find right now so I will paraphrase) is that he felt Apple made the best hardware and software in the world so he wanted Apple’s customers to experience the best software on the best hardware possible which he felt only Apple could provide. (I wish I could find the exact quote.) Anyway according to Steve Jobs Apple is a software first company."},{"id":45595692,"author":"alt227","month":"2025-10","story":"Apple M5 chip","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs on software driving user experience","quote":"Steve Jobs consistently made the point that Apples hardware is the same as everyone elses","text":"You know you might just have a point if you werent completely making that all up. Steve Jobs consistently made the point that Apples hardware is the same as everyone elses, what makes them different is they make the best software which enables the best user experience. Here see this quote from Steve Jobs which shows that his attitude is the complete opposite of what you wrote. [VIDEO]"},{"id":45595904,"author":"alt227","month":"2025-10","story":"Apple M5 chip","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs on hardware and software","quote":"Watch this and maybe you might change your mind","text":"> The hardware doesn't exist merely to run the software Watch this and maybe you might change your mind: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37470014,"author":"ksec","month":"2023-09","story":"As Smartphone Industry Sputters, the iPhone Expands Its Dominance","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs on Apple as software company","quote":"Apple is an Software company, you just have to buy Apple's hardware to use it.","text":">> There’s a general rule about consumer electronics: The older a device becomes, the more competitors appear and prices fall. This was true for televisions, personal computers and portable music players. >>It was supposed to happen with smartphones. But the iPhone has defied gravity. Because it is not the right comparison. Not even for portable music players which was mostly dominated by iPod. Quoting Steve Jobs [1]. > Apple view itself as a software company I remember, possibly in the same conference Steve basically said Apple is an Software company, you just have to buy Apple's hardware to use it. In that sense. iPhone is not consumer electronics. It is not hardware only. That is why you see all the other examples are race to the bottom for Consumer Electronics. If you look at Software, like Windows, It has been in the dominating position for the past 3 decade. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":28911153,"author":"mcovalt","month":"2021-10","story":"MacBook Pro 14-inch and MacBook Pro 16-inch","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs interview on Apple as software company","quote":"Apple views itself as a software company.","text":"> Apple has always been a hardware company though. Software has just been for the purpose of selling their hardware. I'm not sure that's the right perspective. Steve Jobs said in an interview [0] that \"Apple views itself as a software company.\" [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":27221988,"author":"headmelted","month":"2021-05","story":"The level of Mac malware is not acceptable, says Apple exec at Epic trial","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs on hardware versus software value","quote":"The money is in the hardware but the value is in the software.","text":"I think what Tim Sweeney has noticed is what Steve Jobs realised (by his own admission) far too late. The money is in the hardware but the value is in the software. People will buy hardware thinking of that as their investment, without realising that the software is what extracts almost all of the revenue from them as a customer regardless. Steve Jobs talking about this in 2007: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22570541,"author":"ksec","month":"2020-03","story":"WWDC to Be Moved Online","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs says Apple is a software company","quote":"Apple is a software company, you just have to buy its hardware to use it.","text":">Remember they are a hardware company not software, they use the software ecosystem to force you to buy their hardware This is what a lot of people thought and it is actually the other way round, and as Steve Jobs put it himself [1], Apple is a software company, you just have to buy its hardware to use it. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":16461029,"author":"staunch","month":"2018-02","story":"Samsung Galaxy S9 and S9+","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs on iPod, Mac and iPhone being software","quote":"Because an iPod's really just software.","text":"\"If you look at the reason that the iPod exists, it's because these really great Japanese consumer electronics companies who kind of own the portable music market, invented it and owned it, couldn't do the appropriate software, couldn't conceive of and implement the appropriate software. Because an iPod's really just software. It's software in the iPod itself, it's software on the PC or the Mac, and it's software in the cloud for the store. And it's in a beautiful box, but it's software. If you look at what a Mac is, it's OS X, right? It's in a beautiful box, but it's OS X. And if you look at what an iPhone will hopefully be, it's software.\" - Steve Jobs (2007) [VIDEO]"},{"id":15622353,"author":"DRW_","month":"2017-11","story":"iPhone X Teardown","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs credits software for Apple success","quote":"Steve Jobs emphasised a few times that the reason why Apple was successful is because of the software.","text":"Actually, Steve Jobs emphasised a few times that the reason why Apple was successful is because of the software. [VIDEO]"},{"id":14244038,"author":"ksec","month":"2017-05","story":"Modern “Hackintoshes” show that Apple should probably just build a Mac tower","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs says Apple is a software company","quote":"Apple is a Software company, You just have to buy their Hardware first to use it.","text":"Steve Jobs - Apple Is Software - YouTube [VIDEO] Apple is a Software company, You just have to buy their Hardware first to use it. Apple lately has been too focused, or too worried about the Design of the product and lost sight of its main direction. To more accurately phase it, Apple is an Ecosystem company. You use its Software, Hardware and Services across all ( most ) segment. I'd argue Mac Pro should be the one of those segment, ( representing 1% of the total Mac Sales, and more like 0.1% of Apple's total revenue ) where they should make it impossible for average consumer to come up with a similar spec Machine with similar price. It doesn't mean Apple is making a loss, it is more likely Apple is just making very little profits on it. The Apple now, or the Mac Ecosystem now is very different to 20 years ago. iPhone manage to pull in Lots of new developers and Pros."},{"id":11036233,"author":"jad","month":"2016-02","story":"Apple’s declining software quality","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I think there are a few concrete things going on: * There are many more integration points between their products now. Shipping only the Mac or only the Mac and an iPod or even a first gen iPhone that can only get data into system apps via a USB cable is very simple compared to what they're making today. For Apple's best-cast customer, who owns a Mac, an iPhone, an iPad, an Apple TV, an Apple Watch, and who uses iCloud, how many integration points are involved now? Integration points are like the exponent on software complexity. It's where software goes to die. * They are still essentially a fat client company that's trying to build more cloud-oriented applications. This leads to additional complexity in the product that other companies just don't have to deal with. An obvious example that jumps to mind is iTunes vs. Spotify. If iTunes was just Apple's version of Spotify, how much better would it be? * Brain drain. Apple's stock made a lot of people a lot of money, and if you work there, you can't participate in the mobile revolution they started. Steve Jobs's passing could also be a natural book end for people in their careers to try something new, or find a job where they're not working 80 hours regularly, or to just take some time off. I guess the last one isn't really \"concrete\", and is more just me speculating, but I threw it out there because of a decent amount of anecdotal evidence I've seen. Here are some other things that are also just speculative but interesting to "}]},"k238XpMMn38":{"wy":"HN cites it as an example of messy code behind successful games and of the human stories that code comments can hold.","m":[{"id":47594743,"author":"u8","month":"2026-03","story":"Ask HN: Are you too getting addicted to the dev workflow of coding with agents?","stance":"reference","why":"Example of human stories in code comments.","quote":"","text":"I've been coding by hand for nearly 20 years. I don't get any rush from generating code with agents. Most of the time I just feel loss. Coding to me has always been the work of a craftsman. I spent years learning syntax, studying APIs, and generally becoming an expert in a language I could then use to exactly express myself. My code became my way of of telling a story. I picked that language not only because it was the right tool for the job, but because I liked the community, I liked the direction of the tool, and I liked writing it. Agents obliterate all of that. Before, source code was like exploring a canyon. Each function, loop, and nested brackets was someone's choice. There was an implicit story to follow. Agentic code is that valley after the glaciers have retreated. A good illustration of what I'm afraid of losing is in those videos cataloging the [the rapidly dwindling sanity of valve programmers as expressed through code comments]( [VIDEO] ). When AI generates your code, you lose that context. You lose that human connection to code that I care so much about. Unfortunately, I don't think the human touch was as high-of-value to other developers as I previously thought. I've realized that for most developers, it was never about the journey, it was about getting to the destination as quickly as possible. We were infected with a plague of idea-guys and didn't even know it. That's okay. There are a billion things in the world where what was once the sole dominion of craf"},{"id":40172457,"author":"bfors","month":"2024-04","story":"Leaving Rust gamedev after 3 years","stance":"reference","why":"Example of fun games built on messy code.","quote":"","text":"Like the author states, the \"written in Rust\" bonus many projects get does not apply to games. Most of the games I consider to be the most fun are built on an absolute rats nest dumpster fire of code (see: [VIDEO] ). Sometimes bugs even expand game mechanics and make them more fun and expressive. That being said I'll definitely check out Unrelaxing Quacks, it looks great."},{"id":26798735,"author":"oeiiooeieo","month":"2021-04","story":"‘Counter Strike’ Bug Allows Hackers to Take over a PC with a Steam Invite","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Valve's development practices are apparently good for a chuckle.","text":"Valve's development practices are apparently good for a chuckle. [VIDEO] The devs don't seem like they have enough time to deal with the code they have to write, let alone respond to security issues. (not their fault, of course)"}]},"mArrNRWQEso":{"wy":"Cited for specific Larry Page statements, such as on sharing medical data for research and personalized search being a hard problem.","q":{"x":"even Larry Page acknowledged that like 10 years ago[1]","i":41351828,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":41351828,"author":"mrkramer","month":"2024-08","story":"Why don't we have personalized search engines?","stance":"reference","why":"Larry Page acknowledging personalized search is a hard problem.","quote":"even Larry Page acknowledged that like 10 years ago[1]","text":"It's actually quite hard problem to solve; even Larry Page acknowledged that like 10 years ago[1] and nobody is even close to solving it, not even Google. My opinion is that LLMs are good step towards answer machine type of search engine. Perhaps the ultimate search engine would be something like Elon Musk's Neuralink[2] brain-computer interface where chip implant could read your thoughts and know your feelings and based on that give you \"perfect\" results. That would be really personal, I mean like on another level of directly personal. Now all we have is indirect personalisation where search engine gathers everything it can about you and assumes what you would want to see. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":16061925,"author":"rkindi","month":"2018-01","story":"Show HN: VidDistill – Automated YouTube Video Summarization Using Captions","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"That's a really good point. I'll update the site to have a default video in the text field so that you can have a quick and easy way to test the site without thinking about a video you want to try. In the meantime, I've compiled some URLs that you can paste into the text field! Where's Google going next? | Larry Page [VIDEO] The surprising habits of original thinkers | Adam Grant [LINK] The Art of Letting Go | The Minimalists | TEDxFargo [LINK] Thanks for the feedback!"},{"id":8083855,"author":"timothya","month":"2014-07","story":"Google to Collect Data to Define Healthy Human","stance":"reference","why":"Larry Page on sharing medical data for research versus privacy.","quote":"This reminds me of something Larry Page said at TED this year","text":"This reminds me of something Larry Page said at TED this year: I say, wouldn't it be amazing if everyone's medical records were available anonymously to research doctors? And when someone accesses your medical record, a research doctor, they could see, you could see which doctor accessed it and why, and you could maybe learn about what conditions you have. I think if we just did that, we'd save 100,000 lives this year. So I guess I'm just very worried that with Internet privacy, we're doing the same thing we're doing with medical records, is we're throwing out the baby with the bathwater, and we're not really thinking about the tremendous good that can come from people sharing information with the right people in the right ways. Source: [VIDEO] at 14:00."}]},"tQIdxbWhHSM":{"wy":"Cited whenever Nvidia's Linux driver behavior comes up, as the famous example of the kernel community's frustration.","m":[{"id":43207439,"author":"ho_schi","month":"2025-02","story":"AMD RDNA 4 – AMD Radeon RX 9000 Series Graphics Cards","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as Torvalds' view on Nvidia.","quote":"And, Linus Torvalds[4] said everything.","text":"Buy AMD or Intel. Not Nvidia. Rationale: Both AMD and Intel provide well maintained open-source drivers for many years[1]. Nvidia doesn't. You need to use closed-source drivers with Nvidia in practice, which causes issues with kernel upgrades, various issues (for which you don't get help, because the kernel is now marked as tainted) and problems with Wayland. The later are caused by the fact, that Nvidia refused[2][3] to support Wayland for a long time. Blaming that implicit sync was a disadvantage for Nvidia is not appropriate. They could've participated a long time ago and either adapt to implicit sync or help adding explicit sync years ago. Furthermore: * AMD gave us Vulkan as standard, which builds upon AMD's Mantle. * AMD gave us FreeSync i.e. VRR, which is usable by others. * AMD gave us FSR, which is usable by others. What did Nvidia? * High prices. * Proprietary and expensive GSYNC. * Refused to publish documentation and open-source drivers for many years. * Own video-decoding libraries. It is the documentation, drivers and Wayland. But the complete track record of Nvidia is bad. Their only argument are some benchmarks wins and press-coverage. The most important feature to all users is, reliability. And, Linus Torvalds[4] said everything. If Nvidia has changed its company politics and proofs that over years through good work it is maybe possible the reevaluate this. I don't see this within the next years. [1] Well. Decades? [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [VIDEO]"},{"id":38052412,"author":"candiodari","month":"2023-10","story":"Six Great Features with the Upcoming Linux 6.6 Kernel","stance":"reference","why":"Famous example of Nvidia hate.","quote":"And there is much hating on Nvidia, for using a \"workaround\". Including, famously","text":"That's 100% intentional. In any case, non-GPL modules cannot be upstreamed (except with a license that is compatible with the GPL, e.g. BSD is okay), and cannot touch a lot of parts of the kernel. Also, loading a non-GPL module \"taints\" your kernel (this cannot be undone except through reboot) and tells everyone on the support mailinglists that you've loaded proprietary code. [LINK] And there is much hating on Nvidia, for using a \"workaround\". Including, famously: [VIDEO] Also famously, this is the reason ZFS is not in the linux kernel."},{"id":37321021,"author":"gameoverhumans","month":"2023-08","story":"Linux 6.6 to Protect Against Illicit Behavior of Nvidia Proprietary Driver","stance":"reference","why":"Linus giving Nvidia the finger.","quote":"If Linus is happy to literally give NVIDIA the finger [1]","text":"If Linus is happy to literally give NVIDIA the finger [1], then maybe he/Linux Foundation would be willing to give NVIDIA the legal spanking they deserve (IMO)? We can only dream :) [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":42299795,"author":"gondo","month":"2024-12","story":"Intel announces retirement of Pat Gelsinger","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Linus seems to disagree","text":"> Nvidia and their amazing software stack and support for their hardware. Linus seems to disagree [VIDEO]"}]},"zILwgQhjC_Q":{"wy":"HN links it as a hands-on demonstration that oscillator frequency depends on orientation relative to gravity.","q":{"x":"Demonstration","i":38338690,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":38338690,"author":"formerly_proven","month":"2023-11","story":"Why the Position of a Watch Influences Accuracy (2020)","stance":"reference","why":"Demonstration of crystal acceleration sensitivity","quote":"Demonstration","text":"> Typical crystal acceleration sensitivities will range from about 1 x 10-10 per g for specially constructed precision SC-cut and AT-cut resonators to the order of 10-7 per g for tuning fork resonators. [LINK] Demonstration: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19268423,"author":"gugagore","month":"2019-02","story":"TI Claims Breakthrough in BAW On-Die-Oscillators","stance":"reference","why":"Oscillator gravity sensitivity","quote":"","text":"Maybe if you can show me that if you flick an RC oscillator you can detect it, then I'll believe it's a silly distinction. Or if RC oscillators can detect gravity: [VIDEO] I'm also curious what it would mean to you for two things to be doing \"fundamentally different\" things. Do you believe in the difference between a mechanical oscillator and an electrical oscillator? Mechanical oscillators like a spring/mass exchange energy between kinetic and electrical energy (a moving mass and a strained material). An RC oscillator exchanges energy between elements that store energy in an electric field and a magnetic field. That feels fundamentally different even in a four-fundamental-forces kind of manner."},{"id":19015561,"author":"SamReidHughes","month":"2019-01","story":"Masahiro Kikuno, Japanese Independent Watchmaker (2017)","stance":"reference","why":"Related to orientation effects on quartz","quote":"Related EEVBlog link","text":"The Zenith Defy Lab watch has accuracy of -0.5/+0.5 seconds per day, which matches and/or beats quartz watches (that aren't thermocompensated). Rolexes, which have a classical movement design, are pretty close, promising -2/+2 spd. Quartz watches' accuracy also varies depending on orientation, for what it's worth. (It has an effect on the order of 1-2 seconds per year.) Related EEVBlog link: [VIDEO]"},{"id":9600602,"author":"comboy","month":"2015-05","story":"1 second per second is harder than it sounds (2014)","stance":"reference","why":"Frequency depends on orientation","quote":"freqency depends also on gravitation","text":"My bad, I was before my coffee. One additional thing that wasn't yet mentioned is that freqency depends also on gravitation, so orientation of your motherboard could matter (for the curious - [VIDEO] ). I'm not really sure it's big enough to matter though."},{"id":8554223,"author":"snogglethorpe","month":"2014-11","story":"A clock that can detect tiny shifts in the flow of time itself","stance":"reference","why":"Frequency counter detecting gravity effects","quote":"Also, gravity","text":"> Yeah, they're not sensitive to magnetic fields. But they do drift with temperature Also, gravity: [VIDEO]"}]},"zN_Zl64OQEw":{"wy":"Cited as a vivid example of bird strikes and ejection, in threads on drones, aircraft collisions and ejections.","q":{"x":"It's a video from a forward facing camera in an F-16 as it gets hit by a bird, which shuts down the engine.","i":9011118,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":37560856,"author":"chinathrow","month":"2023-09","story":"US Military Asks for Help Locating Missing F-35 Fighter Jet","stance":"reference","why":"Example of dual ejection.","quote":"Sample dual ejection","text":"Depending on the situation, there might be not enough room or time to save lives in ground. Sample dual ejection: [VIDEO]"},{"id":9911416,"author":"tzs","month":"2015-07","story":"Drones visit California wildfire, angering firefighters","stance":"reference","why":"Cockpit view of a Hawk ingesting a bird and crashing.","quote":"Here's a nice cockpit camera view of a CT-155 Hawk ingesting a bird and then crashing in a field [1].","text":"Here's a nice cockpit camera view of a CT-155 Hawk ingesting a bird and then crashing in a field [1]. (The title and description say it is in F-16, but that is incorrect [2]). Why do you think drones would not be capable of causing similar damage? I have no idea about helicopters, but for aircraft with turbojet engines all but the smallest drones would hit the blades if ingested and that would cause damage. It might not cause a reduction or complete loss of engine power if the drone is small enough, but it would still cost a lot of money due to inspection and repair costs and down time. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":9011118,"author":"tzs","month":"2015-02","story":"Why we should worry about domestic drones and how to bring them down","stance":"reference","why":"Cockpit camera as bird strike kills engine and pilot ejects.","quote":"It's a video from a forward facing camera in an F-16 as it gets hit by a bird, which shuts down the engine.","text":"Yet sometimes a single bird will take out an engine: [LINK] Modern engines are designed to contain the damage, and multi-engine jets are designed to be able to maintain control, climb to a safe altitude, and return to the airport with an engine out, so flying a small drone into an engine probably won't cause a major disaster, but it will cause a lot of disruption as the plane declares an emergency and returns to the airport, and cost a lot of money to repair or replace the engine. One of the suggested videos for that one was interesting. It's a video from a forward facing camera in an F-16 as it gets hit by a bird, which shuts down the engine. The pilot tries for a while to restart, fails, ejects, and the plane crashes: [VIDEO]"},{"id":8600373,"author":"neurotech1","month":"2014-11","story":"DJI Systems Introduces Inspire 1 Drone","stance":"reference","why":"Cockpit footage of a CT-155 Hawk bird strike, mislabeled as F-16.","quote":"See video of a Canadian CT-155 Hawk (Not F-16 as titled)","text":"Actually, Relatively small birds have been known to penetrate the windshield of a Cessna and injure the pilot or even wreck the engine of a single engine of a jet like a BAE Hawk. See video of a Canadian CT-155 Hawk (Not F-16 as titled) [VIDEO] For an airliner like an Airbus A320, a small bird can damage the engine fan blades and require an expensive repair. If the bird goes through the core even minor damage becomes very expensive to fix."},{"id":1968093,"author":"blantonl","month":"2010-12","story":"How an RC airplane buzzed the Statue of Liberty, with no arrests","stance":"reference","why":"Single-engine aircraft hits a bird after takeoff, pilots eject.","quote":"This was an F-16 that ingested a bird after takeoff, engine relight failed and pilots ejected after pointing the plane into a field.","text":"This video clearly shows what might happen when a single engine aircraft at <500 AGL hits an object. [VIDEO] This was an F-16 that ingested a bird after takeoff, engine relight failed and pilots ejected after pointing the plane into a field."}]}});
